{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29568": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Professor of Spatial Proteomics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; Competitive; National Heart & Lung Institute; Academic & Teaching Reference: MED05803 About the role: Imperial College London is seeking to appoint an outstanding Academic in Spatial Proteomics within the National Heart and Lung Institute (NHLI). Applications are welcomed from candidates suitable for appointment at Assistant, Associate or full Professor level, commensurate with experience and achievement. The successful candidate will establish and lead an internationally competitive research programme applying advanced spatial proteomics and imaging technologies to cardiovascular and respiratory disease This is an exciting opportunity to shape the future of spatial biology within one of the world's leading centres for cardiovascular and respiratory research. The appointee will play a central role in advancing the NHLI's strategic ambitions in spatial biology, contributing to the development of a newly established Spatial Biology Hub and generating fundamental insights into disease mechanisms with the potential to transform patient care. Supported by significant recent investment in infrastructure, equipment and personnel, the successful candidate will join the Institute at a pivotal moment of growth and ambition. The appointee will lead an ambitious research programme spanning cardiovascular and respiratory disease, including the application of spatial proteomics at subcellular resolution in human tissues. They will develop new spatial biology technologies and methodologies, support collaborative projects addressing high-impact biological questions, and horizon-scan emerging approaches in this rapidly developing field. Furthermore, the successful candidate will foster scientific collaborations within Imperial and with other leading academic and commercial organisations, secure external research funding, publish work of international quality, and supervise and mentor research staff and students. They will contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate education and play an active role in the academic leadership and wider strategic development of NHLI and the Faculty of Medicine. Candidates will hold a PhD in spatial proteomics or a related field and bring a strong national or international reputation in the discipline. They will demonstrate an excellent publication record, experience of developing and leading research programmes, success in attracting external funding, and a coherent and sustainable vision for research that strengthens NHLI’s scientific priorities. Further information: How to Apply: Enquiries can be made, in confidence, to Natalie Derry or Rowan Smith at WittKieffer. Completed applications should be submitted electronically to rsmith@wittkieffer.com . Applications should include the following: An up-to-date Curriculum Vitae; and A cover letter (maximum 2 pages) outlining suitability for the position and addressing how you satisfy the person specification criteria. The closing date for applications is 20 November 2026."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29576": {
      "title": "Ken and Mary Minton Clinical Associate Professor in Kidney Vasculitis",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £113,565 - £150,569 per annum; Department of Immunology and Inflammation; Academic & Teaching Reference: MED06032 About the role: We are seeking to recruit an outstanding clinical academic to join the Centre for Inflammatory Disease (CID), Department of Immunology and Inflammation, Imperial College where we offer exciting academic opportunities within an integrated and supportive environment. We are interested in applications from clinical academics with academic track record in vasculitis specifically in ANCA associated vasculitis, a disease for which Imperial College and Imperial Healthcare NHS Trust are leading research and clinical care centres respectively. You will have experience managing patients with kidney vasculitis, and lead cutting edge research in vasculitis. Your research program will be connected to your clinical practice and complement that of existing groups across the Centre for Inflammatory Disease. You will be contributing to growing the Centre’s research portfolio, to delivering teaching to medical and non-medical students and to administration within the Department. Imperial College has a broad range of clinical and non-clinical research expertise which provides an outstanding multidisciplinary environment in basic, clinical and translational research. The Department of Immunology and Inflammation is embedded within this vibrant research environment and is proud of its supportive ethos and coordinated activities in research, teaching and public engagement. What you would be doing: You will contribute to the academic aims of the Centre and the Department by: Planning and directing relevant basic and translational science research programmes Planning and leading innovative, interdisciplinary research Successfully attracting funds for research initiatives Extending and interlinking the research strengths already existing within Imperial College across the Faculties in your field of research Participating in the planning, design and execution of teaching including postgraduate teaching and research supervision and undergraduate teaching Participating in postgraduate training and courses for junior and senior doctors in training What we are looking for: You will have: Full GMC registration, including entry on the GMC specialist Register, with appropriate specialist qualification. MD, PhD or equivalent Higher Research Degree in a relevant area An established national reputation and a developing international reputation and expertise in the field Track record in attracting independent research funding Experience of training Undergraduate and Postgraduate students to a high standard What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (43 days off a year including public holidays and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Please see our Further Particulars for more information about the College and the benefits available to staff. Further information: Salary : Consultant Clinical Academic scale: £113,565 to £150,569 per annum plus London Allowance of £2,162 per annum Closing date: 26 October 2026, 23:59 hours (Midnight GMT) Expected start date: first quarter of 2027 This is a full-time open-ended position If you require any further details about the role, please contact Professor Matthew Pickering ( maathew.pickering@imperial.ac.uk ), Director of the Centre for Inflammatory Disease As this post is exempt from the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974, a satisfactory Disclosure and Barring Service (DBS) check , at the appropriate level, will be required for the successful candidate. If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29565": {
      "title": "Maintenance Technician - (Mechanical)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01454 About the role: Imperial College London is a world Top 10 university with an international reputation for excellence in teaching and research. The Maintenance Team forms a vital part of the Property Division who help manage one of the largest built estates in the University sector. The department delivers a comprehensive range of services to an academic community who are world leaders in the provision of Engineering, Medicine, Natural Sciences, Business Management, Research and Teaching across a multi-site estate. As a Mechanical Maintenance Technician for Imperial, you will be expected to work as part of a team providing mechanically biased support to our Academic, Research, Teaching and Administrative functions. Our Maintenance Team carry out Reactive and PPM works to assets, project support and infrastructure to ensure all Imperial activities can be carried out with minimal disruption. We expect relevant qualifications and/or experience against the essential criteria of the role and competencies, which should be demonstrated in your personal statement with detailed information on how your skills, knowledge and experience are relevant to this role. What you would be doing: You will carry out reactive and planned maintenance and repairs to mechanical equipment and all associated systems. You will undertake a proactive approach in ensuring all plant and equipment is fully operational and fit for purpose. You will undertake appropriate administrative tasks by fulfilling the work report on the CAFM system Planon. You will maintain safe workplace practice and procedures in accordance with the requirements of Health & Safety Legislation. You will undertake other duties which may arise commensurate with the grade of the post as directed by the line manager/supervisor. What we are looking for: Gained a recognised apprenticeship and attained an appropriate NVQ level 3 in Mechanical discipline with knowledge of hot water systems, pumps, compressed air systems, chillers, AHU’s etc. Have a proven ability to work within a busy multi-tasking team with a flexible approach to daily work and a proactive approach to resolving problems. Excellent communication and customer care skills. Understanding of Health & Safety, preferably IOSH trained Be computer literate and able to use MS Office products: Word, Teams, Outlook for e-mail, and calendar appointments. Knowledge of Planon and use of tablet would be an advantage. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual and mandatory leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: The working hours are 35 hours per week, on a 7-day rotating shift pattern (South Kensington Campus). Please be aware that this role will not permit skilled worker route sponsorship. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Maria White - m.r.white@imperial.ac.uk Closing Date: Midnight on Sunday, 01 November 2026"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29574": {
      "title": "Operations and Resource Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: President’s Team; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Communications Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRS00578 About the role: Are you an experienced operations professional who enjoys bringing order, efficiency and strategic insight to a busy and dynamic environment? We are looking for an Operations and Resource Officer to play a pivotal role in the management and delivery of the Communications Division at Imperial College London. Working closely with the Head of Operations and Planning, you will help ensure the Division operates effectively by overseeing finance, HR, governance, resources and business operations, enabling colleagues to deliver world-class communications that support Imperial's mission and reputation. What you would be doing: As our Operations and Resource Officer, you will be at the heart of the Division's business management functions. You will: Coordinate and support the operational and strategic management of the Communications Division. Lead on divisional finance activities, helping to manage a budget of approximately £4 million and supporting budget planning and year-end processes. Oversee a range of HR activities including recruitment, onboarding, probation, training and staff lifecycle processes. Analyse and interpret operational, financial and workforce data, providing insights and recommendations to support decision-making. Coordinate divisional meetings, preparing agendas, papers, minutes and actions. Support risk management, governance and business continuity planning. Manage divisional systems, records and resources, including SharePoint, contact lists and operational documentation. Oversee office logistics, equipment management and operational support services. Act as the first point of contact for operational enquiries, providing expert advice and resolving issues independently. Deputise for the Head of Operations and Planning on operational matters when required What we are looking for: We are seeking a proactive and highly organised individual who can balance strategic thinking with excellent operational delivery. You will bring: Experience of managing business operations, resources, budgets and administrative processes in a complex organisation. Strong financial and HR administration experience, with the ability to manage multiple priorities effectively. Excellent analytical skills and confidence working with complex information and data. Outstanding organisational skills, accuracy and attention to detail. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build effective relationships at all levels. Strong problem-solving skills and the confidence to work independently and use sound judgement. High levels of digital literacy, including proficiency in Microsoft 365 applications. A flexible and collaborative approach with a commitment to continuous improvement and excellent customer service. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading university and contribute to our mission of Science for Humanity. A varied and influential role with exposure to senior leaders and strategic decision-making. The chance to help shape and improve the operations of a high-performing Communications Division. Sector-leading benefits including 43 days' annual leave, generous pension schemes and flexible working arrangements. Access to a wide range of professional development opportunities and learning resources. A supportive, inclusive and collaborative working environment. A comprehensive range of wellbeing services, staff networks and workplace benefits. Further information: The Communications Division is responsible for protecting and enhancing Imperial's reputation, communicating the impact of our teaching, research and innovation, and supporting engagement with key audiences across Imperial and beyond. This is a full-time, open-ended position primarily based at our White City Campus, however the role will require the post holder to work at our South Kensington Campus at least once per week. Hybrid working available, with at least 60% office attendance required. The earliest start date for this role would be 1 February 2027. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact the Head of Operations and Planning: Tabitha Pearson-Moore – t.pearson-moore@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29572": {
      "title": "Programme Manager, MRes",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Department: Business School; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Professional / Support Services Reference: BUS00899 About the role: The Programme Manager is an integral part of a small team responsible for the successful management and delivery of the post-graduate taught (PGT) MRes programme, the first element of the Business School’s Doctoral Programme. Working across all academic departments within the Business School, you will nurture good working relationships with faculty and a small cohort of students, contributing to the success of future researchers and academics. What you would be doing: Reporting to the Senior Programme Manager, you will play a pivotal role in the student experience through proactive one-to-one engagement with MRes students, offering individualised support that enables academic success, professional growth, and a strong sense of belonging within the research community. Working on a small yet complex programme, you will manage student administration, assessments, timetabling, academic processes and programme communications, while providing expert advice on policies and procedures. You will work collaboratively with faculty and external partners, and contribute to continuous improvements across programme delivery. What we are looking for: Dedicated to providing the highest quality of support, you will need to be confident in working collaboratively with students, faculty and other stakeholders. You will have excellent organisational skills and attention to detail, as well as having strong communication skills, ideally gained through work in higher education. You must be a strong team player with the proven ability to use your own initiative and work with minimal supervision. You will need to demonstrate a pro-active, positive attitude and the ability to juggle several tasks confidently. Experience of working in higher education, ideally in a PGT or PGR environment, is highly desirable. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package including 43 days’ leave and generous pension schemes. Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full time, open-ended position based at South Kensington campus (hybrid). If you require any further details about the role please contact Laura Barkaway, Senior Programme Manager, on l.barkaway@imperial.ac.uk or Nicola Hann, Programme Director, on n.hann@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29560": {
      "title": "Research Associate in High Altitude Platforms systems",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04069 About the role: The Communications and Signal Processing Group at Imperial College has two part-time openings in machine learning (ML) and artificial intelligence (AI) to design a new layer of communication infrastructure using high-altitude platform systems (HAPS) to provide communications services to ground users. The positions are funded by the ARIA EndAP programme: https://aria.org.uk/opportunity-spaces/adaptive-machines/enduring-atmospheric-platforms/funded-projects?tabId=ta3&cardId=ai-enabled-haps . The post-holders will join a diverse team with expertise in network AI and optimization, wireless communications and networks, aircraft design and deployment regulations to leverage the strengths of different HAPS platforms, determine the best fleet composition, and coordinate platform movements for energy efficiency, resilience, and adaptability to traffic demands of ground users. To begin, we will study how multiple HAPS platforms should be strategically moved and positioned in three-dimensional airspace to provide the optimal service coverage and meet communication quality of service (QoS) requirements for ground users subject to navigating weather, energy, and regulatory constraints. A robust backhaul network will be designed to connect HAPS with each other, and with terrestrial and satellite networks, ensuring efficient network-wide connectivity and flexibility. We aim to develop a reinforcement-learning framework for joint optimization of air-vehicle design, real-time control, and provisioning of communication services under changing weather, energy availability, and regulatory constraints. Our goal is to develop novel distributed RL algorithms for each HAPS will function as an autonomous, distributed RL agent, making independent but cooperative control decisions to optimize its position, resource allocation, and operational strategy in real time. This post will also participate in assessment of alternative power architectures and operational constraints to ensure consistent power availability for both individual HAPS units and entire fleets. We will develop robust fleet operational strategies to identify minimum viable fleet sizes and configurations under varying weather, deployment, and coverage requirements. Furthermore, to use the designed algorithm in practice, we will evaluate how physical constraints, such as payload size, weight, and power supply, and techno-economic tradeoffs affect the optimal HAPS system designs. We will adapt and validate the developed HAPS technologies across multiple fleet platforms. The positions are an excellent opportunity for researcher with strong background in machine learning, wireless communications and communication networks. Strong analytic skills are expected. What you would be doing: Research Duties include: To plan and carry out research in accordance with the project aims and timeframes. To propose and investigate new techniques, algorithms and architectures for network design, optimization and orchestration. To verify and characterize system performance both analytically and in realistic simulated testbeds. To collaborate and cooperate with other research staff both within and outside Imperial College working on related projects funded by the UK EPSRC. To act as the representative for Imperial College in meetings, conference calls and conferences. To present research at conferences, workshops, open days and meetings, and publish research results in journals. To develop a sound understanding of ethical and health and safety regulations, and the responsibilities of themselves and their colleagues What we are looking for: Education Research Associate: Hold a PhD in Electrical Engineering, Computer Science, Mathematics, or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant . Research Assistant: PhD (or equivalent) candidates in Electrical Engineering, Computer Science, Mathematics, or a closely related discipline. Experience Machine learning (ML) and/or optimization for communication systems Development, implementation and verification of ML algorithms in code Algorithm development and performance analysis of data-driven decision-making systems Knowledge Machine learning and optimization techniques Key technologies in wireless communication systems Networked and distributed systems What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: Please note that job descriptions cannot be exhaustive, and the post-holder may be required to undertake other duties, which are broadly in line with the above key responsibilities. For any specific queries regarding the post please contact Prof Kin K. Leung (kin.leung@imperial.ac.uk)"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29562": {
      "title": "Senior Building Surveyor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £71,793- £82,031 per annum; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01456 About the role: Imperial is a world Top 10 university with an international reputation for excellence in teaching and research. The Campus Operations Department are responsible for supporting the operational Hospitality Teams of various cafes, restaurants, bars and events located across all their Campuses to provide affordable and high-quality food and services for the Imperial community and external clients. They create a valuable resource for Imperial, which in turn supports our academic community of world leaders in the provision of Engineering, Medicine, Natural Sciences, Business Management, Research and Teaching. As Senior Building Surveyor, you will provide strategic leadership, governance and assurance for building surveying, building fabric asset management and statutory compliance across Imperial’s diverse estate. Working within the Critical Infrastructure and Asset Strategy Team, you will help safeguard and enhance academic, research, residential, healthcare and commercial facilities, ensuring they remain safe, compliant, sustainable, functional and able to deliver long-term value. You will be Imperial’s senior subject matter expert for building surveying, shaping estate-wide asset investment, lifecycle, building safety and compliance strategies, and providing trusted, authoritative advice to senior leaders, project boards and regulatory stakeholders. This is a highly influential opportunity to make a visible impact on a world-class estate. You will work in close partnership with Maintenance Operations, Fire Safety, Capital Projects Delivery, Asset Management and external advisers, translating complex condition, risk and performance information into clear priorities, robust investment cases and practical solutions. You will lead a Building Surveyor and provide professional leadership to consultants, contractors and specialist advisers, fostering a collaborative, customer-focused culture of technical excellence, continuous improvement and high professional standards. What you would be doing: You will lead building condition and measured surveys, inspections and defect investigations across the estate, developing proportionate repair, refurbishment, renewal and replacement solutions. You will prepare specifications, schedules of work, tender and contract information, and drive the development of planned preventative maintenance, risk-based maintenance and lifecycle replacement programmes. Using high-quality asset information, you will establish investment plans, performance indicators and a strategic roadmap for building fabric renewal, ensuring that works are prioritised according to condition, risk, operational need, available funding and long-term estate objectives. Building safety will be central to your remit. You will act as the building fabric lead for compliance with the Building Safety Act 2022, Building Regulations and Higher-Risk Building requirements, supporting robust governance, regulatory engagement and the management of Golden Thread information. You will lead assurance activity relating to building fabric and passive fire protection, including compartmentation, fire doors, fire stopping and cavity barriers; manage statutory approvals and submissions where required; and advise on relevant Client, Principal Designer and Principal Contractor responsibilities. You will also lead complex compliance, refurbishment, lifecycle renewal and remedial programmes from planning through to completion—managing budgets, procurement, contracts, contractor performance, quality, safety, risk, programme reporting and project records. Through clear executive reporting, business cases and investment recommendations, you will enable sound decisions and value-for-money delivery. What we are looking for: Degree or equivalent qualification in Building Surveying, Construction, Architecture or a related discipline. Chartered membership of RICS, CIOB, CABE or an equivalent professional body. Significant experience in building surveying, property, construction or estate management. Strong knowledge of Building Regulations, statutory approvals and current building safety requirements. Knowledge and practical understanding of the Building Safety Act 2022 and Higher-Risk Buildings. Experience of building condition assessment, building pathology, refurbishment and remedial works. Experience of fire safety remediation and passive fire protection. Experience managing consultants, contractors and multidisciplinary teams. Strong commercial awareness, including experience managing budgets and developing investment proposals. The ability to translate complex technical and regulatory matters into clear advice for senior and non-technical stakeholders. A strong commitment to professional development, collaboration and continuous improvement. Experience of complex operational estates, asset management and lifecycle investment planning, heritage buildings or Golden Thread information management would be particularly valuable What we can offer you: At Imperial, our values—respect, collaboration, excellence, integrity, and innovation—guide everything we do. You’ll be part of a supportive and forward-thinking team, working on projects that directly contribute to the safety and wellbeing of our community. We are committed to creating an inclusive working environment and welcome applications from all backgrounds. We particularly encourage interest from individuals who are underrepresented in our workforce. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: If you require any further details on the role please contact: Mickie Turner-Goble, Property Divisional Administration Manager- csadmin@imperial.ac.uk Closing Date: Midnight, Sunday 08 November 2026"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29552": {
      "title": "Finance Service Centre Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £38,608 - £41,666 per annum; Finance Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01438 About the role: The Finance Service Centre is an exciting team within the Finance Division at ICL. Working very closely with the Payments and Transaction teams, the Finance Service Centre assistant will provide friendly advice, guidance and other information about services provided by the Finance Department and is the first point of contact for all its customers. The team also maintains, contributes and add to knowledge management documents, which are available on the Finance website, to aid internal customers. What you would be doing: Provide first-line finance support to college staff and external users through the College’s service management system. Resolve requests and inquiries promptly, aiming for a first-contact resolution within a set timeframe before escalation. Log and track requests received via phone, ServiceNow, and in-person visits. Identify and escalate recurring issues to the Finance Service Centre Manager as needed. Complete ongoing training to stay updated on relevant policies, procedures, and customer service practices. Participate in additional tasks related to your role at the Divisional or College level as directed by the Finance Service Centre Manager. What we are looking for: Recognised continuing professional development and training in customer care. Experience in a Service Desk/Call Centre environment, ideally supporting a large-scale and diverse environment. Excellent knowledge of financial systems. Ability to learn quickly, resolve problems the first time, and exercise initiative. Good interpersonal and organisational skills; good team player. Ability to communicate effectively and clearly to college staff and external customers, in writing and verbally. Commitment to Quality Standards and continuous improvement Good attention to detail Ability to remain calm under pressure. Flexible approach to duties and willing to cover for others. What we can offer you: Competitive pension package Subsidised state-of-the-art gym membership The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity Benefit from sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes) Get access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day 1, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and a cycle-to-work scheme Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel Be part of a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This role is full time, open-ended. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Darrell McKenzie – Finance Service Centre Manager at d.mckenzie@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29447": {
      "title": "Human Anatomy Unit Deputy Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED05956 About the role: Are you an experienced anatomy, laboratory or technical services professional looking to take the next step as a Human Anatomy Unit Deputy and Lab Manager? This is an exciting opportunity to play a key role in the leadership and day-to-day operation of Imperial's Human Anatomy Unit. Working closely with the Human Anatomy Unit Manager, you will help ensure the delivery of high-quality anatomical, clinical skills and pathology teaching facilities that support undergraduate, postgraduate, external education and research activities. What you would be doing: We are seeking an organised, proactive and collaborative individual with experience of working in a technical, laboratory, anatomy or clinical skills environment. You will ideally have: As the Human Anatomy Unit Deputy and Lab Manager, you will: Support the leadership and operational management of a multidisciplinary anatomical services facility. Supervise and support a dedicated technical team, ensuring the effective planning and delivery of teaching and operational activities. Coordinate the preparation and delivery of anatomy, clinical skills and pathology teaching sessions. Ensure compliance with the Human Tissue Act 2004, Human Tissue Authority (HTA) standards, health and safety legislation, and institutional policies. Oversee the maintenance, development and efficient use of teaching facilities, equipment and resources. Work collaboratively with academic, professional services and external partners to support a high-quality learning experience. Contribute to service development, continuous improvement initiatives and operational planning across the Unit. What we are looking for: The post holder should hold a degree in Anatomy, Biological Sciences, or a related scientific discipline, or possess an equivalent vocational qualification or substantial relevant teaching laboratory-based experience. Experience working at a senior technical level in an Anatomy teaching laboratory or similar environment Experience in line management. Experience in supervising staff, students, and groups Experience in training staff Experience in implementing HR protocols Experience in utilising IT packages Experience in providing management and information reports. Experience in Quality management and inventory management practices Working knowledge and experience of HTA regulations Experience in designing and implementing SOPs and HTA documents Working Knowledge of developing and implementing Health and Safety Regulations and Legislation Working knowledge of chemical, hazardous waste, and environmental safety What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time post (35 hours per week), open-ended role based at Charing Cross Campus. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: [Rachael Waddington– [r.waddington@imperial.ac.uk]."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29555": {
      "title": "Laboratory Manager - Cancer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06030 About the role: The Department of Surgery & Cancer (S&C) is seeking a Laboratory Manager to support the administration and management of research laboratories and facilities across the Division of Cancer, which operates across the Hammersmith Hospital and White City, Charing Cross Hospital, and South Kensington campuses. The post holder will work in close and supportive partnership with academics, researchers, technical staff, students, and professional services colleagues to facilitate research and teaching activities and ensure the effective operation of laboratories and facilities across the Division's multi-campus research environment. You will support the Head of Division and Principal Investigators (PIs) in fulfilling their Health and Safety (H&S) responsibilities across the Division's research laboratories and facilities. The post holder will be a member of the S&C H&S Compliance team, carrying out inspections and supporting compliance activities across laboratories and research facilities within the Department. What you would be doing: The role is responsible for the overall management and smooth operation of research laboratories and facilities across the Division's multi-campus research environment, including health and safety compliance, equipment maintenance, procurement, stock control and general day‑to‑day operational support. The post holder will maintain equipment and infrastructure, coordinating refurbishments and relocations, and work closely with central departments including Estates, ICT, Finance and Safety as required. The post holder will lead on training and induction of staff and students, promoting safe and efficient laboratory practices. This includes delivering inductions, supporting compliance, providing first-line advice, resolving operational issues and liaising with a wide range of stakeholders, including researchers, service providers and external contractors. What we are looking for: Excellent organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise competing demands, maintain high standards and provide reliable administrative support to ensure research laboratories and facilities operate efficiently and smoothly. Proactive and effective communication skills, with the ability to support the implementation of Imperial College London policies and procedures. Excellent writing skills, with the ability to prepare reports, procedures and operational documentation, and contribute to grant applications where required. Strong record keeping skills to monitor and maintain critical compliance and operational records including safety documents, equipment assets, service and maintenance records and consumable inventories. The ability to work on multiple projects simultaneously and deliver tasks to agreed deadlines. A collaborative mindset and willingness to work closely and effectively with Imperial contractors, central professional services, academic, clinical and industrial partners across the Department and the College. What we can offer you: The opportunity to work at a world-leading institution and be part of Imperial’s mission in science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This role is full-time, open-ended (35 hours per week) primarily based at Hammersmith Hospital & White City Campus (primarily), travel to other campuses will be required. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr Yoyo Dixon, Senior Laboratory Manager at yoyo.dixon@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29558": {
      "title": "Property Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; N/A; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01457 About the role: Imperial College London is seeking a Property Manager to support the effective management of our diverse property portfolio, including operational, office, retail, residential and agricultural assets. Working within the Real Estate Property Team, you will support the Senior Property Managers with the strategic and operational management of freehold and leasehold property, helping to maintain compliance, enhance value and support Imperial’s wider academic and operational objectives. This is a 12-month fixed-term role, primarily based at White City with travel to other Imperial campuses. What you would be doing: You will manage a range of property matters including site inspections, landlord and tenant issues, lease renewals and terminations, service charges, licences, rights of way and land ownership enquiries. You will also support property acquisitions and disposals, working with solicitors, agents and other professional advisers from valuation and negotiation through to formal completion. You will maintain key property records, support rent, arrears, service charge and business-rate processes, and help monitor the performance of property assets. Working with tenants, landlords, local authorities and colleagues across Imperial, you will help ensure property matters are handled effectively, professionally and in line with statutory requirements and best practice. What we are looking for: We are looking for an experienced property professional with a strong understanding of landlord and tenant matters and the practical management of complex property portfolios. You will bring: Membership of the Royal Institution of Chartered Surveyors (RICS), with at least two years’ post-qualification experience Graduate-level education, or equivalent knowledge and experience Experience of active property or estates management within a complex environment Experience engaging with tenants, landlords, public bodies and communities Experience evaluating commercial opportunities and conducting rent and lease reviews Experience working with legal representatives and external property advisers Up-to-date knowledge of statutory requirements and best practice in property and estates management Knowledge of planning application procedures and experience liaising with local authorities Strong knowledge of service charge management Excellent communication, negotiation and stakeholder-management skills Strong analytical and organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and work independently What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing Further information: This recruitment campaign is being managed by MacDonald & Company on behalf of Imperial College London. Please submit applications directly to MacDonald & Company via Alistair Carter acarter@macdonaldandcompany.com"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29556": {
      "title": "Senior Asset Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; N/A; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01453 About the role: Imperial College London is seeking a Senior Asset Manager to support the strategic and operational management of Imperial’s property and land portfolio, helping to protect and enhance the value, performance and long-term potential of our estate. Working closely with the Director of Real Estate Asset Management, you will take a senior role across commercial, leasing, planning and legal property matters, working with colleagues across Imperial as well as tenants, property professionals, consultants and external advisers. You will help develop active asset management strategies, identify opportunities and ensure appropriate agreements, processes and policies are in place to support Imperial’s wider objectives. What you would be doing: In this role, you will support the development and delivery of asset management plans, undertake negotiations relating to lettings, rent reviews and other lease events, and evaluate opportunities to create value through active property management. You will analyse property performance, market trends and relevant statutory or planning developments, providing clear advice and recommendations to senior colleagues. You will also manage relationships with tenants, landlords, external legal and property advisers, local authorities and other stakeholders, while supporting property budgets, business rates and service charge management. What we are looking for: Chartered membership of the Royal Institution of Chartered Surveyors (RICS), with significant post-qualification experience Senior-level experience in the active management of property or estates within a complex organisation Experience managing budgets and interpreting financial information in a property context Experience engaging with tenants, landlords, public bodies and local communities Experience evaluating development opportunities and conducting rent reviews, lease reviews and other lease events Experience instructing and managing legal representatives and external property advisers In-depth knowledge of cross-sector property and professional asset management practice Up-to-date knowledge of statutory requirements and best practice relating to estates and property management Practical experience of service charge management Strong communication, negotiation and influencing skills, with the ability to build relationships with senior internal and external stakeholders Sound judgement, strong organisational skills and a proactive approach to identifying and resolving property issues What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing Further information: This recruitment campaign is being managed by Forrester Partners on behalf of Imperial College London. Please submit applications directly to Forrester Partners via Nick Howard - nh@forresterpartners.com and Georgie Cantrell - gc@forresterpartners.com"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29544": {
      "title": "Clinical Associate Professor in Haematopoiesis",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £113,565 - £150,569 per annum; Department of Immunology and Inflammation; Academic & Teaching Reference: MED06021 About the role: We are seeking to recruit an outstanding clinical academic to join the Centre for Haematology, Department of Immunology and Inflammation, Imperial College where we offer exciting academic opportunities within an integrated and supportive environment. We are interested in applications from clinical academics with academic track record in haematopoiesis specifically in heritable and acquired bone marrow failure disorders such as Diamond-Blackfan Anaemia Syndrome, a disease for which Imperial College and Imperial Healthcare NHS Trust are leading research and clinical care centres respectively. You will have experience managing patients with bone marrow failure syndromes, and lead cutting edge research in red cell haematology and haematopoiesis. Your research program will be connected to your clinical practice and complement that of existing groups across the Centre for Haematology. You will be contributing to growing the Centre’s research portfolio, to delivering teaching to medical and non-medical students and to administration within the Department. Imperial College has a broad range of clinical and non-clinical research expertise which provides an outstanding multidisciplinary environment in basic, clinical and translational research. The Department of Immunology and Inflammation is embedded within this vibrant research environment and is proud of its supportive ethos and coordinated activities in research, teaching and public engagement. What you would be doing: You will contribute to the academic aims of the Centre and the Department by: Planning and directing relevant basic and translational science research programmes Planning and leading innovative, interdisciplinary research Successfully attracting funds for research initiatives Extending and interlinking the research strengths already existing within Imperial College across the Faculties in your field of research Participating in the planning, design and execution of teaching including postgraduate teaching and research supervision and undergraduate teaching Participating in postgraduate training and courses for junior and senior doctors in training What we are looking for: You will have: Full GMC registration, including entry on the GMC specialist Register, with appropriate specialist qualification. MD, PhD or equivalent Higher Research Degree in a relevant area A national reputation/ international reputation and expertise in the field (commensurate with career stage) Track record in attracting independent research funding Experience of training Undergraduate and Postgraduate students to a high standard Full information on duties, responsibilities and criteria can be found in the attached Job Description. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (43 days off a year including public holidays and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Please see our Further Particulars for more information about the College and the benefits available to staff. Further information: Closing date: 22 October 2026, 23:59 hours (Midnight BST) Expected start date: first quarter of 2027 This is a full-time open-ended position If you require any further details about the role, please contact Co-Directors, Centre for Haematology : Professor Anastasios Karadimitris ( a.karadimitris@imperial.ac.uk ) or Professor Cristina Lo Celso c.lo-celso@imperial.ac.uk ) As this post is exempt from the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974, a satisfactory Disclosure and Barring Service (DBS) check , at the appropriate level, will be required for the successful candidate. If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29540": {
      "title": "Due Diligence Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Division of the University Secretary; £48,246 - £50,462 per annum; Professional / Support Services Reference: CEN00392 About the role: This new role sits within the central Due Diligence function in the Division of the University Secretary. It has been created to support Imperial’s exciting partnership ambitions and to strengthen early-stage assessment of legal, financial and reputational risks associated with third-party relationships. What you would be doing: You will hold responsibility for the production of high-quality due diligence reports on Imperial’s third-party relationships to ensure compliance with Imperial’s Relationships Policy. You will support the Regulatory Compliance Manager in providing advice and oversight to the network of Due Diligence Coordinators across Imperial. You will also assist in identifying and remedying gaps in due diligence training and knowledge, ensuring that staff are equipped with the skills required to use Imperial’s Due Diligence Portal effectively and to produce due diligence reports of the highest standard to support sound decision-making. You will support the provision of relevant training to strengthen staff understanding of relationships management principles and the risks of undue influence or other behaviours that could compromise research integrity, data security, or institutional independence. You will assist the Regulatory Compliance Manager in advising on the appropriate Decision-Making Body (DMB) for specific relationships, in line with the requirements of the Relationships Policy. To help ensure consistent application of the Policy across the University, you will also work periodically with the Risk & Assurance Team to support proportionate audits of decisions made at DMB Levels 1 and 2. What we are looking for: A minimum of degree-level education or equivalent experience Demonstrable experience of delivering effective high quality due diligence or equivalent reports at pace Experience of working effectively within a team Experience of managing data systems and producing reports An understanding of the theory and practice of due diligence intelligence reporting An understanding of governance processes and procedures in a complex organisation Well-developed organisation and coordination skills with a strong, independently driven delivery focus Ability to coordinate a range of different activities at the same time and understand the connections between them Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to build relationships and engage professionally with a wide range of stakeholders across varying seniorities and backgrounds Ability to use initiative and apply a solution-orientated approach to work Able and willing to work flexibly and respond to changing priorities under pressure Analytical skills with the ability to interpret and disseminate information and data from complex documents Ability to work with accuracy and attention to detail Ability to use discretion and judgment when dealing with confidential and sensitive data Willing to undertake training as appropriate A full list of essential and desirable criteria can be found in the Job Description and Person Specification. With reference to each of the individual essential requirements listed in the person specification, please provide specific information describing how you meet these requirements. Please tell us how your work experience, knowledge, skills and abilities, are relevant in no more than 2 sides of A4. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: If you require any further details on the role please contact Chloe Schofield, Regulatory Compliance Manager, on c.ratcliffeschofield@imperial.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29536": {
      "title": "Head of Medical Sciences and Evaluation",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £72,829 - £79,469 per annum; Faculty of Medicine Centre; Academic & Teaching Reference: MED06027 About the role: Imperial College London and the University of Cumbria have entered into a partnership to form the Pears Cumbria School of Medicine (PCSM) , based at the University of Cumbria at the Carlisle Fusehill Street campus. PCSM’s vision is to improve health outcomes for the people of Cumbria. The School is now seeking to appoint an outstanding individual as Head of Medical Sciences and Evaluation In this role, you will lead the Medical Sciences on-campus delivery to ensure an outstanding student experience. This includes curriculum planning and timetabling, optimising the use of teaching spaces and resources across the module. Working closely with the Head of Assessments, you will play a key role developing the assessments ensuring the reliability and validity of questions across the Medical Sciences 1 and Medical Sciences 2 modules. You will have an understanding the standard approaches to medical assessment, question writing and review, standard setting, and post-hoc item analysis. You will also manage the Medical Sciences Virtual Learning Environment (VLE) working closely with the Head of Curriculum Management and Assessments You will lead the implementation of the PCSM Evaluation Strategy, which will determine whether the school is achieving its stated goals and objectives, particularly in relation to the Mission to recruit, educate, and retain doctors for the healthcare needs of Cumbria. You will be responsible for ensuring qualitative and quantitative data is collected (including surveys, focus groups, and secondary data analysis of other data sources) tracking outcomes across all categories listed in the PCSM Evaluation Strategy. What you would be doing: Work independently and collaboratively with the Head of Curriculum Management and Assessment to provide leadership in the delivery of the Medical Sciences modules, Apply subject matter and pedagogic expertise to own teaching practice and to support the teaching teams within the Medical Sciences; ensuring an excellent student experience. Ensure Medical Sciences learning materials, revision resources, and assessment items meet agreed quality standards and timelines. Ensure alignment of the curriculum and assessments with the General Medical Council's Outcomes for Graduates and the requirements of the Medical Licensing Assessment. Manage timetable change requests in accordance with academic regulations and approved governance processes. Provide reports on Medical Sciences curriculum and assessment activities as required. Lead on the implementation of the PCSM Evaluation Strategy, including surveying students at different timepoints, to provide insight enabling the School to accomplish its mission. Support data analysis and critical enquiry across all evaluation data, ensuring that results are communicated within the School, and externally as appropriate. Liaise with the Head of Quality and the PCSM Head of Operations to support the quality assurance of curriculum materials and triangulation with evaluation data. What we are looking for: A PhD and significant postdoctoral experience in an area of medical science Possession of (or working towards) a recognised qualification in medical education or leadership, Advance HE fellowship (or similar) Substantial experience of research in an area of medical science Extensive experience of working in medical education/training A demonstrable track record of leadership in teaching using a variety of strategies Experience in teaching innovation and the production of different learning materials, along with its associated evaluation and management Experience in curriculum planning and development in Higher Education Substantial experience in different aspects of development and delivery of the medical education curriculum, medical school planning and governance A well developed knowledge of assessment theory and practice as it relates to medical education Proven leadership skills, with the capability to rigorously design, develop, communicate and oversee processes in line with guidance and best practice What we can offer you: A unique opportunity to make a substantial contribution to a major strategic project for both Imperial and the University of Cumbria. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year (pro rata) and generous pension schemes) Be part of a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: The role is available on a 1 FTE basis. For more information about the Pears Cumbria School of Medicine, please visit https://cumbriamed.ac.uk If you require any further details on the role, please contact: Dr Andrew Childs, Head of Medical Sciences and Assessment of the Pears Cumbria School of Medicine, email: a.childs@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29533": {
      "title": "Information and Systems Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; Faculty of Medicine Centre; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06019 About the role: Do you want to apply your technical skills and experience of information systems and technology? This is an opportunity to put your technical skills into practice, helping contribute to maintaining, improving and automating the digital systems and processes that support students and staff across the world class Imperial College School of Medicine. The School of Medicine is one of the world’s top medical schools at one of the world’s most respected universities. At the forefront of medical education, your work here will help create the next generation of doctors and scientists—benefiting patients and populations globally. What you would be doing: You will: Provide technical support for the School’s Student Database (SDB), including data entry, checking and data quality activities, and assist with the maintenance of student records and electronic folders. Support the technical administration of student surveying and feedback systems, assisting with survey distribution, monitoring, troubleshooting and the implementation of new functionality and automation. Support the development and maintenance of online forms, applications and automated workflows using Power Apps, Power Automate, Power BI and across Faculty processes. Carry out testing, troubleshooting and routine maintenance of digital systems and automated solutions, maintaining accurate testing records, version controls, technical documentation, user guides and process records. Contribute to Faculty-wide IT and information management projects, including system enhancements, implementation activities, and the audit and mapping of Faculty data and information processes. What we are looking for: A technically capable and detail-oriented administrator with hands-on experience of working with data systems, automated workflows and online platforms in a professional environment. You will bring a logical approach to problem-solving, a strong eye for accuracy and the confidence to support and maintain technical solutions that underpin key processes across the Faculty of Medicine. Proficiency in tools such as Power Apps, Power Automate, Power BI and Qualtrics, with experience of relational databases and programming languages such as SQL or Python, and enthusiasm to utilise technology to enhance how we manage and use information. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days' annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. We are a friendly and collaborative team, with a passion for providing students and staff with high-quality programme support and ensuring an excellent experience for all our stakeholders. Further information: This is a full-time, open-ended post based primarily at South Kensington Campus, with occasional travel to other London campuses and hospital sites. Should you require any further details on the role , please contact Maureen Agu – Information Governance Manager ( m.agu@imperial.ac.uk )"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29541": {
      "title": "Linux SysAdmin and Systems Programmer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Department of Computing; Professional / Support Services Reference: ENG04063 About the role: The role will be based within the Department of Computing’s Computing Support Group (CSG), contributing to the delivery and maintenance of reliable, secure and effective computing services across the Department. Working within a highly collaborative and multidisciplinary environment, the post holder will support a diverse technical infrastructure comprising Linux and Windows servers and workstations, Mac workstations, and an increasing range of virtualised services. The role will involve working closely with systems administrators, developers and user support professionals to ensure that systems and services remain resilient, efficient and responsive to the needs of staff and students. As part of the CSG, the post holder will have the opportunity to contribute to the development of innovative technical solutions while supporting the Department’s commitment to excellence in computing. The role requires a collaborative approach, strong problem-solving skills and the ability to work effectively across different areas of IT. By helping to maintain and improve the Department’s infrastructure and services, the post holder will play an important role in supporting the Department’s teaching, research and day-to-day operations, while contributing to an environment that values innovation, continuous improvement and multidisciplinary working. What you would be doing: In this role, you would be responsible for providing reliable, secure and high-performing Linux computing services to support the Department’s teaching, research and administrative activities. You would work on maintaining and improving departmental infrastructure, with a focus on automation, scalability, performance and resilience. This would include systems administration, monitoring, patching, access control, backups and vulnerability management, as well as contributing to the development of new services and infrastructure solutions. You would also work closely with academic staff, researchers, teaching fellows and technical colleagues to understand their requirements and provide effective systems, network and user support. A key part of the role would be supporting students and teaching activities across undergraduate, postgraduate and PhD programmes. You would help maintain teaching laboratories, development environments, research computing resources and systems used for student projects, ensuring that these services are accessible, secure and fit for purpose. You would also collaborate with College ICT teams and other technical groups to deliver and support shared services and infrastructure. In addition, you would provide technical advice to the CSG Manager, identifying opportunities to improve existing services, automate processes and introduce new technologies that respond to changing teaching, research and administrative needs. You would also contribute to the wider management and development of the Department’s IT services. This would include providing technical input into the procurement, evaluation, installation, configuration and lifecycle management of hardware, software and infrastructure, while maintaining accurate asset and purchasing records. An important responsibility would be ensuring that departmental systems follow appropriate information security, data management and IT policies. Where security or operational incidents occur, you would be involved in detecting, investigating, escalating and resolving issues promptly, as well as identifying preventative measures to reduce the likelihood of similar incidents occurring in the future. What we are looking for: We are looking for someone with a degree in Computer Science or a closely related subject, or equivalent experience, with demonstrable experience in areas such as Linux and Windows systems administration, networking , security, backups, databases , web hosting and configuration management . Experience with virtualisation , cloud services, systems programming, application development or student information systems would be desirable . You should have strong interpersonal, organisational and analytical skills , with the ability to communicate clearly with staff and students, manage multiple priorities effectively and work collaboratively as part of a team. A proactive approach to learning new technologies , diagnosing and resolving technical issues, improving services and maintaining high quality standards is essential, alongside a commitment to continuous improvement and providing a positive and effective service to users . What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including generous annual leave and pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time post. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Marina Hall on marina.hall@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29530": {
      "title": "Maintenance Technician (Mechanical)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01423 About the role: Imperial College London is a world Top 10 university with an international reputation for excellence in teaching and research. The Maintenance Team forms a vital part of the Property Division who help manage one of the largest built estates in the University sector. The department delivers a comprehensive range of services to an academic community who are world leaders in the provision of Engineering, Medicine, Natural Sciences, Business Management, Research and Teaching across a multi-site estate. As a Mechanical Maintenance Technician for Imperial, you will be expected to work as part of a team providing mechanically biased support to our Academic, Research, Teaching and Administrative functions. Our Maintenance Team carry out Reactive and PPM works to assets, project support and infrastructure to ensure all Imperial activities can be carried out with minimal disruption. We expect relevant qualifications and/or experience against the essential criteria of the role and competencies, which should be demonstrated in your personal statement with detailed information on how your skills, knowledge and experience are relevant to this role. What you would be doing: You will carry out reactive and planned maintenance and repairs to mechanical equipment and all associated systems. You will undertake a proactive approach in ensuring all plant and equipment is fully operational and fit for purpose. You will undertake appropriate administrative tasks by fulfilling the work report on the CAFM system Planon. You will maintain safe workplace practice and procedures in accordance with the requirements of Health & Safety Legislation. You will undertake other duties which may arise commensurate with the grade of the post as directed by the line manager/supervisor. What we are looking for: Gained a recognised apprenticeship and attained an appropriate NVQ level 3 in Mechanical discipline with knowledge of hot water systems, pumps, compressed air systems, chillers, AHU’s etc. Have a proven ability to work within a busy multi-tasking team with a flexible approach to daily work and a proactive approach to resolving problems. Excellent communication and customer care skills. Understanding of Health & Safety, preferably IOSH trained Be computer literate and able to use MS Office products: Word, Teams, Outlook for e-mail, and calendar appointments. Knowledge of Planon and use of tablet would be an advantage. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual and mandatory leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: The working hours are 35 hours per week, on a 7-day rotating shift pattern (Hammersmith Campus). Please be aware that this role will not permit skilled worker route sponsorship. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Maria White - m.r.white@imperial.ac.uk Closing Date: Midnight on Sunday, 01 November 2026"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29538": {
      "title": "Partnerships Junior Officer, Faculty of Medicine",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Provost’s Team; £38,608 - £41,666 per annum; Enterprise; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRO01455 About the role: Would you like to be part of a successful team turning Imperial’s research into inventions that tackle real-world problems? Join us and contribute to generating major impact across one of the World’s leading universities. Are you a highly motivated individual with a passion for research and technology? Do you have a background in medicine and life sciences? Would you like to be introduced to industry partnerships and develop your career in successful creation and management of partnerships? If so, we have the perfect opportunity for you, join us in supporting Imperial’s industry partnerships activities. Based in our Faculty of Medicine, you will be supporting the Partnerships team . The main aim of the Partnerships Team is to maximise the impact of Imperial’s academic discoveries by stimulating, identifying, managing and progressing opportunities to develop research and technologies through collaboration and commercialisation. To deliver successfully its mission, the Partnerships team members need to demonstrate a thorough and up-to-date understanding of the College’s expertise to respond quickly and effectively to relevant enquiries and opportunities. A sound awareness of the external environment – markets, technology/science trends, industry news – is also essential for designing and implementing the Partnerships team’s engagement and commercialisation strategy. You will gain experience of partnership creation and management. The main aim of our team is to maximise the impact of Imperial’s academic research by stimulating, creating, managing, and progressing opportunities to develop research and technologies through collaboration and partnerships. Imperial is a leader in industry-university collaborations and technology commercialisation and you will be an integral member of Imperial’s Enterprise Division , working with leading global companies and our world class researchers to develop research and technologies through collaboration and commercialisation. Imperial recognises and rewards collaboration and innovation. We take pride in bringing tangible benefits to society by making an impact in industry and encourage academics and research staff to work across departments, fields, and institutional boundaries to tackle industry challenges. We bring businesses, researchers and students together in a flourishing entrepreneurial ecosystem, which you can be part of. Join us. What you would be doing: As a service provider to the Faculty of Medicine, the successful candidates will work alongside experienced professionals on the following activities: Supporting Corporate Partnerships team in generating and managing collaborative research, education and translation opportunities with corporate partners, both directly funded and via public funders such as Research Councils, government departments, Innovate UK and the European Union Map internal expertise/capability in relevant areas specific to corporate opportunities e.g. within the sectors in areas of medicine and life sciences. Market research, (e.g., monitoring of emerging trends, sector analysis and industrial organisations) to understand market strategies, research challenges and technical priorities, to support development of Faculty technologies Marketing, communication of University research capability and technology through creation of marketing material and planning of engagement activities (e.g., presentations, workshops, showcasing events) Draft communications to external partners, (e.g., industry, government etc) to publicise opportunities for engagement with Faculty led initiatives and external funding opportunities Assist with due diligence processes on existing and prospective industry partners Operate as an autonomous individual, managing own work activities without detailed and continuous supervision, to contribute to the achievement of Partnerships objectives and maintain and improve efficiency What we are looking for: We are looking for applicants with passion for driving industry-academia collaborations. To be successful in this role you will have a background in medicine, or life sciences, preferably evidenced by a MSc or PhD. An understanding of the academic environment, and in particular technology or partnerships management, preferably gained through previous experience of working within HEI or industry is highly desirable. Strong delivery focus, excellent communication and administration skills coupled with a keen interest in industry partnerships are also essential. What we can offer you: This career opportunity will maximise to the full your experience and interest in roles such as Partnerships Officer, Translation Officer, Research Assistant, Research and Business Partnerships Officer within the technology, pharmaceutical, chemicals, higher education, government sectors or similar. Further information: This is a full time, fixed term position for 8 months based at White City campus (hybrid). For further information, contact Eunice Anderson, Administration Coordinator, at eunice.anderson@imperial.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29547": {
      "title": "Research Proposals Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; Department of Physics; Professional / Support Services Reference: NAT02304 About the role: The Department of Physics is developing its research funding and fellowship support to provide a consistently high-quality, researcher-focused service that enables researchers to pursue external funding and fellowship opportunities effectively. As Research Proposals Administrator, you will play an important part in that ambition, providing dedicated operational support across the Department’s substantial and varied portfolio of pre-award research funding and fellowship activity. You will be a key point of contact for researchers, fellowship applicants and academic sponsors, providing a professional, timely, responsive and supportive service throughout the application process. You will provide researchers with clear, timely information about relevant funding opportunities and deadlines. You will take day-to-day ownership of defined application processes, keeping applications on track, following up outstanding actions and keeping applicants informed. You will provide clear first-line guidance on established processes and requirements, using judgement to resolve issues and involve specialist or senior colleagues where needed. An important part of the role will be to support the delivery and continued development of the Department’s wider fellowship activity, including internal selection processes, applicant support, information sessions, peer review, mentoring, mock interviews, support funds and other fellowship-development initiatives. Based within the Physics Knowledge Hub, a recently established team bringing together coordinated support across a number of Departmental activities, you will work in an environment where ideas and practical suggestions are welcomed. The Department is committed to supporting you to succeed and develop in the role through relevant training, continuing professional development and engagement with professional networks. What you would be doing: Act as a key operational point of contact for researchers seeking support with pre-award research funding and fellowship applications. Review funding call requirements and provide clear first-line guidance on established funder and Imperial processes, deadlines and application requirements. Maintain a proactive overview of planned and active applications, monitoring deadlines, approvals and outstanding actions, following up as required and keeping applicants informed of progress or delays. Support researchers with Imperial research management and funder submission systems, including Worktribe. Work with applicants on the preparation and checking of application budgets and costings. Liaise with Faculty and other professional services teams to progress applications and resolve administrative queries. Coordinate Departmental fellowship Expressions of Interest and internal selection processes, including applications, review arrangements, communications, follow-up and the coordination of applicant feedback. Act as the main operational point of contact for Departmentally endorsed fellowship applicants through to submission, providing clear guidance to applicants and academic sponsors on actions, responsibilities and deadlines. Support the delivery of the Department’s fellowship support programme, including information sessions, peer review, mentoring, mock interviews, support funds, travel or visitor support and other agreed initiatives. Maintain accurate and accessible information on research funding and fellowship opportunities and contribute to the Department’s regular funding communications. Work closely with the Physics Business Development Manager to ensure opportunities progressing to formal application move smoothly into established pre-award processes. Maintain accurate and up-to-date records of applications, submissions, outcomes. Work collaboratively across the Knowledge Hub and with colleagues across Imperial, sharing knowledge and ideas and contributing to a professional, supportive and continuously improving service. What we are looking for: Central to the role is demonstrable experience of delivering a professional, timely, responsive and supportive administrative service to customers or service users. You will also have experience of coordinating formal, deadline-driven processes involving detailed information, documentation and multiple stakeholders. You will be able to organise and prioritise a varied workload involving competing and time-critical deadlines, and interpret detailed guidance and requirements. You will be able to respond to routine and non-routine enquiries, explain processes and requirements clearly and helpfully, and use judgement to resolve issues or recognise when specialist or senior advice is required. You will have excellent attention to detail, strong interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to build constructive working relationships. Good numerical skills are essential, including confidence working with budgets, calculations and financial information. You will also be confident working with detailed guidance, records, spreadsheets and digital systems, and able and willing to learn new systems, processes and areas of knowledge quickly. Previous experience in research funding, research administration or higher education, or of supporting grant or fellowship applications or using research management systems, would be advantageous. Candidates must be able to demonstrate the essential administrative, customer-service, organisational and process-coordination experience set out above. Training will be provided in Imperial’s research funding systems and procedures. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: The role is available on a full-time basis. We are also open to applications from candidates seeking part-time working arrangements of at least 80% full-time equivalent. The Knowledge Hub team currently works on campus at South Kensington for a minimum of three days per week. Appointment would normally be made at or near the lower end of the advertised salary range, depending on relevant experience. Interviews are expected to be held on Tuesday 24 November in person at South Kensington campus. For further information about the position contact: Louise Hayward, Knowledge Hub Manager l.hayward@imperial.ac.uk For queries regarding the application process contact: Physics Knowledge Hub physicsconnect@imperial.ac.uk The Department of Physics is proud to hold an Athena SWAN Silver Award, recognising our commitment to fostering a supportive, inclusive and thriving community. We value the strength that comes from diverse perspectives, and we warmly welcome applications from talented people of all backgrounds to join our vibrant community supported by initiatives that promote collaboration, inclusivity and professional development. Find out more about our inclusive environment."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29528": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate / Full Professors in Economics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: Business School; Internationally Competitive; Academic & Teaching Reference: BUS00897 About the role: Imperial Business School is seeking qualified applicants for faculty positions in its Department of Economics & Public Policy at the rank of Assistant, Associate and Full Professor in Economics. Applicants for the position of Assistant Professor should have the potential to publish innovative, rigorous, and relevant contributions in top academic journals and to develop into excellent teachers. Candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position should demonstrate considerable excellence in research, teaching and service. The appointed candidates will be encouraged to proactively engage in research grant applications; Imperial’s strategy “Science for Humanity” provides significant opportunities for interdisciplinary research. As with most business schools, most of the department’s teaching focuses on applied economics, management and analytics modules. The first students on our innovative undergraduate degree in Economics, Finance and Data Science finished their studies this summer; our MSc programmes include Economics and Strategy for Business and an interdisciplinary degree in Climate Change, Management and Finance. Our interdisciplinary MSc in AI, Economics and Policy, taught jointly with the Department of Computing, will admit its first students in September 2027. Applications from women, BAME and other groups who are currently underrepresented in the School are especially welcome. Reasonable adjustments will be made at each stage of the recruitment process for neurodiversity and disability associated needs. The initial interviews will be conducted virtually in December, and fly-outs will be in January/February. What you would be doing: You will contribute to Imperial’s mission as a leading research-intensive university, developing your own range of research and teaching on our degree programmes. You are encouraged to develop research collaborations within and outside Imperial, and we provide travel funding to facilitate this. You will share in the administrative tasks of running the department and the Business School, to an extent appropriate to your career stage. What we are looking for: You will have a PhD in Economics or a related field, or (for an assistant professor) expect to have one before taking up the post You will be able to demonstrate capability for high-quality innovative research in your area, with a strong research agenda and pipeline. Applicants for associate or full professor should be developing or established international reputation for their research (commensurate with career stage). You will have experience (appropriate to your level) in teaching economics to university students. Full information on duties, responsibilities and criteria can be found in the attached Job Description. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year including public holiday and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Please see our Further Particulars for more information about the College and the benefits available to staff. Further information: Closing deadline: 03 November 2026, 23:59 midnight GMT These are full-time posts. Associate and Full Professors are offered permanent contracts. Assistant Professors will be offered a seven-year fixed term contract and will be expected to apply for promotion to Associate Professor during their sixth year. Those who are promoted to Associate Professor will receive a permanent contract. Apply through the Imperial College portal ( https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/ and search job reference BUS00897, read the Job Description and Application Instructions document and submit the following documents: Application form CV Cover letter that explains your reasons for applying to Imperial Research statement Teaching statement Evidence of teaching scores (where applicable) 3 research papers (one paper for current / recent PhD students) Those applying for an assistant professor post should also ask three referees to email a reference letter to EPPrecruit@imperial.ac.uk by the closing deadline. If you require any further details on the role, please contact: Professor Richard Green – r.green@imperial.ac.uk If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help. Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29519": {
      "title": "Clinical Trials Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £55,434 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06003 About the role: We are looking for a Clinical Trial Manager to join our team. The Imperial Clinical Trials Unit-Cancer (ICTU-Ca) , led by Professor Michael Seckl through its close association with the Imperial Clinical Trials Unit and Imperial Experimental Cancer Medicine Centre , provides a core accredited environment to develop and conduct world-class cancer clinical trials led by Imperial College. With a major focus on precision medicine and proof of concept clinical trials, the ICTU-Ca currently undertakes studies in the phase Ib to phase III space across multiple sites of disease, at both National and International levels. The cancer trial portfolio exploits the research expertise in our Department to enable trials that include new and exciting imaging modalities, translational research initiatives and innovative study designs as they evolve. ICTU-Ca forms part of the Imperial Clinical Trials Unit (ICTU). Cancer is just one of several therapeutic areas including cardiovascular and metabolic disease, respiratory, HIV / infectious diseases, surgery and emergency and critical care. These disciplines all come together under the overarching, UKCRC registered ICTU. What you would be doing: You will undertake the day-to-day management of one or more clinical trials currently active in the ICTU-Ca portfolio. You will be responsible for all aspects of trial coordination to ensure that each study is conducted to the highest scientific and regulatory standards, to time and within budget. What we are looking for: You will have proven experience in the setup, coordination and delivery of trials using investigational medicinal products (IMPs). You must be motivated and well organised with the ability to work effectively to deadlines both independently and as part of a team. Full essential and desirable criteria are detailed in the job description. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity Benefit from sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes) Get access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day 1, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and a cycle-to-work scheme Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel Be part of a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This role is full-time and fixed term for one year in the first instance. You will need to complete an online application. Should you require any further details on the role please contact Mr Philip Badman – p.badman@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29514": {
      "title": "Commercial Analyst",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01451 About the role: Imperial is a world Top 10 university with an international reputation for excellence in teaching and research. The Campus Operations Department are responsible for supporting the operational Hospitality Teams of various cafes, restaurants, bars and events located across all their Campuses to provide affordable and high-quality food and services for the Imperial community and external clients. They create a valuable resource for Imperial, which in turn supports our academic community of world leaders in the provision of Engineering, Medicine, Natural Sciences, Business Management, Research and Teaching. As Commercial Analyst, you will help shape the financial performance and future direction of Imperial’s diverse hospitality and accommodation portfolio, with annual turnover of around £50m. Supporting more than 5,000 student rooms, a major summer accommodation business and 30+ catering outlets, you will turn complex financial and operational data into clear, actionable insight for senior leaders. You will be the vital link between finance and operations, helping teams understand profitability, control costs and make confident investment decisions. Working closely with Student and Commercial Accommodation colleagues, you will provide commercial analysis that supports efficient operations, robust VAT and accounting compliance, and a high-performing, sustainable hospitality offer across Imperial’s campuses What you would be doing: You will provide the financial analysis and commercial insight that helps Imperial’s hospitality, catering and accommodation operations make confident, profitable decisions. You will monitor financial performance through management reports, dashboards and variance analysis; assess revenue, labour, cost of sales and gross profit; and develop robust budgets, forecasts, pricing and profitability models. Your analysis will directly support initiatives including accommodation pricing, summer business growth, new outlets and investment projects, identifying opportunities to improve efficiency, recover costs and maximise returns. You will work closely with operational managers, senior leaders, Finance, ICT and external partners to turn complex information into clear, actionable recommendations. You will enhance reporting tools and data quality, establish meaningful KPIs and lead regular performance reviews, while ensuring compliance with financial regulations, VAT requirements and agent/principal principles. By providing trusted commercial advice, maintaining strong governance and promoting financial accountability, you will help embed continuous improvement across the Hospitality and Property Services portfolio. What we are looking for: Degree-level education (or equivalent experience) in finance, business, or an analytical discipline; part-qualified or qualified accountant (ACA, ACCA, CIMA, or equivalent). Proven experience supporting budgeting, forecasting, financial planning, and management accounting cycles. Experience analysing, interpreting, and reporting financial performance within a large, complex, multi-site organisation. Advanced Excel and financial modelling capability, with experience building, maintaining, and improving complex financial models and reporting tools such as Power BI. Experience in hospitality, accommodation, higher education, or commercial services, including familiarity with relevant systems such as Procure Wizard, EPOS, or similar. Strong understanding of cost control, margin analysis, VAT in hospitality/accommodation settings, and the Agent/Principal accounting model. Demonstrable ability to communicate complex financial information clearly and persuasively, influencing senior stakeholders through data-driven insight. Excellent analytical, critical-thinking, and problem-solving skills, with a proactive approach to identifying inefficiencies and developing practical solutions. Ability to manage competing priorities, meet tight deadlines under pressure, work collaboratively with diverse teams, and maintain professional partnerships. High standards of integrity, confidentiality, accuracy, equality, diversity and inclusion; commitment to continuous professional development and effective use of Microsoft Office tools. What we can offer you: At Imperial, our values—respect, collaboration, excellence, integrity, and innovation—guide everything we do. You’ll be part of a supportive and forward-thinking team, working on projects that directly contribute to the safety and wellbeing of our community. We are committed to creating an inclusive working environment and welcome applications from all backgrounds. We particularly encourage interest from individuals who are underrepresented in our workforce. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: Further Information If you require any further details on the role please contact: Ramona Dobrea, Head of Student and Commercial Accommodation Office by email – o.dobrea@imperial.ac.uk Closing date: Midnight on Sunday, 8 November 2026"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29517": {
      "title": "Domestic Support Worker",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £30,699 per annum; Early Years Education Centre; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01452 About the role: We are a 210 place Early Years Education Centre with an outstanding Ofsted rating and reputation, and we serve the community of Imperial London caring for the staff and student’s children. We are looking to recruit one part-time Domestic Support Worker who is committed to the cleanliness of the Centre which provides professional high-quality childcare and education in an environment that is clean, safe, and hygienic for each of the children, and the staff and parents who attend the setting. Safeguarding children is our priority, and all post holders are required to adhere to the EYEC’s Safeguarding children and Whistle blowing policy. All post holders will be required to obtain a satisfactory enhanced level Disclosure and Barring check. What you would be doing: You will work from 06:00-09:00 Monday to Friday and be responsible for providing a high standard of hygiene and maintaining the cleanliness of the Centre’s indoor and outdoor environment by performing various daily cleaning duties. You will also be required to provide regular deep cleaning, identify areas that need particular attention and report any health and safety concerns to ensure a safe environment for all users of the Centre. What we are looking for: Someone willing to undertake training as and when required A willingness to receive training including, general knowledge of Health & Safety issues and Control of Substances Hazardous to Health (COSHH) Previous experience in a domestic role and working as part of a team Previous experience using equipment for polishing and cleaning various surfaces Excellent hygiene practices Understanding the safe storage and use of chemicals, germs and the prevention of spread of infection Understanding the importance of safeguarding children Be able to communicate effectively in English with all users of the Centre Be physically fit to undertake the full duties of the post requirements Show flexibility in relation to time, new ideas and helping other team members Comply with the protective clothing policy and have a clean, smart and tidy appearance at all times Honesty and integrity are essential as is discretion and confidentiality What we can offer you: Working hours are Monday to Friday from 06:00-09:00 Events to come together and celebrate from summer parties to Christmas breakfasts The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including the possibility of flexible working, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: If you require any further details on the role please contact: Liz-Anne Des Vignes, Deputy Head of EYEC – [ l.desvignes@imperial.ac.uk ]. Please note that this post is part-time at 15 hours per week, the actual salary payable will be calculated on a pro rata basis. Closing date: Midnight on Thursday, 22 nd October 2026."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29521": {
      "title": "IT Service Excellence Analyst Apprentice",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £31,677 - £34,810 per annum; Information & Communication Technologies; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01447 About the role: Are you interested in starting a career in IT support and customer service while gaining a nationally recognised qualification? As an IT Service Excellence Analyst Apprentice, you will join Imperial's ICT department and develop the skills needed to deliver excellent IT support within a world-leading university. Working alongside experienced colleagues, you will provide first-line support for ICT services, help users resolve issues, and gain practical experience while studying towards a Level 3 Digital Support Technician apprenticeship qualification. What you would be doing: You will: Act as a first point of contact for routine ICT enquiries through a range of support channels. Log and manage incidents, service requests and enquiries using our service management systems. Provide basic technical support and troubleshooting using established processes and guidance. Maintain accurate records, documentation and knowledge articles. Support day-to-day ICT service operations and administrative activities. Work closely with ICT teams and service users to deliver an excellent customer experience. Spend one day each week undertaking off-the-job learning with our apprenticeship training provider while working towards a Level 3 Digital Support Technician qualification. What we are looking for: We are looking for someone who: Is enthusiastic about developing a career in IT support and service management. Has excellent verbal and written communication skills. Enjoys helping people and delivering a positive customer experience. Is organised and able to manage their time effectively. Can follow processes and procedures accurately. Is willing to learn new technologies and develop new skills. Has GCSE English and Mathematics at Grade 4/C or above (or equivalent). Understands why an apprenticeship is their preferred route into an IT career. Experience of customer service, administration, Microsoft Office applications, IT systems, or working within a team environment would be beneficial but is not essential. What we can offer you: A unique opportunity to start your IT career within a world-leading university. Hands-on experience supporting a large and diverse technology environment. A structured apprenticeship programme leading to a Level 3 Digital Support Technician qualification. Support and mentoring from experienced ICT professionals. The opportunity to develop valuable technical, customer service and professional skills. A sector-leading benefits package, including 43 days annual leave and a generous pension scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time post on a fixed-term contract for 18 months The apprenticeship training is delivered by Learning Curve Group , with apprentices spending, on average, one day per week undertaking off-the-job learning. Successful completion of the programme will provide a nationally recognised Level 3 qualification and support future career opportunities within Imperial or the wider IT industry. Please note that applicants must meet the eligibility requirements for apprenticeship funding, including having the right to work in the UK and meeting the applicable residency requirements. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Mylene Cannon – m.cannon@imperial.ac.uk."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29511": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Bioengineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04056 About the role: Join the Ballester group at Imperial College London as a post-doctoral Research Associate and work on a project to build an integrated AI-driven functional genomic and chemoproteomic platform to improve antifungal drug discovery, targeting the critical f ungal pathogen Candida auris. What you would be doing: You will develop AI models to predict whole-cell antifungal activity across ultra-large, synthesis-on-demand chemical spaces. In addition, you will develop AI models to predict antifungal-induced toxicity across multiple biological contexts, including organ-specific iPSC-derived cell lines. Using pre- and post-treatment scRNA-seq profiles of C. auris , you will develop models to predict the susceptibility or resistance of individual C. auris isolates to specific antifungal treatments based solely on their pre-treatment transcriptional profiles. These models will support the selection of effective drug therapies and the identification of optimal treatment combinations for antifungal-resistant C. auris strains. Some recent publications of the Ballester group in this broad area are: https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/advs.76959 https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13321-025-01039-8 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jare.2024.01.024 https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-023-03948-w https://www.nature.com/articles/s41596-023-00885-w What we are looking for: You will hold a PhD in AI-driven drug discovery, with expertise in areas such as AI-enabled phenotypic drug discovery, as demonstrated by a strong publication record in the field. Candidates with a strong track record in related areas, such as precision oncology and biomedical bioinformatics, will also be considered. What we can offer you: The opportunity to co-supervise master students and co-author the resulting publications. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. As a member of research staff you have 10 development days to use to develop your skills and explore your career prospects Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This project is funded by the Wellcome Trust and will be carried out in collaboration with other Imperial groups within the Fleming Initiative , GSK and the Dundee Drug Discovery Unit . This is a fulltime, fixed term role for 24 months. Please note that this is a PhD-level role, thus if the successful candidate has not yet been officially awarded a PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant . For informal enquiries about this position please contact Dr Pedro Ballester with your CV and list of publications (p.ballester@imperial.ac.uk). Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29526": {
      "title": "Research Technician, Analytical Chemistry",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £42,505 - £ 43,353 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06016 About the role: An exciting opportunity has arisen for a post as a Technician within the Surgery and Cancer Department. The purpose of this post is to provide high-level technical expertise in support of cutting-edge research focused on non-invasive diagnostics using volatile organic compound (VOC) analysis. The post holder will work within a dynamic and collaborative research group, contributing to a portfolio of clinical and translational studies applying breath and skin VOC analysis to the early diagnosis and monitoring of diseases including cancer and neurological conditions. What you would be doing: As a Research Technician you will: Perform VOC analysis using state-of-the-art analytical platforms including GC-ToF-MS, GC-FID, SIFT-MS, and thermal desorption techniques Collect, prepare and process biological samples such as breath and skin swabs, following standardised protocols and rigorous quality control procedures Maintain and troubleshoot analytical instrumentation, ensuring optimal performance at all times Develop and maintain Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) for analytical methods and sample handling Support data acquisition, processing and quality control using relevant software platforms Contribute to the day-to-day running and organisation of the laboratory Provide technical support and guidance to researchers, students and collaborators within the group Liaise with clinical and research teams to coordinate sample collection across study sites Maintain accurate records in compliance with data management and GDPR requirements Contribute to the preparation of research outputs, including data for publications and grant applications This is a hands-on role ideal for someone with a keen eye for detail and a passion for supporting translational research. What we are looking for: We are looking for a motivated and skilled individual with hands-on substantial experience in volatile organic compound analysis, using techniques such as GC-ToF-MS, GC-FID, SIFT-MS, and/or thermal desorption. A degree in Chemistry, Analytical Chemistry, Biochemistry or a related discipline is required, along with strong laboratory skills, excellent attention to detail, and the ability to maintain and troubleshoot analytical instrumentation. Previous experience in VOC analysis in biological matrices such as breath or skin, and familiarity with clinical or translational research settings, is essential. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This position is full time and for 24 months fixed term in the first instance with possibility of renewal. As this post is exempt from the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974, a satisfactory Disclosure and Barring Service (DBS) check , at the appropriate level, will be required for the successful candidate. Should you have any queries please contact: Dr Ilaria Belluomo i.belluomo@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29497": {
      "title": "Project Management Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £35,987 - £37,934 per annum; National Heart & Lung Institute; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED05965 About the role: We are seeking a highly motivated individual with strong administrative experience to join Imperial College’s Cystic Fibrosis and Chronic Lung Infections (CF&CLI) group. Directed by Professor Jane Davies, the group comprises postdoctoral researchers, postgraduate students, research fellows, and clinicians running numerous research projects ( https://profiles.imperial.ac.uk/j.c.davies ). You will support the efficient and effective operation of the research administration. What you would be doing: You will have a key role in the daily administration of the CF&CLI and clinical trials research team, undertaking work disseminated from the Research Project Manager. You will manage a variety of administrative tasks including, assisting in publication submissions, organising meetings, ordering consumables, co-ordinating travel, processing expenses, provide administrative support for clinical trial operations and study visits, monitoring and updating clinical research filing records. What we are looking for: You have proven administration skills You can communicate effectively at all levels, in person, by telephone and in writing, in a confidential manner You have excellent computer and word processing skills You can show initiative and manage your own workload What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity Benefit from sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year (pro-rata) and generous pension schemes) Get access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day 1, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and a cycle-to-work scheme Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel Be part of a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time (part-time considered), fixed-term contract for 1 year initially. To discuss please contact Gina Rivellini ( g.rivellini@imperial.ac.uk ) or Clare Saunders ( c.saunders@imperial.ac.uk ). Email addresses are for enquiries only."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29506": {
      "title": "Recruitment & Admissions Officer (Specialised Suite)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Business School; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; Professional / Support Services Reference: BUS00896 About the role: Would you like to help shape the future of our Business School community? Imperial is seeking a Recruitment & Admissions Officer to join our team and support end-to-end pipeline engagement across our MSc programmes. You will be empowered to attract and select exceptional candidates who will benefit business and improve society through the power of innovative thinking. What you would be doing: You will support the recruitment and admissions processes for Imperial Business School’s Management Suite of Masters’ programmes – engaging with the full applicant journey from initial enquiry through to enrolment. You will play a key role in shaping the composition, quality and diversity of our cohorts, ensuring a smooth, professional and engaging experience for every applicant. You will be guided by data-driven recruitment strategies, delivering application and selection processes with rigour and efficiency, and ensuring a professional, personalised experience for every candidate. Through targeted engagement, events and communications, you will play a part in developing exceptional student cohorts that contribute to the global reputation of the programmes and the institution. Reporting to the Recruitment & Admissions Manager, you will collaborate closely with colleagues across the Recruitment & Admissions team, the Business School and the wider College. You will use data and insights to identify improvements, maintain strong relationships with internal and external stakeholders, and represent Imperial at domestic, international and online events. This people-focused role will require both analytical ability and sound professional judgement. What we are looking for: Experience in recruitment, admissions or a comparable customer-focused environment, ideally within the Higher Education sector. Proven ability to administer our application pipeline from enquiry to enrolment, building effective relationships and rigorously assessing candidate suitability to meet ambitious recruitment targets. Excellent communication, presentation and interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage with a diverse global candidate pool. Strong working knowledge of CRM systems and selection tools. A commitment to continuous process improvement, using insights and feedback to enhance the candidate journey. A proactive team player with the ability to work independently, manage competing priorities and deliver under pressure. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This role is for a fixed-term contract until mid-September 2027. Interviews are expected to be conducted during the week commencing 2nd November, and are intended to be conducted in-person on our South Kensington campus. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Holly Cavanna, Recruitment & Admissions Manager – h.cavanna@imperial.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29510": {
      "title": "Regular Giving and Legacy Giving Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: President’s Team; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Advancement; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRS00576 About the role: We’re searching for a Regular Giving and Legacy Giving Manager to oversee a sector-leading, multi-channel appeal programme to increase numbers of gifts and donors to the University. Following the launch of Imperial’s bold and ambitious strategy we are preparing to launch a comprehensive fundraising campaign. The Regular Giving and Legacy Giving team will play a pivotal role in bringing about a step-change in regular, mid-value and legacy donations to the University. The postholder will oversee and implement the overall strategy and supporter journey for alumni and donors primarily through direct mail, telephone, email and social media. This is an exciting role with opportunities for the postholder to oversee an award-winning acquisition campaign, a brand-new mid-value programme and a successful legacy marketing programme. The post holder will be instrumental in building collaboration with the Alumni Engagement team to promote philanthropy and take alumni on a journey to their first gift. If you are a talented fundraising professional, who loves to collaborate, and has a proven track record in delivering excellent regular giving programmes in either the Higer Education or charity sector, we would love to hear from you. This is a maternity cover role of up to 10 months, starting in December 2026. What you would be doing: Working across the Regular Giving & Legacy Giving team and managing 2 Regular Giving Officer roles , you will oversee supporter journeys for donors, working on cases for support, appeal development and data segmentation. You will have the opportunity to pilot new ideas as well as delivering a mature and sector-leading programme. Working across Advancement, you will build relationships with key internal stakeholders especially the Alumni Engagement team, Major Gift, and Event teams to capitalise on opportunities to embed regular giving and legacy messaging in all University activities. What we are looking for: A successful track record, in delivering direct-marketing fundraising in Higher Education or the charity sector A proven track record of line management and professionally developing members of staff The ability to build strong relationships and collaboration among key stakeholders and teams A significant and demonstrable experience of managing and organising projects to agreed budgets, targets and timescales A significant and demonstrable experience of developing and evaluating compelling and response driven copy and creative A significant and demonstrable experience of interrogating, analysing, manipulating and interpreting large, complex data sets for targeting and segmentation purposes Experience of liaising with and managing a variety of internal stakeholders and obtaining buy in and support for projects Experience of working with external agencies (creative agencies, fundraising consultants, copywriters, mailing houses and printers) Experience of working effectively in a changing, fast paced and innovative environment. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including up to 43 days’ annual leave and a generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time post (35 hours per week) on a fixed-term contract for up to 10 months. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Sacha Anthony, sacha.anthony@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29509": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04054 About the role: Applications are encouraged for a full time, fixed term Research Associate post of 12 months. This post is funded by the Ofwat Water Breakthrough Challenge 5 (Transform Stream) for the multi-partner industry-academic project titled \"Next-Gen Digestion\". The overall project aims to transform UK wastewater treatment infrastructure by advancing full-scale Microbial Hydrolysis Process (MHP) technologies and optimizing anaerobic digestion performance to maximize renewable energy and resource recovery. Joining the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering under the supervision of Dr. Po-Heng (Henry) Lee, you will work as part of a high-profile consortium comprising United Utilities (project lead), Jacobs UK, Severn Trent Water, Yorkshire Water, Anglian Water, Cranfield University, and Imperial College London. What you would be doing: In this role, you will lead the computational and bioinformatic research arm of Imperial’s contribution to the \"Next-Gen Digestion\" project. Rather than performing routine laboratory analyses, you will focus on interpreting rich multi-omics datasets derived from full-scale operational assets (including Leigh WwTW) and translating these biological insights into predictive computational models. You will combine classical bioinformatic pipelines with advanced hybrid quantum-classical modelling techniques to map microbial gene regulation, substrate conversion, and metabolic interactions. By linking microbial dynamics directly to process performance, your work will uncover key operational levers that control gas production (methane, hydrogen, and hydrogen sulfide), resolve operational bottlenecks, and help optimize full-scale anaerobic digestion systems across the UK water sector. What we are looking for: A PhD in a Relevant Discipline: A PhD (or equivalent professional/research experience) in Environmental Engineering, Computational Biology, Bioinformatics, Chemical Engineering, Quantum Information/Computing, or a closely related discipline. Bioinformatics Expertise: Demonstrated track record in processing and analysing high-throughput multi-omics data (16S rRNA, metagenomics, or metatranscriptomics) in environmental or biological systems. Computational Modelling: Proven experience in computational modelling, algorithm development, or numerical simulation using Python, R, Qiskit, PennyLane, or relevant computational frameworks. Anaerobic Systems Knowledge: Strong biological and process understanding of anaerobic digestion, microbial hydrolysis, or bioenergy recovery mechanisms. Communication & Collaboration: Excellent oral and written communication skills with a proven ability to collaborate effectively in multi-disciplinary and multi-institutional project teams. Publication Record: A track record of publishing high-quality, peer-reviewed scientific papers commensurate with career stage. What we can offer you: The opportunity to work at the interface of environmental biotechnology, microbial bioinformatics, process modelling and emerging computational methods on a research programme focused on next-generation anaerobic digestion and sustainable bioresource recovery. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a fixed-term full-time post for 12 months. Please see the job description for full details and specifications of the post. If you require any further details about the role, please contact Dr Po-Heng Lee – po-heng.lee@imperial.ac.uk Any queries regarding the application process should be directed to Briony Webb at b.webb@imperial.ac.uk . Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. We are an equal opportunity employer and welcome applications from all backgrounds and disabilities. We will ensure that individuals with disabilities are provided reasonable adjustments to participate in the interview process and to perform crucial job functions, as well as other support including staff networks. Please contact us to request accommodation."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29499": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Health Data",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; National Heart & Lung Institute; Researcher Reference: MED06004 About the role: The Research Associate will join an international interdisciplinary team funded through the British Heart Foundation Programme Grant (My Heart Counts). The role focuses on developing and maintaining Google Cloud Platform pipelines for smartphone, wearable, molecular and clinical datasets to advance early detection and monitoring of pulmonary arterial hypertension. You will work closely with computational scientists, clinicians, biologists and international collaborators including Stanford, Cambridge and Sheffield, contributing to new AI models and translational research across NHS and academic sites. What you would be doing: Support and develop data pipelines for My Heart Counts smartphone app data and integrate with clinical, biological (omics), and environmental datasets; manage cloud resources (Google Cloud) and ensure data integrity and reliability; assist researchers across institutions with data queries and troubleshooting; perform descriptive and quantitative data analyses and maintain accurate records; support preparation of reports for sponsors and contribute to publications; present work at internal meetings, conferences and workshops; provide guidance to staff and students and contribute to grant bids. What we are looking for: PhD or equivalent experience in Computer Science or closely related field (statistics, physics,biomedical engineering). *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £48,876 per annum Experience with the Google Cloud Platform (Firebase, Big Query) Practical experience with Python and SQL. Knowledge of wearable, molecular and health data. Knowledge of research methods and statistical procedures. Experience communicating with multidisciplinary researchers. Practical experience with large-scale real-world time-series data. Experience with clinical or biological datasets. Ability to organise work independently and take initiative. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Advanced computing skills including cloud services. What we can offer you: This role offers an excellent opportunity to contribute to a high-profile, interdisciplinary research programme at the interface of AI, medicine, and molecular biology. As part of this position, you will benefit from: Work within Imperial’s NHLI, a world-leading centre in cardiopulmonary research. Collaborate with major academic partners and industry (e.g., Google, Apple, NHS, HDRUK/BHF Data Science Centre). Engage in cutting-edge AI and data-driven translational research. Professional development, conference participation and interdisciplinary training. A supportive environment that values excellence, integrity, innovation, respect and collaboration. Further information: This is a part time (21 h/w) and a fixed-term (2 years ) in the first instance, with the possibility of extension based on project progress and funding availability. If you require any further details on the role, please contact: Allan Lawrie [a.lawrie@imperial.ac.uk]"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29502": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Palaeointensity Determination from Chemical Remanent Magnetisation (ChemPI)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Earth Science & Engineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04048 About the role: This Postdoctoral Research Associate position is part of a NERC-funded project to develop ChemPI, the first absolute palaeointensity protocol for chemical remanent magnetisation (CRM). The aim of this project is to recover reliable ancient magnetic field strengths from rocks whose remanence is chemical rather than thermal in origin, and to apply the new protocol to some of the most consequential open questions in early Earth science. All current methods for determining absolute palaeointensities assume that a rock's remanent magnetisation formed on cooling, a thermoremanent magnetisation. Many rocks, especially from the Precambrian, have instead been remagnetised by chemical processes during metamorphism or metasomatism, producing a chemical remanent magnetisation (CRM) whose intensity existing methods can underestimate by more than half. This matters for some of the biggest open questions in Earth and planetary science, including when the Earth's inner core nucleated, whether early Earth had a magnetosphere capable of protecting a habitable atmosphere, and how magnetic fields shaped the protoplanetary disc. You will combine new micromagnetic models, machine-learning inversion of magnetic hysteresis data and laboratory-synthesised CRMs to build and ground-truth ChemPI, then apply it to 3.7-billion-year-old rocks from Isua, Greenland, to test whether the early Archean geomagnetic field was stronger than currently thought. This position at Imperial will lead the experimental development and application of ChemPI under the guidance of Adrian Muxworthy and Dominik Weiss, whilst the numerical and micromagnetic modelling aspects of the project are undertaken at Edinburgh, and the application to Archean samples is led from Oxford. What you would be doing: Working with the NERC project PI Adrian Muxworthy (Imperial), Co-Is Dominik Weiss (Imperial), Wyn Williams (Edinburgh) and Claire Nichols (Oxford), you will develop and ground-truth the ChemPI palaeointensity protocol. This will involve synthesising samples in the laboratory and collecting natural samples, making room-temperature magnetisation measurements, and developing micromagnetic models to predict CRM behaviour. Working with Edinburgh, the project will extend a machine-learning inversion algorithm and micromagnetic CRM database to interacting particle assemblages, and contribute to a user-friendly, open-access ChemPI web app. In the final phase you will apply ChemPI, with Oxford, to re-examine palaeointensity estimates from 3.7-billion-year-old samples from Isua, Greenland. What we are looking for: You will have experience of palaeomagnetic or rock-magnetic measurement techniques, such as demagnetisation, hysteresis or FORC measurements. You will ideally have experience of laboratory synthesis of materials. You will ideally have some experience of micromagnetic modelling or machine-learning approaches to magnetic data. You will have published or submitted first-author peer-reviewed scientific papers. You will be comfortable working independently and collaboratively across three institutions, with regular visits to Edinburgh and Oxford. You will have a PhD in Earth Science, Physics, Materials Science, Chemistry or a closely related discipline. What we can offer you: A chance to develop the first absolute palaeointensity method for chemical remanent magnetisation, with applications ranging from early Earth habitability to planetary science. The opportunity to work across three leading UK palaeomagnetic and micromagnetic modelling groups (Imperial, Edinburgh and Oxford), combining experiment, machine learning and software development. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution Further information: This is a full-time, fixed term position for up to 30 months. Should you require any further details on the role please contact: Adrian Muxworthy – adrian.muxworthy@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29482": {
      "title": "Head Porter",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £31,677 - £34,810 per annum; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01448 About the role: Are you a friendly, organised professional with a flexible approach and a positive, can-do attitude? If so, we have an exciting opportunity for you to join the Property Division as a Head Porter at one of the world’s top 10 internationally renowned universities. As a Head Porter, you will contribute meaningfully to the smooth and efficient operation of Catering, Stores, and Kitchen areas by coordinating staffing levels, cleaning schedules, and the effective allocation of team members. You will also help create a safe and responsible workplace by supporting compliance with health, safety, environmental, manual handling, COSHH, and waste disposal requirements. What you would be doing: You will supervise Catering and Events Porters, coordinating daily tasks to ensure workloads are shared fairly and completed on time. You will oversee the cleaning and maintenance of kitchens, stores, equipment, utensils, floors, and associated areas, including opening and closing procedures and scheduled deep-cleaning programmes. You will support the safe receipt, storage, ordering, and control of catering supplies, cleaning materials, PPE, and porter equipment. You will also maintain accurate cleaning and temperature records, carry out routine inspections, and help ensure all working practices comply with food hygiene, HACCP, health and safety, and environmental requirements. You will lead regular team briefings, promote a positive working environment, and act as the first point of contact for porter-related issues. You will report defects and maintenance concerns promptly, identify opportunities to improve customer service, and support your Line Manager with any additional operational duties when required. What we are looking for: Previous experience of leading a team with proven ability to motivate and lead team members is an advantage Organisational and time management skills, in order to deliver an efficient and effective service A good understanding of the use of different equipment/machinery within the work area and the ability to operate such equipment/machinery Physically able to operate different equipment/machinery and lift objects of varying loads Full clean UK driving license Ability to use own initiative with limited supervision, to deal with problems which may hinder the delivery of an effective service A flexible approach to working, sufficient to be able to work a 5/7 days per week arrangement, work evenings and weekends, and able to work at any location/catering outlet, as reasonably requested Organisational and time management skills, in order to deliver an efficient and effective service Good communications skills, both written and verbal, in order to present and deliver information in an appropriate manner e.g. timesheets, cleaning records, staff rota What we can offer you: At Imperial, our values respect, collaboration, excellence, integrity, and innovation guide everything we do. You’ll be part of a supportive and forward-thinking team, working on projects that directly contribute to the safety and wellbeing of our community. We are committed to creating an inclusive working environment and welcome applications from all backgrounds. We particularly encourage interest from individuals who are underrepresented in our workforce. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: If you require any further details on the role, please contact: Atesh Luximon, Head of Food Production, Service and Development- a.luximon@imperial.ac.uk . Closing Date: Midnight, Sunday, 18 October 2026"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29492": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04061 About the role: The post is to recruit researchers for “Intelligent Transmission and Processing Lab (ITP Lab)” directed by Prof Geoffrey Li, which focuses on exploiting machine learning for signal processing and wireless communications Other aspects of the post include undertaking project management, collaborate with academic and industry partners and/or supervise multi-disciplinary teams. In addition, the Research Associate will be expected to submit publications to refereed journals and to attract external research funding What you would be doing: Research Duties Include: To take initiatives in the planning of research To direct the work of small research teams To ensure the validity and reliability of data at all times To maintain accurate and complete records of all findings To write reports for submission to research sponsors To present findings to colleagues and at conferences To submit publications to refereed journals To provide guidance to staff and students To attend relevant workshops and conferences as necessary To develop contacts and research collaborations within the College and the wider community To promote the reputation of the group, the Department and the College To provide guidance to PhD Students What we are looking for: Education Research Associate : (or equivalent) in the general area of (a) Machine Learning, (b) Statistical Signal Processing, (c) Wireless Communications, (d) Applied Mathematics, or a closely related discipline or equivalent research or industrial experience. The candidates with experience in hardware implementation are strongly encouraged. Research Assistant: Hold a Masters degree and nearing completion of a PhD or equivalent in the general area of (a) Machine Learning, (b) Statistical Signal Processing, (c) Wireless Communications, (d) Applied Mathematics, or a closely related discipline or equivalent research or industrial experience. The candidates with experience in hardware implementation are strongly encouraged. Experience Practical experience within a research environment and / or publication in relevant and refereed journals Practical experience in a broad range of techniques, including, • optimisation and signal processing systems. • wireless systems • semantic communications Knowledge Knowledge in the general areas of Applied math Machine learning Statistical signal processing Wireless communications Semantic communications Knowledge of research methods and statistical procedures What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: Please note that job descriptions cannot be exhaustive, and the post-holder may be required to undertake other duties, which are broadly in line with the above key responsibilities. For any specific queries regarding the post please contact Prof Geoffrey Li ( g.li@imperial.ac.uk )"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29490": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Dexterous Manipulation and Robot Learning",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Computing; Researcher Reference: ENG04060 About the role: Are you a robot learning researcher eager to push the boundaries of dexterous manipulation? Join DexLab at Imperial College London, led by Dr. Rolandos Potamias and Stefanos Zafeiriou, in a postdoctoral research role to lead transformative projects spanning robot foundation models and real-world dexterous manipulation. DexLab at Imperial is seeking a highly motivated and talented Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA / PostDoc) who has demonstrated competence in conducting cutting-edge robot learning research. The position focuses on building dexterous, high-degree-of-freedom (high-DoF) manipulation policies and robot foundation models, such as vision-language-action models (VLAs), learned from human demonstrations, but is not limited to this. The successful candidate will work on a state-of-the-art robotic platform comprising Sharpa Wave dexterous hands and UR7e arms, contributing both to methodological advances and to the deployment of learned policies on real robots. To this end, the role integrates knowledge from robot learning, computer vision, generative modelling and control. What you would be doing: The post will focus on research topics in probabilistic programming. The specific details of the project are flexible and will be determined based on the interests and strengths of the successful candidate. You will conduct original research at the intersection of dexterous manipulation, robot learning and foundation models, exploring how large-scale human demonstration data (e.g., video, motion capture and teleoperation) can be turned into capable, general-purpose robot behaviour. In particular, you will develop novel algorithms and policies for high-DoF hands and validate them on real hardware. In doing so, you will collaborate with a team of expert researchers in robotics, computer vision and machine learning. We strive to publish in top-tier conferences such as CVPR, NeurIPS, ICLR, ECCV, ICCV, ICRA and RSS. What we are looking for: A strong track record in top conferences and journals in robot learning, machine learning or computer vision such as CVPR, NeurIPS, ICLR, ECCV, ICCV, ICRA and RSS. Demonstrated robot learning experience (e.g., imitation learning, learning from demonstrations, VLAs / robot foundation models, or reinforcement learning) is a must. Strong PyTorch programming skills and working knowledge of ROS are a must. Hands-on experience deploying learned policies on real robots is a must; experience with dexterous hands, teleoperation or motion capture for demonstration collection is a plus. Applicants must hold a PhD in computer science, robotics, engineering or equivalent. You should have proven research record and publications in the relevant areas. Please see job description for a full list of requirements. What we can offer you: The chance to work in an exciting and rapidly developing area, with access to state-of-the-art hardware including Sharpa Wave hands and UR7e arms. This is a chance to grow and establish yourself as a leader in this field. Dedicated mentorship as well as the freedom to explore your own ideas. Working closely with a team of PhD students and postdocs from different backgrounds. The Department of Comput i ng is the top-1 ranked computer science department in the UK, according to REF 2021. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year). Disseminating your research in major robotics, machine learning and computer vision conferences is supported and travel costs will be covered. Further information: Full-time, fixed term position for 12 months to start as soon as possible *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £48,876 per annum. In addition to completing the online application candidates should attach: A full CV with a list of all publications A research statement indicating what you see are interesting research issues relating to the above post and why your expertise is relevant Informal enquiries related to the position should be directed to Dr Rolandos Potamias r.potamias19@imperial.ac.uk For queries regarding the application process contact Jamie Perrins: j.perrins@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29495": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neurotechnology and Bioelectronic Devices",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £52,675 per annum; Dyson School of Design Engineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04038 About the role: Funded by the UK Advanced Research + Invention Agency (ARIA) through its Massively Scalable Neurotechnology Programme, this Research Associate position will contribute to an ambitious interdisciplinary project developing novel bioelectronic technologies for minimally invasive neural monitoring. The successful candidate will play a leading role in the design, fabrication, integration and characterisation of advanced bioelectronic devices and experimental platforms. The role will involve device development, system integration, electrophysiological and acoustic testing, signal acquisition and analysis, and close collaboration with researchers in bioengineering, ultrasonics, neuroscience, robotics and computational modelling. This is an exciting opportunity to work at the interface of bioelectronics, neuroscience and advanced sensing technologies, contributing to the development of next-generation neurotechnology platforms . What you would be doing: You will contribute to the development, fabrication and evaluation of novel bioelectronic devices and experimental platforms for physiological signal sensing and analysis. Working within a highly interdisciplinary team spanning bioengineering, ultrasonics, neuroscience and advanced manufacturing, your responsibilities will include: Designing, fabricating and characterising novel bioelectronic devices and prototypes. Developing device fabrication, assembly and integration workflows. Establishing electrical, electrochemical and acoustic testing methodologies. Building and maintaining experimental platforms for device evaluation and validation. Acquiring, processing and analysing physiological, electrical and acoustic signals. Evaluating device performance, sensitivity, stability and reliability. Contributing to system integration and prototype optimisation. Working closely with collaborators across multiple work packages to deliver bespoke devices and testing solutions. Supporting laboratory-based validation studies where appropriate. Maintaining accurate and complete experimental records. Contributing to project milestones, technical reports and research deliverables. Presenting research findings at project meetings and scientific conferences. Publishing research in high-quality peer-reviewed journals. Supporting student supervision and the preparation of research funding applications. What we are looking for: We are looking for a motivated and collaborative researcher with experience in bioelectronics, biomedical devices, advanced sensing technologies, or a closely related area. You should have: A PhD, or equivalent research experience, in bioengineering, biomedical engineering, electronic engineering, materials science, physics, neuroscience, or a related discipline. Experience in the fabrication, integration or characterisation of bioelectronic devices, sensors, or related technologies. Experience in electrical, electrochemical, acoustic, or other experimental device characterisation techniques. Experience in experimental data acquisition and quantitative data analysis. Experience with signal processing and data interpretation. The ability to troubleshoot complex experimental systems. The ability to work independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Strong organisational, communication and scientific writing skills. A publication record appropriate to career stage. Experience in one or more of the following areas would be advantageous: Bioelectronic devices, implantable technologies, or advanced sensing systems. Device fabrication, advanced manufacturing, microfabrication, additive manufacturing, 3D printing, or related technologies. Electrophysiological, electrochemical, acoustic or ultrasonic measurement techniques. Signal processing, computational analysis or quantitative modelling of experimental data. In vitro or animal research experience and associated experimental procedures. Experience working in interdisciplinary teams spanning engineering, physical sciences, life sciences or clinical research. What we can offer you: The opportunity to contribute to an ambitious ARIA-funded neurotechnology project at a world-leading institution and support Imperial’s mission to use science for humanity. A collaborative, multidisciplinary working environment spanning design engineering, bioengineering, mechanical engineering, and brain sciences. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. As a member of research staff you have 10 development days to use to develop your skills and explore your career prospects Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: Please note that this is a PhD level role but candidates who have not yet been officially awarded will be appointed as a Research Assistant . This is a full-time post (35 hours per week) on a fixed-term contract for 24 months. An extension to 30 months is anticipated, subject to satisfactory progress and the availability of funding. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr Yunlong Zhao- yunlong.zhao@imperial.ac.uk . Planned Start Date: 19/01/2027"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29488": {
      "title": "Research Support Technician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; Department of Chemical Engineering; Professional / Support Services Reference: ENG04064 About the role: Are you excited by the challenge of furthering your career supporting world-class research in a leading Chemical Engineering department? As a member of our research support team, you will support the effective operation and development of the Department’s research laboratories and facilities. Working under the direction of the Research Support Manager, you will provide technical, operational and safety support to ensure that research activities are conducted efficiently and safely. What you would be doing: You will provide operational support for departmental research laboratories and shared research facilities, working with researchers and PIs to specify, procure, install, and maintain fixed research infrastructure and equipment. You will support the setup and commissioning of new research equipment and spaces, and maintain up-to-date records of laboratory equipment, calibration schedules, and maintenance logs. You may also be responsible for maintenance and operation of shared research facilities. As an RST, you will take an active role in the day-to-day laboratory safety management, including advising researchers on laboratory safety procedures and controls. You will review and approve risk assessments in consultation with the Research Support Manager (RSM), deliver safety inductions and training for researchers and students, and assist the RSM in preparing and updating safety briefing and training material. You will ensure that safety infrastructure within laboratories is maintained, calibrated and tested, and assist with chemical storage. Additionally, you will participate in laboratory safety inspections. You will work collaboratively with colleagues across the Department and University to ensure efficient operation of the laboratories. This will include providing technical advice and guidance to researchers and students as appropriate and contributing to the continuous improvement of laboratory processes and services. What we are looking for: We are seeking an experienced research laboratory technician with a grounding in chemical engineering and a solid health and safety background. You will have: A degree, BTEC/HNC, recognised apprenticeship or equivalent experience in chemical engineering or a related field A health and safety qualification such as IOSH Managing Safely or NEBOSH General Certificate Specific knowledge and experience in one or more of the following key areas: Analytical techniques and spectroscopy Bioscience High-pressure facilities Experience supporting laboratory operations, equipment maintenance, or technical services Experience of contributing to safety management, including preparation of risk assessments Knowledge of laboratory practices, research infrastructure, and relevant safety standards Understanding of health and safety regulations, including relevant risk assessment, fire safety, and manual handling We are looking for someone with the following key skills: Proficiency in using laboratory management systems and standard administrative software (e.g. MS Office, databases, or equivalent tools) Strong organisational and problem-solving skills Good communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work effectively with staff and students Ability to manage multiple tasks and prioritise workload effectively Commitment to maintain high standards of safety, quality, and service What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes) Access to a range of workplace benefits, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing Further information: Our mission is to deliver world-leading research, education, enterprise, leadership and inspiration in chemical engineering, locally, nationally and globally. Our department is consistently ranked within the top 10 in chemical engineering globally and the top 3 in Europe. We have 45 academic staff, 60 teaching, professional and technical staff and about 120 postdoctoral researchers The Department offers a four-year MEng degree programme in chemical engineering, attracting about 150 entrants each year, a one-year MSc course with an intake of about 120 and we have some 200 PhD students. This is a full-time, open-ended post. This post is on site only (5 days on campus). If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Chemeng.staffing@imperial.ac.uk To apply, please click the “Apply now” button at the top of the page. You will find this vacancy by searching either the position title or job number: ENG04064. Candidates will need to complete an online application. Further information about the post is available in the job description. Closing date: 1st November 2026, 23:59:59 (GMT). Interviews: week commencing 30 November 2026."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29479": {
      "title": "Division Manager (x2)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Department of Brain Sciences; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06014 About the role: Are you an experienced professional ready to take on a diverse research and administration role as a Division Manager with the Department of Brain Sciences at Imperial College London? The postholder will provide high-quality, customer-focused support across staffing, research management (pre- and post-award), and local financial management. What you would be doing: Manage a portfolio of research grants and funding applications from proposal development through to award management and closure. Provide proactive financial, contractual, and compliance advice to academics and research teams. Deliver accurate reporting, analysis, and guidance to support effective research project management. Manage divisional finances, providing analysis, insight and assurance on budgets, grants and expenditure. Lead and develop administrative staff, driving high standards of service, performance and continuous improvement. Work across the University and with external partners to ensure research activity is delivered efficiently, compliantly and in line with strategic priorities. What we are looking for: We are looking for an experienced and skilled individual who can bring excellent organisational skills, sound judgement and a collaborative approach. You should have: Significant experience of research finance, grant management or research administration within a complex organisation, with the ability to interpret and apply funding and governance requirements. Strong financial and analytical skills, including experience of managing budgets, monitoring expenditure and using Excel to analyse and present information. Excellent stakeholder management skills, with the confidence to build credibility, influence decisions and provide constructive challenge to academics and colleagues at all levels. The ability to manage multiple priorities and see the bigger picture, understanding how decisions in one area can impact wider operational, financial and compliance requirements. A proactive, solutions-focused approach, with sound judgement, attention to detail and the confidence to work independently whilst operating within agreed policies and procedures. Balances excellent customer service with a firm understanding of policy, compliance and governance requirements. Takes ownership, exercises good judgement and thrives in a busy environment with competing priorities. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time, open-ended post (35 hours per week) based at our Hammersmith Campus. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr Emma Watson – e.watson@imperial.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29472": {
      "title": "Research Assistant or Associate in Mechanics of Advanced Composite Structures",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £45,399- £59,484 per annum; Department of Aeronautics; Researcher Reference: ENG04049 About the role: This post (Research Assistant/Associate in Mechanics of Advanced Composite Structures) will allow you to contribute to a greater project developing the next generation of aero-engines. You will develop strong research, supervision, network, communication and project management skills. You will gain unique hands-on experience with both numerical techniques and with the most advanced lab equipment. You will get the opportunity to travel to International Scientific conferences and to University (Oxford) and industrial (Rolls-Royce) partners. Your work will focus on developing innovative concepts to characterise and simulate translaminar fracture (such as in the case of a blade out event). You will develop experimental and simulation methods for actual deployment at Rolls-Royce, namely translaminar fracture toughness characterisation methods and FE / AI-assisted surrogate models for translaminar fracture of composite structures. What you would be doing: You will design and develop innovative translaminar fracture characterisation and simulation capabilities for CFRP with advanced and complex microstructures (such as braided composites), suitable for aircraft engine fan blades. This will entail design, testing, analysis and simulation. The simulation will be done in collaboration with the University of Oxford; our focus will be on translaminar fracture propagation both directly with physically-based models and via incorporating Artificial Intelligence surrogates into the numerical Finite Element framework. What we are looking for: Research Associate: Hold a PhD in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience Research Associate and Assistant: A recognised first class masters degree (or equivalent) in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. Experience in failure modelling of fibre-reinforced composites using FEM An interest or experience with AI Practical experience in a composites lab An interest in working in a larger group, collaborating with other universities and industry. What we can offer you: The opportunity to build a strong network with contacts at other universities (University of Oxford, University of Bristol) and at Rolls-Royce The opportunity to develop test methods and computer codes that should be deployed and used in practice by Rolls-Royce Ample scope for travelling to international conferences to disseminate the work done. Ample scope for publishing in leading journals. The opportunity to gain hands-on experience with unique equipment, such as an AFP machine. The opportunity to gain supervision and project management experience. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: This post is available until 31 st Oct 2029. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Silvestre Pinho – silvestre.pinho@imperial.ac.uk Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you encounter any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29475": {
      "title": "Research Assistant or Associate in Mechanics of Advanced Composite Structures",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £45,399- £59,484 per annum; Department of Aeronautics; Researcher Reference: ENG04050 About the role: You will to contribute to a large industry focussed project developing the next generations of aero-engines. You will develop strong research, supervision, network, communication and project management skills. You will gain unique hands-on experience of failure analysis polymer composites with the world experts in fractography and failure modelling of polymer composites, and use our advanced characterisation facilities. You will get the opportunity to travel to International Scientific conferences and to Universities (Oxford and Bristol) and industrial (Rolls-Royce) partners. Your work will focus on developing and using fractographic techniques for characterisation of coupons, elements and components made using advanced technologies, such as 3D polymer composites. Your research outcomes will underpin predictive model development, our understanding of how damage and failure progresses in these materials, and motivate improvements in the design of future aerostructures. What you would be doing: Visual inspection, optical and scanning electron microscopy of composite coupons, elements and components using fractographic techniques. This is to deduce damage and failure processes in coupons and glean the source and sequence of failures in elements and components. This will entail development of new fractographic techniques to characterise failures in advanced and complex microstructures, such as 3D composites. An important aspect will be efficient communication of your fractographic findings to academic and industry partners to inform future design of aerostructures and the development of their Failure Investigation (FI) capability to support the introduction of the next generation of aeroengine components. What we are looking for: Research Associate: Hold a PhD in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience Research Associate and Assistant: A recognised first class masters degree (or equivalent) in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. Experience in characterisation methods such as non-destructive evaluation and optical and scanning electron microscopy. Practical experience, such as mechanical testing in composites, and a good knowledge of polymer composite failure mechanisms. An interest in working in a larger group, collaborating and working closely with other universities and industry. What we can offer you: The opportunity to build a strong network with contacts at other universities (Oxford) and at Rolls-Royce The opportunity to develop and apply fractographic techniques that could be deployed and used in practice by Rolls-Royce by their FI team. Ample scope for travelling to international conferences to disseminate the work done. Ample scope for publishing in leading journals. The opportunity to gain hands-on experience with state of the art optical and scanning electron microscopes. The opportunity to gain supervision and project management experience. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: If you require any further details on the role please contact: For the first position / task (translaminar fracture characterisation and simulation) - Silvestre Pinho – pinho@imperial.ac.uk For the second position / task (Fractography) – Emile Greenhalgh greenhlagh@imperial.ac.uk Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you encounter any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29476": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £52,675 per annum; Department of Bioengineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04051 About the role: The Billerbeck and Ledesma Amaro Labs in the Department of Bioengineering at Imperial College London – the UK’s leading Bioengineering department – have an open position for a joint Postdoctoral Research Associate in Fungal Pathogen Genetics/Engineering Biology/Single-Cell Analysis. The position is part of a Wellcome Trust-funded multi-PI initiative to develop an artificial intelligence-driven antifungal drug discovery platform for the high-priority pathogen Candida auris. This PDRA will lead efforts for essential gene prediction and verification via large CRISPR screens, and study population heterogeneity via single-cell sequencing, including tool and assay development for Candida auris . What you would be doing: You will conduct world-class research by helping to build this antifungal drug discovery platform. The role is interdisciplinary and involves bioinformatics, yeast genetics, CRISPR tool development, competitive growth assays in small-scale bioreactors, Next-generation sequencing (NGS) and single-cell assay development and analysis. You will be responsible for conducting the research associated with one of the work packages in the grant and taking the initiative in the planning and execution of the proposed research. You will identify and develop suitable designs, methodologies and approaches for the collection and analysis of data, ensuring the validity and reliability of data at all times . Additionally, you will work collaboratively with the multi-PI consortium , which will include presenting and discuss ing your findings , exchang ing ideas , learn ing, and implement ing new techniques and approaches where necessary. You will be encouraged to present your research at international conferences and expected to submit publications to refereed journals as well as project reports to the research sponsor. You will also assist in the supervision of both undergraduate and postgraduate research students, as well as contribute to the smooth running of the group’s laborator y . What we are looking for: You will have: A PhD in (bio) engineering, yeast genetics, engineering biology/synthetic biology, natural sciences, biological sciences or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience. Practical experience within a research environment, publication(s) in relevant (refereed) journals and experience of dealing with e.g., collaborators, sponsors Practical experience in a broad range of techniques including molecular biology (e.g. cloning, genome engineering), bioinformatics/analysis of large data sets, NGS, microbiological culturing, assay and/or tool development. The ability to develop and apply new concepts, a creative approach to problem-solving Excellent verbal and written communication skills The ability to organise own work with minimal supervision, be proactive and meet deadlines What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time (35 hours), fixed term post for 48 months and is expected to start January 2027. For further information on the post, please contact Dr. Sonja Billerbeck ( s.billerbeck@imperial.ac.uk ) or Prof. Rodrigo Ledesma-Amaro ( r.ledesma-amaro@imperial.ac.uk ) To find out more about the Billerbeck and the Ledesma-Amaro Labs and our research , please visit us at www.billerbecklab.com and https://rlalab.org/ For more information about Bioengineering at Imperial College London please visit: https://www.imperial.ac.uk/bioengineering/ ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29473": {
      "title": "Research Associate: Measuring the electron's electric dipole moment with ultracold molecules",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Physics; Researcher Reference: NAT02303 About the role: Do you want to challenge the Standard Model by making ultra-precise measurements? Applications are invited for a Research Associate within the Centre for Cold Matter to measure the electron’s electric dipole moment using ultracold molecules. What you would be doing: You will work as part of a small team aiming to measure the electron's electric dipole moment using ultracold YbF molecules. You will develop new experimental methods and models, acquire and analyze data, and present your work through publications and talks. What we are looking for: A strong background in Experimental Atomic, Molecular and Optical Physics. Technical knowledge of precise laser systems and the control of laser light. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Experience working with cold atoms or molecules. Experience in the simulation of experiments. A strong background in data analysis methods. A strong background in the construction of new apparatus Experience with data acquisition hardware and software What we can offer you: An opportunity to make a world-leading measurement that tests fundamental physics An intellectually stimulating, collaborative and rewarding research environment The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: More information about the Department of Physics , the Centre for Cold Matter and the staff benefits that we offer, can be found on our webpages. This is a full-time post. This role is for a fixed-term contract for 24 months. Closing date: 31 October 2026 Queries relating to the application process should be directed to: Ms Sanja Maricic, CCM Operations Manager, email: s.maricic@imperial.ac.uk . For more information about the research project email Prof. Michael Tarbutt, Professor of Experimental Physics, email: m.tarbutt@imperial.ac.uk . The Department of Physics is proud to hold an Athena SWAN Silver Award, recognising our commitment to fostering a supportive, inclusive, and thriving community. We value the strength that comes from diverse perspectives, and we warmly welcome applications from talented people of all backgrounds to join our vibrant community supported by initiatives that promote collaboration, inclusivity, and professional development. Find out more about our inclusive environment ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29460": {
      "title": "Early Years Educator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £35,987 - £37,934 per annum; Early Years Education Centre; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01316 About the role: ***Please note that if you have applied for this role within the last six months and were unsuccessful, please do not reapply at this stage unless you have since gained additional relevant qualifications, skills, or experience that meet the requirements of the role.*** For over 50 years, we have been providing high quality care and education to children of Imperial’s staff and students aged from 6 months to 5 years old. We are based at South Kensington campus and are a three-times awarded outstanding Early Years provider by Ofsted. Our well-resourced rooms as well as bespoke designed garden were developed to provide an excellent stimulating environment for children to thrive and learn in. This allows them to reach their full potential alongside our passionate and dedicated team. Our curriculum incorporates the Froebelian approach to learning, where we understand the importance of how a child’s connectiveness with nature, personal experiences and their family and community makes them all unique. What you would be doing: We are currently looking for two Early Years Educators who are enthusiastic, hardworking, and Level 3 Qualified to join our well-motivated team. The roles are full time, permanent positions with an immediate start. You will have excellent childcare and developmental knowledge, confident written and spoken communication skills and can plan and implement exciting learning experiences for children of all ages between 6 months - 5 years. Candidates must have Full and relevant qualifications – please check your qualifications here Early years qualifications achieved in the United Kingdom - GOV.UK (www.gov.uk) before applying. You will be required to have a sound knowledge of the Children’s Act, Every Child Matters document, the Early Years Foundation Stage and latest legislation. In addition to have a sound knowledge of child development 0-7 years. You will need to have the ability to promote and maintain high standards of care and education and be understanding of children’s emotional needs and be able to respond appropriately. To be able to recognise good practice and have the confidence to challenge bad practice (See Whistle Blowing Policy). Safeguarding children is our priority, and all post holders are required to adhere to the EYEC’s Safeguarding children and Whistle blowing policy. All post holders will be required to obtain a satisfactory enhanced level Disclosure and Barring check. What we are looking for: Full and relevant childcare qualification e.g. NNEB, BTEC a minimum Level 3 To have a sound knowledge of child development 0-7 years To have an understanding of Safeguarding children and own responsibilities to protect children To build positive caring relationships with all children attending the Centre ensuring that their needs take priority To work as a key person to a group of designated children To keep accurate, clear, up to date developmental records and observations on children To have a working knowledge and understanding of the Early Years foundation Stage framework To plan and organise daily learning experiences with the team to promote the development of each individual child taking into consideration their varying interests and needs To develop and maintain professional relationships with parents and carers by warmly welcoming them to the Centre daily and communicating with them effectively about matters concerning their child To arrange to meet with parents regularly to discuss children’s progress and any concerns What we can offer you: Working hours are Monday to Friday from 08:30-17:30 Events to come together and celebrate from summer parties to Christmas breakfasts The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes) Access to a range of workplace benefits, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing Further information: Please note that if you have applied for this role within the last six months and were unsuccessful, please do not reapply at this stage unless you have since gained additional relevant qualifications, skills, or experience that meet the requirements of the role. All appointments will begin at the bottom of the salary range (plus 2.5 hours of guaranteed contractual weekly overtime). If you require any further details on the role, please contact: Tracy Halsey, Head of EYEC – t.halsey@imperial.ac.uk Closing Date: Midnight on Sunday, 01 November 2026"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29470": {
      "title": "Faculty Technical Operations Coordinator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Faculty of Medicine Centre; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06009 About the role: The Faculty of Medicine has 8 Departments, spread over 7 Campuses. We are seeking to appoint a Faculty Technical Operations Coordinator within the Faculty of Medicine Operations Team, to deliver effective and joined up asset and facility management processes. You will be expected to cover the FoM estate, supporting efficient management of the equipment and shared facilities that are jointly occupied by various Departments. What you would be doing: You will be expected to support each constituent Department in the management of their physical equipment, including computing and telecoms infrastructure, and physical environment operational issues using your expertise and experience. You will require a high level of organisation with the ability to manage varying activities within your workload and have a good understanding of using databases and laboratory management systems. You will be working closely with key Faculty Centre teams and Senior Laboratory Managers within Departments to deliver a clear asset management system, ensuring that relevant asset data is maintained, and facilities are monitored and supported across the faculty. You will report to the Faculty Campus Operations Manager and will be responsible for line management of the FoM Equipment and Service Technician. You will be accountable for ensuring your direct reports are managed in line with Imperial’s Values, supporting them to deliver their key functions and facilitating both personal and professional development. What we are looking for: You will be able to work autonomously, be proactive in organising your workload and establishing strong working relationships across the Faculty of Medicine, Departments, facilities management and ICT. Having good communication and organisation skills will be key to the role. You will be expected to have experience in line with the key responsibilities of the role and person specification, knowledge of higher education environments, laboratory and ICT/AV equipment, databases and laboratory management systems alongside the softer skills and desirable skills required for line management. What we can offer you: The opportunity to progress your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time, open-ended post (35 hours per week). If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Sharron Stubbs – s.stubbs@imperial.ac.uk Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29468": {
      "title": "MSci Curriculum Development & Innovation Lead",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £76,313 - £82,954 per annum; Faculty of Medicine Centre; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06006 About the role: Do you have the vision and expertise to design an innovative undergraduate curriculum that connects medical science with real-world impact? Imperial College London's Faculty of Medicine is seeking a Curriculum Development and Innovation Lead to help shape and deliver our new Medical Sciences and Innovation (MSci) degree. This is a rare opportunity to play a leading role in developing a flagship undergraduate programme, creating an integrated and inclusive curriculum that prepares students to address complex scientific and healthcare challenges through innovation, collaboration and critical thinking. Working closely with academic colleagues, students and external partners, you will provide leadership for curriculum design, implementation and enhancement, while also leading the programme's distinctive Innovation Studies strand and challenge-based learning activities. What you would be doing: You will: Lead the development, implementation and continuous enhancement of key elements of the MSci curriculum, ensuring a coherent and engaging student learning experience. Shape innovative approaches to teaching, learning and assessment that reflect best practice in higher education and support student success. Lead the design and delivery of the programme's Innovation Studies theme, including interdisciplinary and challenge-based learning opportunities. Build and sustain partnerships with organisations across healthcare, industry, policy, entrepreneurship and the wider community, creating authentic learning opportunities for students. Contribute to teaching, supervision, assessment, educational scholarship and programme evaluation. Use evidence, feedback and evaluation to drive educational enhancement and ensure an outstanding student experience. What we are looking for: We are looking for an experienced and collaborative educational leader who brings: Significant experience of curriculum design, development and innovation in higher education or a comparable setting. Experience leading educational change and delivering successful programme enhancement initiatives. Strong understanding of inclusive, student-centred and interdisciplinary approaches to learning. Experience building effective partnerships and working with a range of internal and external stakeholders. Excellent communication, leadership and influencing skills, with the ability to bring people together around shared goals. A doctoral qualification, or equivalent professional standing, in a relevant discipline. We recognise that talent is developed through a variety of professional and academic experiences and welcome applications from candidates whose career paths may not follow a traditional route. What we can offer you: The opportunity to shape a new and distinctive undergraduate programme at one of the world's leading universities. A key leadership role in delivering innovative, research-informed education that has the potential to influence practice across the sector. Access to Imperial's Educational Development Unit and a wide range of professional development opportunities. A diverse and collaborative educational community committed to excellence, innovation and continuous improvement. A competitive salary and benefits package, including generous annual leave and pension provision. An inclusive workplace supported by staff networks, wellbeing resources and opportunities to thrive professionally and personally. Further information: This is a full-time position based across Imperial's South Kensington and White City campuses. The Faculty of Medicine is committed to delivering world-class education that equips students with the knowledge, skills and adaptability needed to address the challenges facing healthcare and society. The Medical Sciences and Innovation MSci is a key part of this ambition, combining scientific excellence with innovation, entrepreneurship and real-world problem solving. For an informal discussion about the role, please contact Professor Alison McGregor, FOM Director of Undergraduate Sciences ( a.mcgregor@imperial.ac.uk )."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29455": {
      "title": "Project Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; School of Public Health; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED05896 About the role: Applications are invited for a Project Manager post in the Department of Infectious Disease Epidemiology, based at the White City Campus of Imperial College London. The department undertakes leading interdisciplinary research on infectious diseases, using approaches ranging from molecular biology, environmental surveillance to mathematical studies of transmission dynamics and disease control. A wide range of pathogens are studied in the department, including vector-borne diseases, enteroviruses, several major bacterial pathogens and a variety of parasitic infections among others. The Project Manager will be based within the Vaccine Epidemiology Research Group, which is headed by Professor Nicholas Grassly. The department offers excellent research facilities and a friendly, intellectually stimulating working environment. What you would be doing: The Project Manager will be responsible for supporting a Gates Foundation project focusing on direct detection of poliovirus in its implementation and successful delivery. This includes general project administration, project, scientific and communication management and support, liaising internally and externally about contracting, finance tracking and forecasting, travel arrangement, reporting to the funding body and horizon scanning to identify new funding opportunities. The Project Manager will also support projects related to the interruption of poliovirus transmission. The Project Manager will report to the Senior Project Manager for the Vaccine Epidemiology Research Group. What we are looking for: The Project Manager will be responsible for: Tracking progress on project working groups, organising travel and all meetings including steering committee and annual meetings; Remaining in constant communication with the project PI and Senior Project Manager; Taking minutes and summarising action points, ensuring that these are followed up in a timely manner; Facilitating contract set up with project partners; Assistance in the preparation of ethical approval protocols, as needed; Support to the senior project manager in financial management, including financial forecasting to monitor spending; Horizon scanning to identify new stakeholders and funding opportunities. Candidates should have a Master’s degree in an area related to infectious diseases or global health, or extensive career experience of working in administrative posts supporting global health projects. Experience in the management and coordination of international collaborative research projects and/or administrative activities and financial reporting is desirable. Applicants should be able to work independently and as part of an international research team. Good communication skills are essential. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time and fixed-term contract until 31 December 2027 (part-time/flexible working will be considered, and details can be discussed at interview). If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Tabassum Iqbal at t.iqbal@imperial.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29466": {
      "title": "Sleep  Research Nurse",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £32,073 - £39,043 per annum; National Heart & Lung Institute; NHS Nurses Reference: MED05998 About the role: You will be collecting, analysing, and presenting data throughout the study informing the intervention and app and contributing directly to the education/instructional videos contained within them. Therefore, you will be contributing to the development and refinement of a potential medical device that could become adopted into clinical practice, not just in the UK but globally. What you would be doing: You will run the project day-to-day, supervised by the project lead and other senior team members, and with some help from another RA. This involves liaising with patients to arrange their (online) clinical trials visits, collecting, verifying and recording, and analysing, data, for example. You will also be responsible for the care of the patients in the trials regarding their CPAP therapy, for anything that the app cannot provide. You will also contribute to the intervention and app as discussed above. What we are looking for: We are looking for a highly organised, driven, and dynamic individual who is a team worker, has a positive outlook, and is adaptable and flexible in their working methods. It is also essential that you are highly experienced in setting up CPAP for patients with OSA and dealing with the technical difficulties that can arise with CPAP that can create barriers for adherence, and in the solutions that can be offered. What we can offer you: This is a highly innovative project that breaks barriers; we are creating something new which is always exciting. The team consists of several experts who can teach you many translatable skills and knowledge from the fields of sleep medicine, sleep physiology, statistics, artificial intelligence, and psychology for example. A very significant and specific benefit we can offer is the option for to complete an MPhil in the 30-months if you wish, while being paid a clinical-equivalent salary rather than a PhD stipend. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing Further information: This is a full time (37 h/w) and fixed-term role for 30 months . If you require any further details about the role, please contact: [Dr Amanda Sathyapalal] – [a.sathyapala@imperial.ac.uk]."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29435": {
      "title": "GPPHC Placement Lead (Maternity Cover)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £76,313 - £82,954 per annum; School of Public Health; Academic & Teaching Reference: MED05981 About the role: Imperial College London is seeking applications for a GPPHC Placement Lead (maternity cover). The successful candidate will work with Undergraduate Primary Care Education Unit faculty, General Practitioner tutors and other medical school faculty to organise the day-to-day running of the fifth year GP placement and plan its future development. What you would be doing: The post holder will lead the GP placement in year five of the six-year Imperial MBBS programme. The core duties of the post consist of placement planning and management, teaching, assessment, quality improvement, GP tutor training and student support for the year 5 GPPHC component of the MBBS course. What we are looking for: Full registration with the General Medical Council. Eligibility for appointment as an NHS GP. Higher degree of other postgraduate qualification in medical education. Substantial experience of undergraduate clinical teaching in primary care setting. Experience in managing a teaching programme. Evidence of critical reasoning, including through presentation or publication of evaluation and/or research relating to medical education. Strong understanding of the undergraduate medical education curriculum. Sound understanding of the organisation of general practice and current developments in primary care. Strong skills, knowledge and achievement in clinical general practice. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity by sharing your passion and inspiring the next generation. Gain access to Imperial's top-tier Educational Development Unit , which offers a wide range of tailored educational development services to meet your needs. Support to apply for a post graduate qualification in Medical Education. Join a diverse and innovative learning and teaching community at an institution that values research-led educational provision. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: The post holders will be based in the multi-award-winning Undergraduate Primary Care Education Unit, one of the largest of its kind in the UK, fellows will contribute to MBBS teaching and assessment, gain experience in curriculum design, evaluation, and educational research, and participate in the Unit’s educational offerings. This is a part-time post (14 hours per week). This role is for a fixed-term contract for 12 months with opportunities for potential extension If you require any further details about the role, please contact Dr Arti Maini– a.maini@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29426": {
      "title": "Policy Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; Department of Bioengineering; Professional / Support Services Reference: ENG04047 About the role: Are you passionate about sustainability, and the future of food? Do you want to work at the intersection of policy and cutting-edge research? Join the Bezos Centre for Sustainable Protein and Microbial Food Hub at Imperial College London; the regional flagship hub leading the UK’s sustainable protein revolution. Based within the Department of Bioengineering, the Centre connects science, industry, and policy to accelerate the transition toward nutritious, affordable, and sustainable protein solutions. You will play a key role in delivering the Centre’s high-profile policy focused research projects, workshops, events and collaborative grants that bring together policy makers, regulators, global leaders, innovators, and researchers. What you would be doing: In this dynamic role, you will: Deliver the Centre’s key policy reports; from leading UK ecosystem research and horizon scanning in collaboration with GFI, to working with regulatory groups to best inform innovators. Coordinate events such as workshops, conferences, webinars and launches. Work closely with the Regulation and Scale-Up Manager and Centre leadership to support the planning and delivery of impact-focused projects. Build and maintain networks across Imperial, partner universities, and the wider ecosystem of start-ups industry, and policy stakeholders. Work in partnership with organisations such as the Good Food Institute, the Alternative Proteins Association and NFX UK to deliver impactful policy recommendations. Work to inform policy makers across predominately the UK and at times EU stakeholders on the science and opportunities surrounding alternative proteins. Help capture and report on the Centre’s outputs, contributing to its growing visibility as the regional leader driving the sustainable protein transition. Representing the Centre at stakeholder meetings and external events. What we are looking for: We’re seeking a proactive, organised and enthusiastic individual who can thrive in a fast-paced, cross-disciplinary environment. You will have: Proven experience writing reports, coordinating events and managing multiple projects simultaneously. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work with a wide range of internal and external stakeholders. Strong attention to detail and confidence working to deadlines. A friendly, professional, and solutions-focused approach. Familiarity with alternative proteins, engineering biology, or sustainable food systems. Desirable: A degree in a relevant discipline. An understanding of the academic, research, or innovation landscape; ideally within food systems, sustainability, or biotechnology. Experience of working on policy in the EU or UK, or at a national or multilateral level, for example in a government, public institution, political party, NGO, think tank, industry association, public affairs agency, consulting firm, lobbying organisation or other relevant institution. A demonstrated ability to work effectively with scientists, research organisations, and think tanks. Experience working on UK or EU policy related to research & innovation, food, biotechnology, or agriculture. Experience in science communication. A strong network of relevant contacts. What we can offer you: The opportunity to join a world-class university driving global impact through science and innovation. A front-row seat in one of the UK’s most ambitious initiatives transforming how we produce and consume food. Exposure to major partners across academia, industry and government. A collaborative, inclusive and forward-thinking team environment. Competitive pay, 39 days annual leave (including public holidays) and a generous pension scheme. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a fixed-term position for 6 months, with potential for extension depending on funding. If you’d like to learn more about the role, please contact: John Murphy, Regulation and Scale-Up Manager, Bezos Centre for Sustainable Protein bezos.centre@imperial.ac.uk Join us at the forefront of the sustainable protein revolution; where science, innovation and impact meet to shape the future of food."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29444": {
      "title": "Research Inclusion Advisor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; School of Public Health; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED05987 About the role: Are you passionate about ensuring health and social care research is designed to be as inclusive as possible and want to further your career as a Research Inclusion Advisor? As part of a collaborative, multidisciplinary team, you will have a unique opportunity to contribute to strategy across the RSS Imperial and Partners Hub. The role combines contributing to the strategic direction of our Hub with direct advisory support to innovators, clinicians, health and social care professionals, and academics on incorporating meaningful research inclusion in research design, funding applications, and project implementation. What you would be doing: As the Research Inclusion Advisor, you will be responsible for contributing to the implementation and refinement of the RSS Hub's Research Inclusion Strategy, which reflects NIHR's Research Inclusion Strategy. You will support the implementation and reporting of the national RSS Research Inclusion Strategy and contribute to the development of Hub and national RSS Annual Reports. You will be an active participator in the national RSS Research Inclusion Group meetings and collaborate across the RSS Network to achieve shared strategic objectives. What we are looking for: You will have a strong track record in delivering Research Inclusion within the context of applied health or social care research, including supporting researchers in embedding inclusive approaches. You will understand the NIHR funding landscape and inclusion requirements across different funding streams. Other responsibilities include: Providing advice to clients about how to meet NIHR (and/or other funder) research inclusion requirements, and promoting inclusive research practices. Developing, identifying and sharing up-to-date guidance around inclusive research and methods. Highlighting development needs for the Hub around Inclusion and inclusive practice. Providing annual reports of Inclusion activities. Ensuring that Inclusion advice is reflected in the Hub’s databases and reporting metrics. What we can offer you: The opportunity to join a collaborative, high-performing partnership that unites the expertise of four leading London Universities with an outstanding record in designing and delivering research. A supportive environment to develop in your role and contribute to our joint mission to make research more inclusive and meet the needs of the whole population. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a part-time role (0.4 FTE/14 hours per week) on a fixed-term contract until September 2028 in the first instance. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Gareth O’Brien – g.obrien@imperial.ac.uk."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29429": {
      "title": "Senior Network Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Department of Chemistry; Professional / Support Services Reference: NAT02287 About the role: We’re looking for a Senior Network Manager to provide professional research network, project and community management support to the SCALE-UP NetworkPlus project and its leadership team. You’ll manage funding calls for the project’s flexible fund, oversee a portfolio of funded projects, develop and maintain links to other UKRI Digital Research Infrastructure (DRI) projects, and help to grow the community for digital Research Technical Professionals (dRTPs) nationally. What you would be doing: The central aim of the SCALE-UP network is to develop an inclusive, enthusiastic and skilled community for digital Research Technical Professionals through managing a flexible fund (£1M) for a set of targeted regional and national initiatives: supporting cohorts of Research Technical Champions and Research Technical Experience placements, organising events, and developing a programme to make research computing more visible through small low-powered computing clusters and related training materials. You will help to develop these schemes and will support their operation and management alongside the project leadership team. The project will build on work being undertaken in the regional STEP-UP ( https://step-up.ac.uk ) project funded under an earlier DRI call. You will be responsible for the day-to-day operations of the network, including managing funding calls and internal budgets, monitoring progress of funded activities and project evaluation, planning and running meetings and conferences and providing overall project management support. You will also build a community around our Champions and Experience programme participants, supporting the cohorts in these programmes and developing an enthusiastic and energetic group of ambassadors to promote the approaches developed through this network to others. You will maintain communications between project collaborators, external project partners, and other Digital Research Infrastructure initiatives, attending national meetings to support and represent the network both as part of the project team and independently. What we are looking for: The ideal candidate will have experience of managing calls for grant funding from announcement to award, with experience of both project management and community management. You should be a good communicator, and enthusiastic about research, computing, research data and / or software. This is a lot to ask: but the right candidate could match this description in different ways. For example, for someone who already has project and funding management experience (essential) in an academic or related setting (preferably), we’d be looking for enthusiasm for engaging with our community of digital Research Technical Professionals and the researchers they work alongside and to develop their community management skills. Essential requirements: Educated to degree level or possess equivalent experience. (A Masters or PhD in a technical or scientific discipline or other experience of working within a research community is desirable). Proven project management experience, preferably in a Higher Education setting, developing operational plans and coordinating their execution. Financial and budgetary management experience including record-keeping, forecasting and monitoring. Experience of organising events within the Higher Education sector or industry. Enthusiasm and strong communication skills – ability to engage and inspire community members. Self-motivated – ability to identify and explore community, technical and strategic opportunities within project guidelines. Able to work constructively and effectively as part of a distributed project team. Responsible and reliable – able to deliver high-quality results on time and within budget. Willingness to undertake occasional travel to project meetings, workshops or conferences within the UK and abroad. Please see the job description for a full list of essential and desirable requirements. What we can offer you: Be part of a wider network and community of projects helping to shape UK Digital Research Infrastructure and associated career support for the people who make this work. Work alongside other Imperial-led projects managing flexible funding schemes such as STEP-UP and AIChemy and with their community and project managers. Progress your career at a world-leading institution with inspiring colleagues and be part of our mission to develop science for humanity. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks , career development opportunities and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This position is available to start immediately, and is fixed term until 31 March 2029. SCALE-UP is a UKRI NetworkPlus for digital Research Technical Professionals, part of UKRI’s Digital Research Infrastructure programme. The network is led by Imperial College London with partner Universities of Sheffield, Northumbria, Glasgow and Queen’s University Belfast. At Imperial you’ll be based in the Department of Chemistry, working also with Research Computing Services teams within Information and Communication Technologies (ICT). This position will also be considered as a secondment opportunity for existing staff at Imperial College London. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Prof. Michael Bearpark: m.bearpark@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29419": {
      "title": "Communications Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Strategic Programmes & Change; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01434 About the role: Are you an experienced communications professional who can turn complex programmes into clear, compelling communications? Do you enjoy shaping communication strategies, creating high quality content and advising colleagues on how best to communicate with a wide range of audiences? If so, we would love to hear from you. What you would be doing: We are seeking a Communications Manager to join Imperial’s Transformation Portfolio Team, supporting the Great Environment Transformation Portfolio. This strategic portfolio brings together activity across South Kensington and White City campuses, enabling Imperial to grow its academic endeavour through world-leading campus infrastructure.​ As a Communications Manager, you will play a key role in delivering communications across the portfolio. Working closely with programme and project teams, academic and professional services colleagues, you will develop and deliver communication plans for major transformation projects. You will act as a trusted communications adviser to programme leaders and project teams, helping to shape messaging. From communications planning to presentations, events and digital content, you will use a range of channels and approaches to build understanding and support successful project delivery. We are looking for someone with strong communications experience gained within a complex organisation, excellent writing and editing skills, and the ability to make complicated subjects accessible and engaging. You will be confident building relationships with people at all levels, advising and influencing colleagues, managing competing priorities and working collaboratively across multidisciplinary teams. Excellent organisational skills, sound judgement and a creative approach to communications is essential, alongside experience of supporting transformation or organisational change. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a major strategic portfolio and help shape the future environment of a world-leading university. What we are looking for: The post holder will be part of the Communications team, which sits within the Transformation Team at Imperial. The Communications Manager is accountable for the development and delivery of communications strategies to increase engagement and raise awareness of the Transformation portfolio. You will also lead on the delivery of communications workstreams to foster widespread understanding of the visions and aims of major strategic programmes and projects, including stakeholder engagement plans, user stories, and internal communications campaigns. In addition, the Communications Manager will be responsible for providing dedicated communications support to the Transformation team – including the review and development of its communications strategy – as the function navigates an exciting period of growth and development. What we can offer you: • The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. • Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). • Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. • Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. • Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Zoe Cheung – z.cheung@imperial.ac.uk. Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk. We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29425": {
      "title": "Product Analyst - Pre-enrolment Software Engineer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Information & Communication Technologies; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01443 About the role: Imperial College London are seeking a skilled Dynamics 365 and Power Platform Engineer to drive the delivery lifecycle of cutting-edge business applications. Your Role: As part of a collaborative Agile squad, you will take end-to-end technical ownership of our external PowerApps Portals alongside our core business systems. In this role, you will act as the team’s specialist for Power Pages while contributing broadly across model-driven apps, canvas apps, Power Automate, and Dataverse. You will also serve as lead for critical UCAS and Admissions systems, working directly with vendors, databases, and end users. The position suits an engineer who looks beyond individual tickets to diagnose root causes, improve architectures, and help shape the long-term direction of our platform. What you would be doing: Key Responsibilities: Lead the design, development, and maintenance of external Power Pages portals using Liquid, FetchXML, JavaScript, HTML, CSS, and Web APIs. Configure, extend, and support solutions across the broader Microsoft stack, including model-driven apps, canvas apps, Power Automate, and Dataverse. Manage source control, review pull requests, and execute deployment pipelines across non-production and Production environments using Azure DevOps and GitHub. Act as the technical lead for UCAS data exchanges, managing regular send and receive processes, upgrades, and operational reporting. Manage integrations between UCAS and core student record systems (Banner via Data Connect) and Dataverse, writing and optimising SQL queries to support data flows. Liaise directly with external vendors, including UCAS and Ellucian, to resolve technical issues and co-ordinate system enhancements. Identify, diagnose, and resolve complex defects and architectural issues to prevent data discrepancies and safeguard user journeys. Represent portal and integration delivery in sprint reviews and technical forums alongside Product Owners, Tech Leads, and senior stakeholders. Progress DevOps maturity by contributing to the creation, maintenance, and automation of CI/CD pipelines. What we are looking for: What We’re Looking For: Education: A degree in computer technology or a related discipline, or equivalent practical industry experience. Experience: Proven background in Power Platform development, with a clear specialism in Power Pages and external portal delivery. Technical Knowledge: Strong practical skills in Liquid, FetchXML, JavaScript, HTML, CSS, Dataverse, and managing SQL data flows or systems integrations (ideally including UCAS and Banner). DevOps & Delivery: Hands-on experience with Azure DevOps, managing source control, pull requests, and multi-tier environment deployment pipelines. Skills: Excellent communication and analytical abilities to diagnose complex technical issues, engage directly with vendors and senior stakeholders, and take genuine ownership of solutions. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time post (35 hours per week)."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29423": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Heat and Health Modelling",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; The Grantham Institute for Climate Change; Researcher Reference: NAT02302 About the role: Are you a statistically skilled researcher interested in the health impacts of extreme heat and climate change? Join EUROASIS, a major European consortium funded through an EU Horizon grant, and contribute to interdisciplinary research spanning climate science, epidemiology, statistics and public health. Based at the Grantham Institute for Climate Change and the Environment and the School of Public Health, you will help improve understanding of temperature-related harms and evaluate innovative solutions for heat resilience. What you would be doing: You will develop a coherent research programme on the role of climate change in health impacts during heatwaves. Your work will include assembling, harmonising and analysing climate and health datasets; estimating heat-related mortality and morbidity burdens across European and international settings; and developing advanced statistical models where open or granular health data are limited. You will create a methodological pipeline for heat-related health impact assessment across European cities and develop evidence-synthesis models that integrate different data sources to quantify the impact of heat on health. You will also actively participate in, and lead where appropriate, rapid attribution studies, including analyses during heatwaves. The role involves using and developing methods such as case-crossover designs, distributed lag non-linear models, Bayesian hierarchical models, uncertainty quantification and potentially machine-learning approaches, with a strong emphasis on reproducible science. You will publish in high-quality journals, present findings at national and international meetings, and communicate results and uncertainty to scientific, policy and public audiences. What we are looking for: We are looking for a motivated and collaborative researcher with a strong statistical background and expertise in epidemiological and environmental health data. In particular, we are looking for candidates who demonstrate: A PhD in statistics, epidemiology, environmental health or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience. Practical experience within a research environment and/or a record of publication in relevant, refereed journals. Experience with epidemiological data designs, such as cohort and case-crossover studies. Experience with Bayesian hierarchical models and associated software. Knowledge of the interface between climate science and health impacts, and ideally knowledge of climate attribution research. Strong statistical skills, including regression models, distributed lag non-linear models, hierarchical models and/or machine learning, together with proficiency in R. Excellent data-wrangling skills for environmental and climate datasets, including downloading, manipulating and linking data with health datasets. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to present findings clearly to specialist and non-specialist audiences and to write for publication. The ability to review recent literature, prioritise your work in response to deadlines, and work flexibly and cooperatively as part of a team. Willingness to travel within the United Kingdom and internationally to conduct research and attend conferences. This role is well suited to candidates who are motivated to develop rigorous, reproducible research that informs understanding of heat-related health risks and supports effective climate adaptation and resilience. We encourage applications from candidates from diverse backgrounds and are committed to creating an inclusive working environment for all. What we can offer you: The opportunity to work at the forefront of interdisciplinary research connecting climate science, epidemiology, statistics and public health. The chance to contribute to research on heat-health hotspots, heat-health impact models and the attribution of health burdens to anthropogenic climate change. Collaboration with investigators and partners across Imperial, external climate attribution groups and the wider EUROASIS consortium. A stimulating and supportive research environment at the Grantham Institute and the School of Public Health. Opportunities to publish in high-quality journals, present at national and international meetings, and contribute to consortium deliverables. The opportunity to provide guidance to staff and students and assist in the supervision of postgraduate research students and research assistants. Support for career development and opportunities to build your academic and professional profile. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package, including generous annual leave and pension schemes. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture, with resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant. The position is available to start as soon as possible for the successful candidate. The successful candidate will be based at the Grantham Institute for Climate Change and the Environment and the School of Public Health at Imperial College London. They will join the EUROASIS consortium and work closely with Dr Garyfallos Konstantinoudis, Imperial colleagues and external research partners. This is a full-time post. This role is for a fixed-term contract for 3 years. For further information about the role, please contact Dr Garyfallos Konstantinoudis g.konstantinoudis@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29391": {
      "title": "Clinical Data Programmer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; School of Public Health; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED05891 About the role: Do you enjoy solving complex data challenges and using technology to improve the delivery of clinical trials? We are looking for an experienced data programmer who can take ownership of data management activities, work autonomously, and act as a trusted expert to trial teams. The ideal candidate will bring a blend of clinical data management experience and strong technical capability in Python, SQL, electronic data capture systems, and API integration to drive efficient, high-quality trial delivery. What you would be doing: You will: Support data management activities across the full trial lifecycle for assigned studies. Use and develop expertise in the unit’s EDC systems (currently OpenClinica and REDCap) to support complex clinical trials. Develop and maintain scripts for data validation, quality control, and the production of reports, listings, and metrics that support trial conduct, monitoring, and oversight. Collaborate with trial teams to plan and deliver data management activities, ensuring key timelines and milestones are met. Support compliance with Good Clinical Practice (GCP) and data protection requirements. What we are looking for: You will have: A strong interest in data management and its role in improving the quality, efficiency, and impact of clinical trials. A Bachelor’s degree (or equivalent experience) in computing, health informatics, data science, or a related field. Experience using clinical data management systems and electronic data capture (EDC) platforms in clinical trials. The ability to communicate technical and data management concepts clearly to non-technical colleagues, through participation in meetings at all levels. See the Job Description and Person Specification for more details. What we can offer you: A friendly and supportive team The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a readvertisement. Kindly note that further applications for the same job number will not be considered. Previous candidates need not apply. This is a full-time post (35 hours per week) for 12 months in the first instance. If you require any further details about the role, please contact Francesco Lala ( f.lala@imperial.ac.uk )"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29393": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in AI",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £45,399 - £48,876 per annum; Institute of Clinical Sciences; Researcher Reference: MED05968 About the role: Are you a computationally-minded scientist looking to apply artificial intelligence to help address a major clinical challenge in cardiology? We are seeking an enthusiastic Research Assistant to join the O’Regan Lab and contribute to a British Heart Foundation–funded programme investigating disease mechanisms and precision risk prediction in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM). You will work at the intersection of cardiac imaging, artificial intelligence and genetics, integrating 3D imaging with clinical and multi-omic data to help explain why HCM affects patients so differently, and to improve the identification of those most at risk. What you would be doing: In this role, you will: Process, analyse and quality-control large-scale cardiac imaging data, including machine-learning derived 3D cardiac models. Integrate imaging, genetic, multi-omic and longitudinal clinical data into analysis-ready datasets. Apply and help develop statistical and machine-learning models to characterise disease heterogeneity and predict patient risk. Write clean, well-documented and reproducible code, contributing to shared analysis pipelines and open-source tools. Present your findings at group meetings and, where appropriate, external conferences, and contribute to manuscripts and reports (including for the British Heart Foundation). Handle sensitive patient data responsibly and in line with information-governance and ethical requirements. What we are looking for: Masters (MSc) degree in a biomedical discipline, or equivalent research experience. Previous programming experience in Python and/or R, and a sound understanding of statistics and data analysis. A careful, well-organised approach to data handling, documentation and reproducibility. Clear written and verbal communication, and the ability to work both independently and within a multidisciplinary team. Ideally, some experience of machine-learning methodology, medical image analysis, or biomedical datasets (desirable, not essential). What we can offer you: Access to one of the largest and most deeply characterised HCM datasets available, combining genetics, AI-based imaging phenotypes and longitudinal outcomes. Dedicated mentorship from Dr Lara Curran within a multidisciplinary team spanning imaging, AI, genetics and cardiology, led by Professor Declan O’Regan. Strong scope for professional development. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. As a member of research staff you have 10 development days to use to develop your skills and explore your career prospects. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This post is based in the Computational Cardiac Imaging Group (O’Regan Lab) at the MRC Laboratory of Medical Sciences, Imperial College London. This is a full-time post and is offered on a fixed-term contract for 24 months. Hybrid working is permitted, but in-person working minimum of 3 days a week. As this post is exempt from the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974, a satisfactory Disclosure and Barring Service (DBS) check , at the appropriate level, will be required for the successful candidate. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr Lara Curran ( l.curran@imperial.ac.uk )."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29400": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; School of Public Health; Researcher Reference: MED05994 About the role: We invite applications for a Research Associate to develop novel methods for pathogen genomic epidemiology in the Department of Infectious Disease Epidemiology, School of Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, Imperial College London. The post is funded by a Wellcome Early-Career Award and will be based in a new group within the Department of Infectious Disease Epidemiology. The group develops scalable mathematical and statistical approaches leveraging large pathogen genome datasets to understand the determinants of infectious disease transmission . Our work combines data analysis, genomic epidemiology, mathematical modelling and statistical inference to answer public health relevant questions. We are committed to building a positive and inclusive environment, where all team members feel that they belong and are excited to do research. We recognise the systemic barriers that exist in higher education and research, particularly for people from historically marginalised groups. Applicants from groups underrepresented in science and research are particularly encouraged to apply. What you would be doing: The successful candidate will develop and apply statistical and computational methods to characterise infectious disease transmission from pathogen genomic data, with an initial focus on seasonal influenza and opportunities to extend the work to other pathogens. Projects will build on approaches developed in the group that rely on genetically proximal sequences and do not require full phylogenetic tree reconstruction. The work will include a combination of method development and validation, computational simulations and data analysis, which can be balanced depending on the postholder’s interests and experience. This work is fundamentally interdisciplinary, combining infectious disease epidemiology, pathogen evolution, genomic epidemiology, mathematical modelling, statistical inference and quantitative data analysis. We do not expect candidates to be experts in all these areas, and welcome applicants with a strong foundation in one or more relevant disciplines who are enthusiastic about developing expertise across these areas. What we are looking for: Hold a PhD in infectious disease epidemiology, computational biology, population biology, biostatistics or a related quantitative discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. Demonstrated experience developing mathematical and/or statistical approaches to answer complex biological questions. Experience conducting quantitative analyses of complex biological data using rigorous analytical approaches. Experience disseminating research through high-quality scientific outputs, such as peer-reviewed publications, preprints, software tools and conference presentations. Advanced programming skills in R and/or Python. Knowledge of, or strong interest in learning about, infectious disease epidemiology. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. As a member of research staff you have 10 development days to use to develop your skills and explore your career prospects. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: Please note that this is a PhD level role but candidates who have not yet been officially awarded will be appointed as a Research Assistant . This is a full-time and fixed-term contract for two years. The expected start date for this post is 01 st February 2027 (with flexibility on the start date), and the contract end date will be 31 st January 2029. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr Cecile Tran Kiem – cecile.tran-kiem18@imperial.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29405": {
      "title": "Senior Platform Manager (Server Infrastructure)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £71,793- £82,031 per annum; Information & Communication Technologies; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01428 About the role: We are looking for a skilled and motivated Platform Team Lead (Server Infrastructure) to join our Infrastructure team. In this role, you will take management of the server infrastructure platforms and services that underpin our core server infrastructure, with responsibility for designing, building, automating, and operating resilient compute, storage, and backup environments across the organisation. Working as part of the wider Hybrid Cloud team, you will help deliver secure, scalable, and highly available infrastructure services that support critical research, teaching, and professional services systems. You will manage the evolution of our infrastructure strategy, ensuring our platforms remain reliable, cost-effective, and aligned with current and future organisational needs. This is an exciting opportunity to play a key role in modernising infrastructure services and driving adoption of automation, observability, and platform reliability best practices. What you would be doing: You will be responsible for the operational management, maintenance, and continual improvement of enterprise server infrastructure platforms, including hosting, virtualisation, compute, storage, and backup technologies. You will: Manage physical and virtual server infrastructure across colo and campus datacentres Manage enterprise storage platforms, ensuring performance, resilience, scalability, and effective lifecycle management. Manage backup and recovery platforms, including testing, monitoring, retention, and disaster recovery processes. Monitor platform health, capacity, and performance, proactively identifying opportunities for optimisation and service improvement. Manage the support incident response, root cause analysis, and problem management activities for critical infrastructure services. Work closely with security, networking, cloud, and application teams to ensure infrastructure services meet operational and compliance requirements. Produce and maintain clear technical documentation, operational procedures, and knowledge articles. Attend the college’s datacentre locations when required. What we are looking for: Proven experience in managing enterprise server infrastructure in a complex environment. Experience in Leading Technical Teams. Knowledge of infrastructure monitoring, alerting, and observability tooling. Strong troubleshooting and problem-solving skills with the ability to manage competing priorities effectively. Excellent communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to work collaboratively across technical teams. Desirable experience includes: Public cloud infrastructure experience (Azure, AWS, or Google Cloud). Knowledge of security and compliance standards relevant to infrastructure operations. Experience working within ITIL-aligned service management environments. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time post (35 hours per week). If you require any further details about the role, please contact: [Iain Darvell] – [i.darvell@imperial.ac.uk]."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29379": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Long-Wavelength Oxygenic Photosynthesis",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Life Sciences; Researcher Reference: NAT02297 About the role: Biochemical/biophysical research in a field-leading team on one of the hottest areas of photosynthesis research: the recently discovered, low-energy chlorophyll-f variants of the photosystems. Structure/function studies on this system have provided surprises and a new angle from which to view and understand oxygenic photosynthesis: the process that puts the energy into the biosphere. What you would be doing: You will use a combination of interdisciplinary methods to solve the current burning questions in the field. These methods include biochemistry, biophysics, spectroscopy, molecular bioenergetics, redox methods, structural biology (e.g., cryo-EM), molecular biology, molecular enzymology, evolutionary considerations, and comparative work with other photosystems and other reaction centres. You will have the opportunity not only to assimilate in-house expertise but also to gain further experience through collaborative work (on theory, computational chemistry, other spectroscopies, etc) elsewhere in Imperial, the UK, and abroad. The team’s energy accounting expertise has also allowed insights useful for energy policy; you are welcome to contribute to these activities. What we are looking for: Ideally, you would have specific experience of the subject, as described below and in the job description as being “desirable”. Practical experience of biophysical methods on the photosystems and or bioenergetic complexes (spectroscopy, electrochemical approaches, structural approaches, etc). Practical experience in isolation and characterisation of the membrane protein complexes of bioenergetics. Practical experience in microbiology and molecular biology of cyanobacteria. However, excellent candidates with less direct experience of the specific field or with other relevant expertise will be considered (see job description). What we can offer you: The opportunity to work in a world leading group on what is arguably the hottest subject in the field: long wavelength oxygenic photosynthesis. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant. The candidate is expected to start preferably before February 2027. The team is currently 3 grad students, 2 post docs, 2 staff, 1 technician/lab manager, usually a few masters and UG students. We share lab facilities and office space with 3 other groups with related interests and other expertise. We work closely with a group in physics on theory and experiments and have several established collaborations with other spectroscopy and theory groups in the UK and abroad. (Some relevant publications from the team DOI: 10.1126/science.aar8313 , https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.79890 , https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-025-08326-y , DOI: 10.1126/science.ado6830, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-73964-7). If you require any further details about the role, please contact: A.W. Rutherford and Andrea Fantuzzi – a.rutherford@imperial.ac.uk and a.fantuzzi@imperial.ac.uk . Be sure to put “Far-red PS Post Doc position” as the subject please."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29373": {
      "title": "Research Assistant or Associate in Data-Driven Modelling of Fuel Cell Dynamics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £45,399- £59,484 per annum; Department of Aeronautics; Researcher Reference: ENG04040 About the role: We are looking for a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join an Airbus- and Aerospace Technology Institute (ATI)-funded project to develop data-driven modelling methods for next-generation hydrogen-powered commercial aircraft. This post is one of three postdoctoral positions within the wider Airbus-ATI project developing computational and experimental methods to build interpretable models of the complex, nonlinear dynamic behaviour of fuel cells. These models will provide an in-depth understanding of fuel cell behaviour under different operating conditions, helping to improve design, control, performance, and reliability. What you would be doing: You will focus on developing nonlinear dynamic models of fuel cell electrochemistry and on identifying interpretable reduced-order models for fuel cell control using the Sparse Identification of Nonlinear Dynamics (SINDy) algorithm applied to numerical and experimental data. You will work closely with the Chemistry group of Prof Anthony Kucernak and the Mechanical Engineering group of Dr Ludovic Renson . What we are looking for: You should have a strong background in fuel cell systems and the numerical modelling of electrochemical systems, together with experience in machine learning and data-driven modelling. Research Associate: Hold a PhD in Aerospace Engineering, Chemistry, or a related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience Research Assistant: A first / masters degree (or equivalent) in Aerospace Engineering, Chemistry, or a closely related discipline. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. What we can offer you: The opportunity to join a large research team across three departments at Imperial. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: If you require any further details on the role please contact: Dr Urban Fasel – u.fasel@imperial.ac.uk Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you encounter any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29365": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04045 About the role: Applications are encouraged to work on a full time, fixed term position for 12 months in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering. It forms part of the Floods and Droughts Research Infrastructure (FDRI) programme, a major five-year investment by the UK Government designed to enhance the UK's resilience to floods, droughts and other hydrological extremes through world-leading environmental monitoring, research and innovation. You will play a key role in developing and evaluating innovative approaches to environmental sensing and telemetry within FDRI. You will focus on implementing and piloting novel telemetry systems, including LoRaWAN networks and open data pathways, enabling researchers and technology developers to test and deploy innovative sensing technologies across FDRI observatories. This work will contribute to the creation of innovation testbeds that allow next generation environmental monitoring technologies to be demonstrated, evaluated and refined in real-world conditions, helping to ensure that FDRI remains accessible to a wide range of research and industry communities. What you would be doing: Your key activities will include implementing, operating and evaluating LoRaWAN infrastructure within selected FDRI catchments; developing open telemetry workflows and experimental platforms that enable researchers and technology developers to trial third-party sensing technologies at FDRI sites; and providing technical expertise for environmental sensor networks. You will support innovation pilots and technology demonstrations, work across FDRI to align activities with observatory deployment, site access and governance requirements, and help define future service models for integrating external technologies. The role offers substantial scope for methodological innovation and conceptual leadership at the interface between hydrology, data science and socio-technical systems. You will work within a highly interdisciplinary and international environment, collaborating closely with experts in research software engineering, technological innovation, environmental policy, and development studies. You will have strong coordination skills across local and distributed teams in the UK, as well as undertaking the supervision and mentoring of PhD students. What we are looking for: Hold a PhD in hydrology, environmental engineering, or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience Practical experience within a research and/or innovation environment, with evidence of delivering high-quality research or technical outputs Experience developing, deploying or operating sensor networks, telemetry systems, environmental monitoring platforms or related digital infrastructure Knowledge of hydrological data collection, curation, and analysis Creative approach to problem-solving Excellent verbal and written communication skills What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a fixed-term full-time post for 12 months. Please see the job description for full details and specifications of the post. If you require any further details about the role, please contact Prof Wouter Buytaert at w.buytaert@imperial.ac.uk . Any queries regarding the application process should be directed to Briony Webb at b.webb@imperial.ac.uk . Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. We are an equal opportunity employer and welcome applications from all backgrounds and disabilities. We will ensure that individuals with disabilities are provided reasonable adjustments to participate in the interview process and to perform crucial job functions, as well as other support including staff networks. Please contact us to request accommodation."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29352": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Space & Planetary Physics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Physics; Researcher Reference: NAT02250 About the role: We are seeking a Research Associate in space & planetary physics to make important contributions to our understanding of the largest moon of Jupiter: Ganymede. You will work both independently and as part of UK and international teams tied to the ESA JUICE mission which is expected to arrive at the Jupiter system in July 2031. You will analyse existing magnetic field and plasma measurements at Ganymede, revealing the electromagnetic and plasma environment around the moon and constraining the electrical conductivity of the of the subsurface ocean. What you would be doing: You will synthesize existing in-situ magnetic field and plasma measurements around Ganymede, augmented by theory and well-established modelling techniques, to characterize the time-varying magnetic field and plasma environment of Ganymede, which is essential for high-precision electromagnetic sounding of Ganymede’s subsurface ocean. You will regularly interact with other space and planetary scientists at Imperial College London, other members of the JUICE J-MAG team, and the wider ESA JUICE team. You will present your results at conferences and via peer-reviewed publications. What we are looking for: A PhD in Space/Planetary Physics or a closely related discipline Experience in the analysis of spacecraft data, theory, or numerical modelling A track record of original research that includes peer-reviewed publications Excellent communication skills What we can offer you: Membership of the JUICE J-MAG science team as the mission enters its exciting science phase. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant. Where Imperial or funder conditions necessitate, you will be required to complete timesheets for your work on projects in a timely manner. This is a full-time position with an anticipated start date between January 2027 and September 2027. The earliest possible start date is 1st January 2027 while the latest possible start data is 30th September 2027. You will join a research group that has extensive science heritage through major involvement in the past Cassini-Huygens mission and the ongoing JUICE mission. We play an active role in the definition of future missions spanning space and planetary physics. For more information about the post please contact Associate Professor Hao Cao h.cao@imperial.ac.uk and Associate Professor Adam Masters a.masters@imperial.ac.uk . Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help. The Department of Physics is proud to hold an Athena SWAN Silver Award, recognising our commitment to fostering a supportive, inclusive, and thriving community. We value the strength that comes from diverse perspectives, and we warmly welcome applications from talented people of all backgrounds, and particularly those underrepresented in physics, to join our vibrant community supported by initiatives that promote collaboration, inclusivity, and professional development. Find out more about our inclusive environment."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29333": {
      "title": "Research Assistant or Associate in Aircraft Multidisciplinary Design Optimisation",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £45,399- £59,484 per annum; Department of Aeronautics; Researcher Reference: ENG04036 About the role: This post (Research Assistant/Associate in Aircraft Multidisciplinary Design Optimisation) will allow you to contribute to a major industry-funded project developing the next generation of aircraft wings, led by Airbus and funded by the Aerospace Technology Institute. You will develop strong research, supervision, network, communication and project management skills. You will gain unique experience developing cutting-edge computational tools that bridge aeroelasticity, structural analysis and manufacturing constraints for early-stage design. You will get the opportunity to travel to international scientific conferences and to industry partners (Airbus). Your work will focus on development methods for aircraft design in dynamic environments. Computational strategies for aircraft design optimisation are currently based on static (or static equivalent) conditions, but this can be too conservative for high-aspect-ratio wings under dynamic conditions, such as flight through atmospheric turbulence. This project will develop algorithms to bring dynamic aeroelasticity into the optimization loop on a framework that scales to industrial-level analysis. We will use high-performance numerical computing on modern hardware, thus leveraging the vast computational infrastructure built for AI to redefine the art of the possible in next-generation aircraft design. What you would be doing: You will develop computational methods for wing optimization that consider the nonlinear dynamic aeroelastic behaviour of the wings under a vast numbers of scenarios and identifies critical conditions and their changes with respect to wing design parameters. This will be achieved through GPU-accelerated computing, programme-level algorithm differentiation, and advanced software methods for collective operations over large parameter spaces. Outcomes will be embedded in an optimization framework that also includes structural sizing and failure analysis of wing components. What we are looking for: Research Associate: Hold a PhD in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. Research Associate and Assistant: A recognised first class masters degree (or equivalent) in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline In addition: Experience in computational modelling and design optimization, preferably in an aircraft design context Experience in scientific computing, in particular, using Python and JAX. Some experience with machine learning algorithms and GPUs An interest in working in a larger group, collaborating with industry. What we can offer you: The opportunity to build a strong network with contacts at Airbus The opportunity to develop computational tools for early-stage aircraft wing design that will inform design practice at Airbus Ample scope for travelling to international conferences to disseminate the work done. Ample scope for publishing in leading journals. The opportunity to gain experience with cutting-edge computational methods at the interface of aeroelasticity, structural analysis and optimisation. The opportunity to gain supervision and project management experience. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: This post is available until 31st October 2029. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Professor Rafael Palacios r.palacios@imperial.ac.uk Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you encounter any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29325": {
      "title": "Research Assistant or Associate in Computational Methods for Structural Wing Design",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £45,399- £59,484 per annum; Department of Aeronautics; Researcher Reference: ENG04034 About the role: This post (Research Assistant/Associate in Computational Methods for Structural Wing Design) will allow you to contribute to a major industry-funded project developing the next generation of aircraft wings, led by Airbus and funded by the Aerospace Technology Institute. You will develop strong research, supervision, network, communication and project management skills. You will gain unique experience developing cutting-edge computational tools that bridge aeroelasticity, structural analysis and manufacturing constraints for early-stage design. You will get the opportunity to travel to international scientific conferences and to industry partners (Airbus). Your work will focus on developing integrated computational tools for structural wing design, linking advanced failure models, uncertainty quantification, and structural surrogate frameworks based on Artificial Intelligence. You will develop tools that bridge from detailed simulation to rapid predictions, compute structural sensitivities, address uncertainty in material properties, and integrate these capabilities into a multi-disciplinary optimisation framework. What you would be doing: You will develop computational tools that link aeroelastic analysis with structural sizing and failure assessment for early-stage wing design. This includes developing AI-based structural surrogate frameworks, computing failure index sensitivities using analytical or automatic differentiation approaches, quantifying uncertainty in material properties, and integrating these into a multi-disciplinary optimisation framework. What we are looking for: Research Associate : Hold a PhD in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. Research Associate and Assistant: A recognised first class masters degree (or equivalent) in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline In addition: Experience in computational mechanics, failure modelling of fibre-reinforced composites using FEM, use of Artificial Intelligence for surrogate models An interest or experience with structural optimisation Experience in scientific coding (e.g. Fortran, Python, C++) An interest in working in a larger group, collaborating with industry. What we can offer you: The opportunity to build a strong network with contacts at Airbus The opportunity to develop computational tools for early-stage aircraft wing design that will inform design practice at Airbus Ample scope for travelling to international conferences to disseminate the work done. Ample scope for publishing in leading journals. The opportunity to gain experience with cutting-edge computational methods at the interface of aeroelasticity, structural analysis and optimisation. The opportunity to gain supervision and project management experience. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: This contract is available until 31st October 2029. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Silvestre Pinho – silvestre.pinho@imperial.ac.uk Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you encounter any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29330": {
      "title": "Research Assistant or Associate in Modelling Tow-Steered Composite Wing Structures",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £45,399- £59,484 per annum; Department of Aeronautics; Researcher Reference: ENG04035 About the role: This post (Research Assistant/Associate in Modelling Tow-Steered Composite Wing Structures) will allow you to contribute to a major industry-funded project developing the next generation of aircraft wings, led by Airbus and funded by the Aerospace Technology Institute. You will develop strong research, supervision, network, communication and project management skills. You will gain unique experience developing physically-based and AI-based failure modelling methodologies for advanced tow-steered composite laminates, working closely with Airbus and iCOMAT. You will get the opportunity to travel to international scientific conferences and to industry partners (Airbus and iCOMAT). Your work will focus on developing failure modelling methodology for tow-steered composite laminates manufactured using automated fibre placement. Your research outcomes will underpin the development of next-generation composite wing structures with improved structural efficiency, and inform the design of future aerostructures. What you would be doing: You will develop physically-based and AI-based failure modelling methodology for tow-steered composite laminatesy. This will involve developing and implementing physics-based failure models and AI-surrogates that account for the variable fibre orientations and stiffness distributions characteristic of steered laminates. You will work closely with iCOMAT to understand manufacturing constraints and their effect on structural performance. An important aspect will be efficient communication of your findings to academic and industry partners to inform future design of aerostructures. What we are looking for: Research Associate: Hold a PhD in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. Research Associate and Assistant: A recognised first class masters degree (or equivalent) in Aerospace, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related disciplineExperience in failure modelling of fibre-reinforced composites using FEM In addition: Experience in using AI for modelling of fibre-reinforced composites Practical experience with coding failure models for composites An interest in working in a larger group, collaborating with industry. What we can offer you: The opportunity to build a strong network with contacts at Airbus and iCOMAT The opportunity to develop failure models for advanced tow-steered composites that will be deployed and used in practice Ample scope for travelling to international conferences to disseminate the work done. Ample scope for publishing in leading journals. The opportunity to gain experience with cutting-edge computational methods for novel composite manufacturing technologies. The opportunity to gain supervision and project management experience. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: This contract is available until 31st October 2029. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Silvestre Pinho – silvestre.pinho@imperial.ac.uk Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you encounter any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29306": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Computational Biomolecular Physics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 31/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; Competitive; Department of Physics; Researcher Reference: NAT02237 About the role: You will play a key role in a multidisciplinary programme on computational proteomics in a new physical dimension, supported by UKRI and the NSF, involving Imperial College London (Physics), the University of California San Diego (Computing), as well as industrial and academic partners in Europe and the US. The programme aims to develop conceptually new de novo sequencing tools based on two-dimensional partial covariance mass spectrometry, invented and developed in the Department of Physics at Imperial College London. This role offers a unique opportunity for a physicist interested in developing transformative analytical technologies. What you would be doing: This role combines computational and experimental aspects. You will design and implement advanced computational algorithms for reconstructing peptide sequences directly from experimental mass spectral data, without recourse to protein sequence databases. You will also be engaged in mass spectral data acquisition in an analytical mass spectrometry laboratory. You will work collaboratively with the Imperial College team and US project partners on the development and optimisation of de novo sequencing algorithms. You will liaise with project partners and participate in data acquisition campaigns in partner laboratories. What we are looking for: You will hold a PhD in Physics (or a closely related quantitative discipline) and have an outstanding record of research achievement. We are particularly interested in candidates with exceptional computational and analytical skills, demonstrated through the development of novel algorithms, advanced data-analysis methodologies, scientific software, machine learning approaches or other computational research tools. The ideal candidate will be intellectually ambitious, highly self-motivated and excited by the prospect of working at the boundary between physics, computation and biomolecular science. Experience in proteomics or mass spectrometry is not required; candidates with strong backgrounds in computational physics, statistical physics, machine learning, signal processing, inverse problems, optimisation, applied mathematics or related fields are particularly encouraged to apply. What we can offer you: The opportunity to contribute to a pioneering international research programme aimed at establishing an entirely new physical paradigm for biomolecular sequencing. You will help develop both the underlying physical methodology and the computational frameworks required to unlock information inaccessible to conventional analytical approaches, creating new capabilities with broad scientific and technological impact. Collaboration within a multidisciplinary team involving physicists, computer scientists and analytical mass spectrometrists. Involvement in publications, conference presentations and future grant applications. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and contribute to our mission of “Science for Humanity”. Access to Imperial’s sector-leading career support for researchers, as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. A sector-leading salary and remuneration package, including 41 days’ leave per year and generous pension schemes. A diverse, inclusive and collaborative working culture, with staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: The position is available immediately. We would ideally like the successful candidate to start as soon as possible, but the start date is flexible and can be agreed to accommodate notice periods and other commitments. This is a full-time (35 hours per week), fixed-term role based at our South Kensington Campus. If you require further details about the role, please contact: Professor Vitali Averbukh – v.averbukh@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29275": {
      "title": "Legal Counsel (Employment)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Department: Division of the University Secretary; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Professional / Support Services Reference: CEN00391 About the role: This is an exciting opportunity for an early career level employment lawyer with experience in advising on a broad range of employment matters to play a key role in the Legal & Regulatory Affairs team supporting Imperial College London’s People Function Team. The Legal & Regulatory Affairs team is well‑respected within Professional Services and plays a central role in enabling the University to achieve its strategic objectives. The team delivers consistently excellent, timely and pragmatic legal advice across the breadth of Imperial’s activities, with employment law forming a significant and high‑impact area of work. As the University continues to navigate a complex and evolving employment landscape, including litigation, organisational change, trade union engagement, and specialist matters such as TUPE, safeguarding and staff immigration, a requirement has arisen for this new post. What you would be doing: You will provide high quality, timely, commercially focused legal support on a broad range of employment law matters, with a primary focus on litigation. The role will assist the Senior Legal Counsel (Employment), where capacity permits, in advising on disciplinaries; grievances, TUPE transactions, investigations and restructuring exercises. You will work collaboratively with colleagues across Legal & Regulatory Affairs, People Function and external stakeholders, delivering pragmatic, robust, solutions focused advice and litigation support that is based upon a sound assessment of legal and other risks and represents excellent value for money to support the operational and strategic objectives of the University. What we are looking for: A minimum of degree-level education or equivalent experience; Qualified as a solicitor or barrister; Current Practising Certificate to practise law in England and Wales; Demonstrable experience of delivering high-quality employment litigation advice and representation together with providing legal advice at pace on a range of employment law work; Experience of working within a unionised work environment and having advised on collective consultation and pay and reward matters; A sound understanding of core employment law principles, with the confidence to apply legal judgement pragmatically across varied employment law queries; Clear ability to analyse and evaluate often complex data to identify risks and make well considered, pragmatic decisions that demonstrate reliable legal judgement; Excellent oral and written communication skills, with demonstrable ability to explain complex legal matters in a clear, concise way, both orally and in writing; Strong drafting and negotiation skills. A full list of essential and desirable criteria can be found in the Job Description and Person Specification. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: In your application, with reference to each of the individual essential requirements listed in the person specification, please provide specific information describing how you meet these requirements. Please tell us how your work experience, knowledge, skills and abilities, are relevant in no more than 2 sides of A4. If you require any further details on the role please contact Senior Legal Counsel (Employment), Melissa Powys-Rodrigues on m.powys-rodrigues@imperial.ac.uk . Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29255": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Marie Skłodowska-Curie Doctoral Candidate)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; Competitive; Department of Physics; Researcher Reference: NAT02280 About the role: Do you want to understand metamaterials that vary in time? Imperial College London is recruiting two Doctoral Candidates (DCs) to work in the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions (MSCA) Doctoral Network (DN) \"SPARK\" (HORIZON-MSCA-2025-DN-01-01), devoted to spatiotemporal metamaterials and structured light fields. The DN will provide multidisciplinary training culminating in PhDs awarded by Imperial College London. What you would be doing: The goal of SPARK is to train a top-class cohort of 15 DCs, that will become the future R&D staff within the area of spatiotemporal metamaterials and light fields. We will create and consolidate links between strategic industry partners and leading academic partners including Eindhoven University of Technology (coordinator), Umeå University, INESC MN (Lisbon), Université de Namur, Universidad Pública de Navarra, Universidad Autónoma de Madrid and Imperial College London by means of common engagement in research and training courses. This will result in the new understanding of nature and creation of new photonic technologies based on spatiotemporal metamaterials and structured light. A crucial feature of the programme will be the close interaction with partners in both industry and academia and projects will involve a high degree of teamwork and interdisciplinary research, including secondments at a partner organisation. The two DCs at Imperial will each work on an experimental project while they are expected to work closely within Imperial and the whole network. The titles of the two projects are: Project DC4: Time-Varying Metamaterials for Light – Professor Riccardo Sapienza ( sapienza@imperial.ac.uk ), with a 6 months stay at TU/e (Prof. H. Caglayan) and a 3 months internship with AMAT Project DC9: Space-Time Beams for and from Space-Time Metamaterials – Professor Riccardo Sapienza ( sapienza@imperial.ac.uk ), with a 6 months stay at UPNA (Prof. I. Liberal) and a 3 months internship with Nireos What we are looking for: It is essential to the project that you have the following Masters degree in Physics, Engineering or equivalent An interest in one of the topics listed above (nanophotonics, ultrafast optics, metamaterials) Experience of undertaking a Masters research project (or equivalent) in a relevant experimental field Excellent verbal and written communication skills and ability to develop new concepts. In accordance with the criteria set out by the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Networks, the researchers must not have resided or carried out main activity (work, study, etc) in the UK for more than 12 months in the 3 years immediately prior to taking up the fellowship. Also, in accordance with the criteria the researcher at the time of recruitment must not yet have been awarded a doctoral degree. What we can offer you: Very competitive salary with potential for additional funding depending on your family status. 36 months funded through the SPARK MSCA network, with the additional benefits for employees. 6-month research stay at our academic partners, and 3-month industrial internship. Training program including research-specific and transferable skills courses. Active participation in workshops, conferences, and network-wide events to build professional and scientific connections. Stimulating, multidisciplinary, and international research environment within a prestigious European training network. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: In accordance with the criteria set out by the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Networks, the researchers must not have resided or carried out main activity (work, study, etc) in the UK for more than 12 months in the 3 years immediately prior to taking up the fellowship. Also, in accordance with the criteria the researcher at the time of recruitment must not yet have been awarded a doctoral degree. This is a full-time (35 hours per week), fixed-term role for 36 months, based at our South Kensington Campus. A competitive salary of £44,284 (£48,796 if family allowance applies) in accordance with the MSCA regulations for Doctoral Researchers will be offered, including a living allowance , a mobility allowance , and a family allowance (if applicable). Queries relating to the application process should be directed to Light Community Administrator , Sunita Aubeeluck - light@imperial.ac.uk Queries relating to the research project should be directed to Professor Riccardo Sapienza – r.sapienza@imperial.ac.uk The Department of Physics is proud to hold an Athena SWAN Silver Award, recognising our commitment to fostering a supportive, inclusive, and thriving community. We value the strength that comes from diverse perspectives, and we warmly welcome applications from talented people of all backgrounds, and particularly those underrepresented in physics, to join our vibrant community supported by initiatives that promote collaboration, inclusivity and professional development. Find out more about our inclusive environment."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29257": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Vector Control Strategies",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Life Sciences; Researcher Reference: NAT02290 About the role: We seek to appoint an experienced and dynamic Research Associate to join the Crisanti group. This role will contribute to a research award that aims to build and improve self-sustaining gene drive systems, based on the use of CRISPR/Cas9, with the ultimate goal of malaria control by decreasing the reproductive potential of mosquito populations and will be involved in the development of innovative genetic strategies to help reduce the disease burden. You will help produce independent and original research as part of a large team of postdocs and technicians within the group based at the South Kensington Campus. The project aims to design, build, and test novel gene drives by incorporating three components: nucleases with improved activity; rational choice of new genetic targets to be affected by the gene drive; and modulating and regulating gene drive expression in time and space. The post involves a high degree of teamwork and interdisciplinary research as well as daily interactions with other members of the lab. The post is funded by the multi-disciplinary research consortium called Target Malaria. What you would be doing: You will be expected to take initiative in planning their research, maintain an overview of the group’s efforts, submit publications to refereed journals, present their findings to colleagues and at conferences, contribute to the preparation of grant requests and take the lead in compiling quarterly reports to research sponsors. You will also be required to supervise the work of smaller research teams including undergraduate and postgraduate students; mentor and train less experienced members of the laboratory in learning and adapting new molecular approaches to vector control. We strongly encourage applications from experienced, self-driven individuals with an established interest in cutting-edge scientific research that has the real potential to contribute to global malaria eradication efforts. What we are looking for: The ideal candidate must have a PhD in molecular biology and genetics, or a closely related discipline, and significant experience in a similar research environment working on genetic approaches to population control of disease-vector mosquitoes, particularly CRISPR/Cas9 gene drive development and testing. Expertise to be supported by a track record of publication in relevant and refereed journals. The post holder should be comfortable with molecular biology methods and proficient in working with genome editing tools like CRISPR/Cas. Experience in laboratory and insectary practices and in experimental manipulation techniques is also required. The role also requires excellent organisational, communication and problem-solving skills, and the ability to lead effectively small teams and planning work to achieve research goals and milestones. Proficiency in transcriptomic analysis and DNA sequencing, including molecular workflows and bioinformatic analysis of genomic and gene expression data are highly desirable, though not essential. Demonstrable experience in supervising and training junior technical staff and students is also required. Flexibility is also essential, including a willingness to support operational needs and occasionally travel to collaborating sites. You will also be expected to work collaboratively and be open-minded, taking a positive approach to routine work and training opportunities relevant to the role. What we can offer you: This role offers an exciting opportunity to work within a pioneering research group focused on advancing innovations that could significantly improve malaria control. It provides the chance to collaborate with leading scientists across Europe, Africa and North America as part of a major international consortium, while working with cutting-edge molecular and insectary technologies that support ongoing professional development. You will join a supportive, inclusive community at one of the world’s top universities and benefit from a competitive salary and generous leave. Further information: This is a full-time fixed-term post to 31 December 2029. Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr Federica Bernardini ( f.bernardini11@imperial.ac.uk ) or Ms Clelia Supparo ( c.supparo@imperial.ac.uk )."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29181": {
      "title": "Chapman Fellow in Mathematics (Applied Mathematics Section)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Mathematics; Researcher Reference: NAT02281 About the role: Applications are invited for one or more Chapman Fellowships in Applied Mathematics within the Applied Mathematics Section of the Department of Mathematics, commencing September 2027. These will be for a fixed term of 3 years. The posts provide an excellent opportunity for those seeking to pursue an academic career in Applied Mathematics. What you would be doing: You will produce independent and original research in Applied Mathematics, interpreted broadly, within the Department of Mathematics, submit publications to leading refereed journals, and help with teaching and administration. You will be expected to undertake a self-directed program of research and scholarship, play a role in the national and international Mathematics community, in addition to teaching students at undergraduate level. You will also take a role in MSc teaching and project supervision, as appropriate. What we are looking for: A track record of research in Applied Mathematics (interpreted broadly) of the highest level. Drive to engage actively with Imperial’s thriving ecosystem of seminars, conferences and other events. Background and motivation for undergraduate teaching and also to teach on one of the leading MSc programmes in the field and to supervise MSc theses, in collaboration with external partners, as appropriate. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to advance science for humanity. Build your profile in the international research community by becoming an active part of one of the leading research groups in Applied Mathematics, pursuing research of the highest level across the whole breadth of the field. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: Positions are available for a fixed duration of 3 years. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £48,876 per annum. In addition to completing the online application, candidates should upload following documents: A full CV Cover letter Research Statement Teaching Statement List of publications If you have any queries about the posts, please contact the relevant section coordinator, Dr Philipp Thomas ( p.thomas@imperial.ac.uk ), or the Chair of the Chapman Fellowship panels, Professor Alastair Young ( alastair.young@imperial.ac.uk ). You may also want to contact somebody in the department whose research is close to yours. Closing date: 29 October 2026 Interview dates: January 2027"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29183": {
      "title": "Chapman Fellow in Mathematics (Mathematical Finance Section)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Mathematics; Researcher Reference: NAT02282 About the role: Applications are invited for one or more Chapman Fellowships in Mathematical Finance within the Mathematical Finance Section of the Department of Mathematics, commencing September 2027. These will be for a fixed term of 3 years. The posts provide an excellent opportunity for those seeking to pursue an academic career in Mathematical Finance. What you would be doing: You will produce independent and original research in Mathematical Finance, interpreted broadly, within the Department of Mathematics, submit publications to leading refereed journals, and help with teaching and administration. You will be expected to undertake a self-directed program of research and scholarship, play a role in the national and international Mathematics community, in addition to teaching students at undergraduate level. You will also take a role in MSc teaching and project supervision, as appropriate. What we are looking for: A track record of research in Mathematical Finance (interpreted broadly) of the highest level. Drive to engage actively with Imperial’s thriving ecosystem of seminars, conferences and other events. Background and motivation for undergraduate teaching and also to teach on one of the leading MSc programmes in the field and to supervise MSc theses, in collaboration with industrial partners, as appropriate. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Build your profile in the international research community by becoming an active part of one of the leading research groups in Mathematical Finance, pursuing research of the highest level across the whole breadth of the field of quantitative finance. Interact with the Mathematical Finance Section’s network of high-level industry collaborators in leading hedge funds and banks. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: Positions are available for a fixed duration of 3 years. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £ £ 48,876 per annum. In addition to completing the online application, candidates should upload following documents: A full CV Cover letter Research Statement Teaching Statement List of publications If you have any queries about the posts, please contact the relevant section coordinator Dr Yufei Zhang ( yufei.zhang@imperial.ac.uk ), or the Chair of the Chapman Fellowship panels Professor Alastair Young ( alastair.young@imperial.ac.uk ). You may also want to contact somebody in the department whose research is close to yours. Closing date: 29 October 2026 Interview dates: January 2027"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29187": {
      "title": "Chapman Fellow in Mathematics (Pure Mathematics Section)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Mathematics; Researcher Reference: NAT02284 About the role: Applications are invited for one or more Chapman Fellowships in Pure Mathematics within the Pure Mathematics Section of the Department of Mathematics, commencing September 2027. These will be for a fixed term of 3 years. The posts provide an excellent opportunity for those seeking to pursue an academic career in Pure Mathematics. What you would be doing: You will produce independent and original research in Pure Mathematics within the Department of Mathematics, submit publications to leading refereed journals, and help with teaching and administration. You will be expected to undertake a self-directed program of research and scholarship and to play a role in the national and international Mathematics community, in addition to teaching students at undergraduate level. You will also take a role in MSc teaching and project supervision, as appropriate. What we are looking for: A track record of research in Pure Mathematics (interpreted broadly) of the highest level. Drive to engage actively with Imperial’s thriving ecosystem of seminars, conferences and other events. Background and motivation for undergraduate teaching and also to teach on one of the leading MSc programmes in the field and to supervise MSc theses, as appropriate. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Build your profile in the international research community by becoming an active part of one of the leading research groups in Pure Mathematics, pursuing research of the highest level across the whole breadth of the field. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: Positions are available for a fixed duration of 3 years. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £48,876 per annum. In addition to completing the online application, candidates should upload following documents: A full CV Cover letter Research Statement Teaching Statement List of publications If you have any queries about the posts, please contact the relevant section coordinator, Prof Steven Sivek ( s.sivek@imperial.ac.uk ), or the Chair of the Chapman Fellowship panels, Professor Alastair Young ( alastair.young@imperial.ac.uk ). You may also want to contact somebody in the department whose research is close to yours. Closing date: 29 October 2026 Interview dates: January 2027"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29185": {
      "title": "Chapman Fellow in Mathematics (Statistics Section)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Mathematics; Researcher Reference: NAT02283 About the role: Applications are invited for one or more Chapman Fellowships in Statistics within the Statistics Section of the Department of Mathematics, commencing September 2027. These will be fixed term for 3 years. We welcome applications across the breadth of Statistical Science. These posts provide an excellent opportunity for those seeking to pursue an academic career in Statistics. What you would be doing: You will produce independent and original research in Statistics within the Department of Mathematics, submit publications to leading refereed journals, and help with teaching and administration. You will be expected to undertake a self-directed program of research and scholarship, play a role in the national and international Statistics community, in addition to teaching students at undergraduate level. You will also take a role in MSc teaching and project supervision. What we are looking for: A track record of research in Statistics (interpreted broadly) of the highest level. Drive to engage actively with Imperial’s thriving ecosystem of seminars, conferences and other events. Background and motivation for undergraduate teaching and also to teach on one of the leading MSc programmes in the field and to supervise MSc theses, in collaboration with external industrial partners, as appropriate. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Build your profile in the international research community by becoming an active part of one of the leading research groups in Statistics, pursuing research of the highest level across the whole breadth of the field. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: Positions are available for a fixed duration of 3 years. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £ £ 48,876 per annum. In addition to completing the online application, candidates should upload following documents: A full CV Cover letter Research Statement Teaching Statement List of publications If you have any queries about the posts, please contact the relevant section coordinator, Associate Professor Robin Ryder ( r.ryder@imperial.ac.uk ), or the Chair of the Chapman Fellowship panels, Professor Alastair Young ( alastair.young@imperial.ac.uk ). You may also want to contact somebody in the department whose research is close to yours. Closing date: 29 October 2026 Interview dates: January 2027"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29169": {
      "title": "Scientific Project Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Department of Brain Sciences; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED05963 About the role: We are looking for an enthusiastic and dynamic candidate who will provide day-to-day scientific project management and clinical trials operations support to Professor Edison to ensure the successful delivery of multiple studies and other research initiatives. What you would be doing: Join the Imperial College Memory Research Centre at Hammersmith Campus, Imperial College London, as a Scientific Project Manager working with the internationally renowned Professor Paul Edison . In this pivotal role, you will drive the delivery of a diverse and ambitious portfolio of interventional, observational, neuroimaging, and translational research studies across neurodegenerative conditions including Alzheimer's disease, mild cognitive impairment, and vascular dementia, utilising cutting-edge PET, MRI, and CSF biomarker technologies to investigate neuroinflammation and neurodegeneration across Phase 1 and Phase 2/3 studies. You will play a central role in grant writing, manuscript development, protocol creation, and horizon-scanning for new funding opportunities, while ensuring all studies are delivered to the highest scientific, operational, and regulatory standards. Professor Edison’s team has an outstanding international track record, publishing in world-leading journals including Nature Medicine, The Lancet Neurology, The BMJ, and other Nature journals and producing transformational research with significant societal impact. This is an exceptional opportunity to make a lasting and meaningful contribution to world-leading dementia and neuroimaging research at one of the UK's most prestigious and impactful research institutions. What we are looking for: You should be a highly motivated individual educated to postgraduate level, with a PhD in neuroscience and substantial experience working in this field. You should have excellent writing skills, with the ability to prepare scientific reports, draft grant applications, and contribute to high-quality manuscripts. You should have experience in preparing ethics submissions, managing budgets, supporting staff across research and healthcare settings, and contributing to effective project management. You should have in-depth working knowledge of UK Clinical Trials Regulations, Principles of GCP, the Data Protection Act, and the Research Governance Framework, together with a proven ability to apply these to the coordination of clinical trials. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution The opportunity to play a key role in transforming the treatment paradigm in neurodegenerative diseases and making a significant impact in the field. Further information: This is a full-time, fixed-term post for one year and is available to start immediately. There is the possibility for extension based on funding. You based at Imperial’s Hammersmith Campus in East Acton / White City. Please note that this is an office-based full-time onsite post, and may require travelling within the UK Should you require any further details on the role please contact Professor Paul Edison ( paul.edison@imperial.ac.uk ) and Hyacinth Henry ( hyacinth.henry@imperial.ac.uk )"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29046": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; Internationally Competitive; Department of Mechanical Engineering; Academic & Teaching Reference: ENG03987 About the role: The Department of Mechanical Engineering at Imperial College London is seeking two outstanding academics to join our world‑leading community. As part of this recruitment, we are seeking individuals who will contribute to our vibrant research culture, deliver inspiring teaching, and help drive the department’s strategic vision. The appointed Academics will contribute to one of the following strategic areas: Tribology & Interfacial Science, Computational Fluid Dynamics for Turbomachinery, Advanced Experimental Thermofluids Systems. As the #1 Mechanical Engineering department in the UK in REF 2021, we are internationally recognised for excellence in research and innovation. Our work spans fundamental discovery to real‑world application, shaping technologies and solutions that address global challenges. We are equally proud of our commitment to an exceptional student experience. The department consistently ranks in the top 10% in the UK for student satisfaction, reflecting our dedication to high‑quality teaching, supportive learning environments, and meaningful engagement with our students. In addition, Imperial’s Mechanical Engineering programmes regularly place in the UK top 10 in the QS World University Rankings, highlighting our global reputation for academic excellence. Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) is central to who we are. We believe that excellence thrives in an environment where diverse perspectives are valued and supported. We strongly encourage applications from candidates who are underrepresented in Mechanical Engineering, and we are committed to fostering an inclusive culture where all colleagues can succeed. If you are excited by the opportunity to shape the future of Mechanical Engineering at one of the world’s leading universities, we would be delighted to hear from you. What you would be doing: As a member of our academic community, you’ll drive forward world‑class research in one of our strategically identified areas. You’ll lead innovative projects, secure research funding, publish high‑impact work, and build collaborations across Imperial, industry, and the global research community. You will be fundamental in inspiring the next generation of engineers through exceptional teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels; contributing to curriculum development, supervising PhD researchers, and providing thoughtful academic and pastoral support. You’ll champion high‑quality education and help shape teaching practice within the department and beyond. You’ll also play an active role in shaping the department’s strategic direction; contributing to planning, representing Imperial externally, and strengthening partnerships across the Faculty of Engineering. Strong leadership, teamwork, and a commitment to an inclusive, collaborative culture are essential, as is a willingness to take on key administrative and managerial responsibilities that support our research and teaching mission. What we are looking for: We welcome applications at both Assistant Professor (Lecturer) and Associate Professor (Reader) level. Requirements vary by seniority and subject area, and applicants should review the Job Description for full details. Assistant Professors will demonstrate: A developing national reputation, evidenced by high‑quality publications Experience preparing competitive grant applications Experience teaching and supervising undergraduate and postgraduate students Evidence of contributing to the development and performance of colleagues Associate Professors will additionally demonstrate: An established national and emerging international reputation, supported by an excellent publication record A strong track record of securing significant research funding Extensive experience leading the design and delivery of research programmes The ability to act as a role model in research, teaching and academic leadership Proven management and administrative experience What we can offer you: Joining the Department of Mechanical Engineering at Imperial College London means becoming part of a world‑leading community that pushes the boundaries of research and education. You’ll work alongside exceptional colleagues, access state‑of‑the‑art facilities, and collaborate across disciplines to tackle global challenges The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Salaries Assistant Professor – from £70,718pa Associate Professor - from £82,969pa Further information: Application documents must include: 1. Comprehensive 2-page CV including: Degrees & other relevant qualifications (including universities and dates); Past and present positions, Memberships, awards, certifications and training courses, Noteworthy talks and engagements (e.g., keynote speeches etc), 2. Publication list, with your top four publications highlighted and containing links to the full text. Applications that do not include information in this format may not be considered. If you require any further details about the role, please contact Helen Stoneham: me-hr.requests@imperial.ac.uk . Interviews are expected to take place in April 2027."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29040": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor in Data-Intensive Physics and AI",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; Competitive; Department of Physics; Academic & Teaching Reference: NAT02275 About the role: The Department of Physics at Imperial College London seeks an outstanding academic to join us as an Assistant Professor or Associate Professor in Data-Intensive Physics and AI. Exceptional candidates may be considered for appointment at Associate Professor level. Many areas of physics rely on experiments, observatories, missions, and simulations that produce data at increasing scale and complexity. Inferring reliable physical conclusions from these data often requires methods designed around the scientific questions rather than the application of generic tools. We are therefore seeking a Data Physicist who combines a strong research programme in physics with expertise in machine learning, AI, statistics, or scientific computing. Your research will develop new approaches to analysis, inference and data mining for challenging physics datasets. It will also advance the machine learning and AI methods needed for this work, including the treatment of uncertainty, physical structure, robustness and computational scale. The search is department-wide and welcomes applicants rooted in any area of physics. The appointment will be made on the basis of scientific excellence and the strength of the proposed research programme. Illustrative areas in which you might work include inference and discovery in large, heterogeneous or high-dimensional physics datasets, simulation-based inference, inverse problems and surrogate modelling, fast and resource-efficient machine learning for real-time analysis, adaptive experimental control and autonomous instruments, and physics-informed and generative AI, including uncertainty quantification, robustness and interpretability. These areas indicate the breadth of the role and do not prescribe particular tools or physics subfields. What you would be doing: As a member of our academic faculty, you will: Conduct and publish innovative, internationally leading research within your field . Contribute to teaching and curriculum development at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Supervise and mentor PhD students and junior researchers. Secure external research funding and build productive collaborations within and beyond Imperial. Play an active role in the life and strategic development of the Department and the wider Faculty. Serve as a mentor and role model for colleagues and students. Contribute to the MRes in Machine Learning and Big Data in the Physical Sciences. Help develop proposals for CDT, Doctoral Focal Awards and related doctoral training initiatives, and contribute to their delivery where funded. Depending on your level of appointment, you may also: Contribute to departmental leadership and management. What we are looking for: We are seeking an outstanding academic who is committed to excellence in research, teaching, and contribution to the academic community. You will be able to demonstrate: A PhD in Physics or in a closely related field, or equivalent. An independent research programme of international quality that addresses clearly defined questions in physics, evidenced by high-quality publications in peer-reviewed journals (commensurate with your career stage). Demonstrated experience in research using large or complex datasets, simulations or data streams in physics, or a track record of developing original AI or machine learning methods for physics analysis and inference. Original methodological contributions to data-intensive science, machine learning or AI, evidenced by published research and not limited to the application of existing tools. Evidence that your ideas and methods transfer between datasets, simulations, experiments or physics settings. The potential to collaborate with researchers across the Department. Commitment to high-quality teaching and supervision of students. Excellent communication, collaboration, and leadership skills appropriate to your career stage. At the Assistant Professor level, you will have clear potential to develop an international reputation in data-intensive physics. At the Associate Professor level, you will have an established profile and emerging international recognition, independent scientific leadership including leading a team of researchers, and sustained success in securing external research funding. It would also be desirable for you to have: Experience of developing or deploying methods on accelerated or heterogeneous computing platforms, including GPUs, FPGAs or other specialised hardware, for real-time or large-scale physics applications. We welcome candidates whose work is grounded in one area of physics. We are looking for methodological depth and ideas that can be applied more widely, not evidence that you already work across several communities. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Access to state-of-the-art facilities, including significant high-performance computing resources through Imperial’s Research Computing Service. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year including public holidays and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: Salary Assistant Professor: £73,194 to £82,954 per annum Associate Professor: Minimum starting salary of £85,873 per annum Closing date: Monday, 19 October 2026, 23:59 hours (Midnight BST) This is an open-ended, full-time post (35 hours per week). The starting date will be agreed with the successful candidate. The Department of Physics is part of the Faculty of Natural Sciences at Imperial College London. We are an internationally renowned Physics department, which enables us to create groups of international standing, and with sufficient scale and critical mass, that they influence the research agenda and attract the world’s top researchers. The Department is structured into five Research Communities that drive new research directions: Light, Matter, Physics of Particles, Physics of the Universe, and Space, Plasma and Climate. Data-intensive methods and machine learning are used across all five research communities, and this appointment represents a strategic investment in a priority growth area for the Department. The post is department-wide and is intended to connect research across the communities. The Department is investing in AI infrastructure and has been bringing together its significant computing resources and expertise by setting up a new Computing Centre. Together with Imperial’s Research Computing Service and national facilities, the Centre will support data-intensive research across the Department, and the successful candidate will draw on these resources in developing their research programme and play a leading role in growing this activity. Imperial College is committed to equality of opportunity, eliminating discrimination and fostering an inclusive workplace culture. The Department of Physics is also proud to hold an Athena SWAN Silver Award, recognising our commitment to fostering a supportive, inclusive, and thriving community. We value the strength that comes from diverse perspectives, and we warmly welcome applications from talented people of all backgrounds, and particularly those underrepresented in physics, to join our vibrant community supported by initiatives that promote collaboration, inclusivity, and professional development. Find out more about our inclusive environment. If you would like more information or have specific queries about the role, please contact: Professor Stefan Söldner-Rembold, Head of Department – s.soldner-rembold@imperial.ac.uk If you have any questions about the application process, please contact: the Physics Knowledge Hub – physicsconnect@imperial.ac.uk If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=28968": {
      "title": "Professors (Assistant/Associate/Full) of Finance",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/11/2026 Department: Business School; Competitive; Academic & Teaching Reference: BUS00888 About the role: Imperial Business School is seeking qualified applicants for faculty positions in its Finance Department at the rank of Assistant, Associate and Full Professor. Some of the initial screening will be conducted, via Zoom. What you would be doing: You will contribute to the academic mission of Imperial College London to deliver world class scholarship, education and research. In addition, you will be required to contribute to administration within the Department. What we are looking for: In order to be successful in this role, you have: A PhD in Finance or related field or equivalent Have a National/ International reputation (commensurate with grade) Track record in attracting high levels of research funding (commensurate with grade) Extensive experience in leading the design of research programmes (commensurate with the grade) Applicants for positions of Assistant Professors should have the potential to publish innovative, rigorous, and relevant contributions in top academic journals. Candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position should demonstrate considerable excellence in research. A full list of responsibilities and essential requirements can be seen in the attached Job Description. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: Imperial is committed to equality of opportunity, to eliminating discrimination and to creating an inclusive working environment. We particularly welcome applicants that will increase diversity within the Department of Finance. Interested candidates will need to click \"Apply now\" and submit an online application form. Your application will need to include a CV and a Job Market paper. Your CV must be uploaded in the field marked as \"CV\". The Job Market paper must be clearly named and can be uploaded in the field marked as “other”. Your application will need to include a CV and and a job market paper . For Assistant Professor entry level, we will also require 3 reference letters to be emailed to financerecruitment@imperial.ac.uk Closing date: 15 November 2026 (midnight GMT)"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=28877": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Fair and Responsible Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; Competitive; Dyson School of Design Engineering; Academic & Teaching Reference: ENG03889 About the role: Do you want to change the world for the better? Do you believe this can be done through research and education? At the Dyson School of Design Engineering , our motto is “from making better things to making things better”. Join an internationally leading institution, aiming to shape the future of engineering, at the interface between technology, people and society. The Dyson School of Design Engineering is the newest engineering department at Imperial College London and we strive to bring a unique value proposition to our students, research/industry partners, and to society as a whole. It was founded in 2014 and was partly funded through a generous donation from the James Dyson Foundation. Our vision is to empower innovators and decision-makers to harness rigorous and creative disciplines towards constructing better social and technical systems using new technologies as building blocks. By fusing the technical to the human, we aim to help solve global problems with emphasis on people, sustainability, entrepreneurship and societal impact. We are seeking to appoint one academic staff member at Assistant Professor level to conduct high-impact research and to deliver outstanding teaching and student experience in areas relating to Design Engineering, defined broadly, in both the School and across Imperial. We are looking to invest in future research and academic leaders who have the ambition to make a substantial impact in the UK and globally. The role is within Fair and Responsible Artificial Intelligence, part of Imperial's Science for Humanity strategy, which seeks to maximise the College's contribution as a force for good in the world. We are seeking candidates who develop novel AI methods and systems that are technically rigorous, trustworthy and deployable in practice, advancing areas such as fairness, interpretability, robustness, safety, privacy and human-AI interaction while contributing to the next generation of AI technologies. This post is unlikely to be the right fit for candidates whose research primarily uses existing AI tools or models as instruments within human factors, sustainability or social-impact studies, without making a substantive contribution to the design, development, adaptation, evaluation or responsible deployment of AI systems. What you would be doing: Research – As a part of the Dyson School of Design Engineering, you will actively develop and lead your own research programme, in line with our research themes and vision. You will be expected to undertake high-quality research and to publish in well-regarded journals/conferences in your field. You will be active in the pursuit of external funding to support your research, while enhancing the reputation of our institution through your external engagements. Since the School promotes a strong collaborative and inclusive research environment, you will be expected to initiate and expand research collaborations with colleagues at the School, at Imperial College London, and beyond. Most importantly, we want your research to have a meaningful impact in the world through translation, patents, policy influence, spin-outs, and wider public and industry engagement. Teaching – The Dyson School of Design Engineering provides exceptional learning experience and environment to our students, and is continuously recognised for it. As our new Faculty staff member, you will contribute to the School’s teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. You will also undertake supervision of undergraduate and MRes/MSc projects. And, in view of our new programmes and of our continuously evolving teaching portfolio, you may be expected to contribute, as appropriate, to the development of new teaching materials at all levels. Administration – As part of a collegial and inclusive academic community, you will be expected to make a contribution to the day-to-day running of the School when required. This will translate to taking on School-wide administrative role(s) commensurate with the level of appointment. You will also attend School-wide meetings and contribute to student recruitment activities. You will support with promoting equality, diversity and inclusion. Eventually, you may be expected to undertake other duties appropriate to the grade as specified by the Head of School. Responsibilities will be allocated in a way that is proportionate to career stage, with mentoring and workload planning during probation to support development in research, education and citizenship. This post is unlikely to be the right fit for candidates whose research primarily uses existing AI tools or models as instruments within human factors, sustainability or social-impact studies, without making a substantive contribution to the design, development, adaptation, evaluation or responsible deployment of AI systems. What we are looking for: We invite applications from candidates whose research and teaching interests align with Fair and Responsible AI (including trustworthiness, explainability, robustness, safety, privacy and governance), as applied to: Human-centred AI , including methods that explicitly model human needs, behaviours, preferences, values and decision-making. Human-AI collaboration , including interactive, collaborative and adaptive AI systems that augment human capabilities. Behavioural modelling and computational understanding of human behaviour , including AI methods for modelling cognition, perception, decision-making, social interaction and human adaptation. Multimodal AI for understanding people and environments , including learning from language, audio, touch, vision, physiological, behavioural and other multimodal data. AI for societal impact , including applications that address challenges in healthcare, wellbeing, accessibility, sustainability, energy, education, manufacturing, business and the public sector. AI assurance, risk and insurance , including methods for evaluating and managing the technical, human and societal risks associated with AI systems. Candidates are expected to demonstrate a track-record of executing research which combines deep knowledge of state-of-the-art AI with design thinking, evidencing a shared ethos to the School’s approach to societal challenges. Design engineers develop their research and teaching with the human at the centre. The research portfolio and vision should combine methodological contributions and focus on relevant application areas (e.g., healthcare, sustainability, etc.). Besides these research-focused areas, we expect the successful candidate to be able to contribute to our teaching portfolio, particularly on the topic of state-of-the-art machine learning methods (for example, explaining the latest foundation models). We additionally are keen for the successful candidate to have a flair for entrepreneurship and innovation through, e.g., engagements with industry, spin-off companies, advising roles, etc. What we can offer you: The Dyson School of Design Engineering will offer you: The opportunity to grow your leadership in research and teaching at one of the World's top universities. The opportunity to connect within our urban campus in the middle of London with a huge network of world-leading researchers, groups and facilities. Opportunities to influence industry, policy and institutions worldwide, and for high-profile engagement with leading external bodies including industry and government. Supported opportunities to develop technology transfer, via consultancy, licensing and spin-outs. A merit-based structure for promotion, with a wide range of opportunities for training and support. The opportunity to shape the research agenda of a young and dynamic department. Being part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . A welcome package that includes one PhD studentship that can be recruited immediately. We offer an attractive sector-leading salary and remuneration package (with 43 annual leave days per year) , including a pension scheme with additional employer contribution and generous conditions of service with flexible and family-friendly working policies. The School and its members have an open-minded and diverse culture, based on a shared ambition, constructive feedback and respect. This is a rare opportunity for you to continue your career at a world-leading institution. Further information: This is a full-time, open-ended position, subject to probation. How to apply: Please apply online, uploadin g the following supporting documents (observing page limits): Cover letter (1 page), detailing your motivations and suitability to join the School Research statement on past impact and future vision (3 pages), highlighting the applicant’s 3 most important publications to date (only refer to papers that are already published (i.e. not “in preparation”) and accessible to the panel) and describing their impact within academia, industry and society, then detailing expected future directions, funding, and any necessary equipment and infrastructure. Teaching and supervision statement (2 pages) detailing past teaching experience (offering examples and evidence where possible) and future teaching vision, as well as past supervision and mentorship roles. Full CV , with degrees (including subject, universities and dates), past and present employment, a complete list of publications (only those publicly available, not “in preparation”), past and current funding (please state your role in the project and make clear what share of the funding was awared to you personally vs the consortium as a whole), supervision, contribution to the scientific community, public engagements, teaching contributions etc. Imperial is a signatory to DORA , and so applicants are discouraged from offering contrived metrics like cumulative impact factor as evidence of impact; these will be ignored. Please use a size 11 font, with reasonable spacing and margins, for all documents (except the CV, which can be as you wish). Please refer to the attached ENG03889 How to apply - Structure questions for longlisting document for further information on the application requirements. Applications without all the requested information will not be considered. Enquiries should be directed to the Head of School, Prof. Robert Shorten – r.shorten@imperial.ac.uk or the hiring lead, Prof. Sam Cooper – designeng-recruitment@imperial.ac.uk All candidates will be contacted after the shortlisting is completed. Application Closing Date : 19 October 2026 Provisional Interview Dates : 8-12 February 2027 Expected start date: between May and September 2027"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=28890": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Quantum Computation",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Computing; Researcher Reference: ENG04009 About the role: Dr Zhenyu Cai’s research group in the Department of Computing seeks a highly motivated and exceptionally talented quantum theorist to lead projects aimed at developing a full-stack approach to suppressing errors in quantum hardware. This research focuses on achieving practical quantum computation by integrating techniques ranging from hardware-level noise suppression, quantum error mitigation, all the way to full fault-tolerant computation. What you would be doing: The successful candidate will contribute to cutting-edge theoretical research in topics including (but not limited to): Quantum architectures, compilation, and scalable quantum systems Quantum error correction Quantum error mitigation Hardware implementation of quantum error correction/mitigation protocols This post is primarily funded by the EPSRC Quantum Technologies Fellowship held by Dr Cai. Although theory-focused, the position encourages engagement with experimental partners, including those in industry. The project will support research visits and joint activities with these collaborators. The research will involve both analytical work and numerical computations. The balance between analytical and numerical type work is flexible and can depend on the preferences and skills of the successful applicant. Access to high-performance computing facilities and cloud-based quantum hardware will be provided to support the simulation and verification of theoretical methods. What we are looking for: A PhD in Computer Science, Mathematics, Physics or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research / industrial experience. Practical experience within a research environment and publication in relevant and refereed journals / conferences in the field of quantum computation. Experience of dealing with industry partners and research collaborators. Experience of error correction simulation using packages like Stim and/or conducting large quantum circuit simulation and experience of scientific programming involving HPC or GPU systems; experience of participating in collaborative software projects; use of GitHub or similar are desirable. Please see job description for full list of requirements What we can offer you: You will have the opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution. Imperial College is currently ranked #2 in the QS world university rankings with the Department of Computing ranked top of the 2021 UK REF assessment. You will receive a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year) and a comprehensive early career development support package including 10 training and development days. Further information: *Candidates who have not been awarded a PhD you will be appointed at Research Assistant level within the salary range: £45,399 - £48,876 per annum Full-time, Fixed term to start as soon as possible for 36 months In addition to completing the online application candidates should attach: A full CV, highlighting publications or preprints relevant to the position A 1-page research statement on how your experience meets the above requirements. If you require any further details on the role, please contact Dr Zhenyu Cai: z.cai1@imperial.ac.uk For queries regarding the application process contact Jamie Perrins: j.perrins@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29638": {
      "title": "Early Years Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £38,608 - £41,666 per annum; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01462 About the role: Are you a dynamic and proactive organised individual? Do you enjoy each day being different from the other? Do you enjoy being hands on active and working with people? If so, we currently have a full-time position for a Departmental Administrator. We are a busy 210 place Early Years Education Centre with an outstanding Ofsted rating and reputation, and we serve the community of Imperial College London caring for the staff and student’s children. We are looking to recruit a dynamic administrator with a can-do attitude to support our Monday to Friday day to day operations and work in our busy centre. Due to the nature of the role and environment, working will be onsite only. Not an ordinary administrative role, but an exciting role that requires multi skills with new learning opportunities to develop new skills. You will be the first point of contact with existing and prospective families. You will liaise and support the Early Years Team and Management and working with external personnel and internal departments to deliver an outstanding service. This is an active role that is multi-faceted with no two days the same. You will have excellent communication, time management, organisational and interpersonal skills. You will have the ability to record accurate minutes, use Microsoft packages, collect and input data as well as more practical tasks such as centre tours and supporting/ coordinating required and urgent defects and pre-planned maintenance and supporting the wider Team. What you would be doing: Work with and support all aspects of the EYEC Administration Office work and associated Team Liaise with the Admin Team and EYEC Team to support recruitment Set up of new staff on ICT systems, including email and printers Ensure the prompt payments of casual worker hours and overtime payments to staff Regular and ongoing training with EYEC staff on Microsoft, email, printing and photocopying Keeping an internal log of relevant details and update regularly and as required Creating and maintaining the office procedures manual Maintain and update EYEC records and create databases to streamline administrative systems, by inputting of records on computer system Reception/telephone duties: provide an information service for personal callers and telephone enquiries Preparation of correspondence and reports as required and improve standard letters and forms Calculating and comparing costs for required goods or services to achieve maximum value for money Assist the Head of Centre regarding the preparation of annual budget figures What we are looking for: Proven previous administrative and/or a relevant vocational qualification Communicate effectively with a wide range of staff and external visitors and have excellent spoken and written knowledge of the English language Experience of using data bases to store, manipulate and retrieve information A high level of accuracy and consistent attention to detail Excellent organisational and time management skills and be able to plan and prioritise tasks effectively Ability to work and communicate effectively with a wide range of internal personnel and external visitors from various disciplines and backgrounds Sound and accurate IT skills (Word, Outlook & Excel) Respect for confidentiality, including the integrity and sensitivity of records and awareness of data protection guidelines To work collaboratively towards the aims of the Centre and its policies and procedures Ability to work flexibly, cooperatively, and independently using initiative Ability to demonstrate a knowledge and understanding of safeguarding children Ability to learn new skills quickly Flexibility and capacity to adapt to the changing demands of the job and ability to learn new skills quickly Reporting to the Departmental Finance Administrator, you will liaise with the Head of Centre and senior Management Team on all relevant administrative matters affecting the Centre. You will work closely with the Team in a busy environment and be an effective and committed team member with a positive approach to change and delivery of an outstanding service. The role will require full-time working on site and the hours of work are 35 hours within 08:45-17:15, Monday to Friday. Safeguarding children is our priority, and all post holders within the EYEC are required to adhere to the EYEC’s Safeguarding children and Whistle Blowing Policy. All post holders will be required to obtain a satisfactory enhanced level Disclosure and Barring check. What we can offer you: At Imperial, our values—respect, collaboration, excellence, integrity, and innovation—guide everything we do. You’ll be part of a supportive and forward-thinking team, working on projects that directly contribute to the safety and wellbeing of our community. We are committed to creating an inclusive working environment and welcome applications from all backgrounds. We particularly encourage interest from individuals who are underrepresented in our workforce. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and Imperial closures, and a generous pension scheme). Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: If you require any further details on the role please contact: Tracy Halsey, Head of EYEC – t.halsey@imperial.ac.uk Closing date: at Midnight on Sunday, 8 November 2026 Please note that if we receive a high volume of applications the advert will be withdrawn prior to the closing date."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29633": {
      "title": "Faculty Admissions Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Academic Services; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; Marketing, Outreach, Recruitment and Admissions; Professional / Support Services Reference: ACA00543 About the role: The Admissions service within Marketing, Outreach, Recruitment and Admissions (MORA) are seeing an Admissions officer to join the division’s well established central admissions team. We are seeking an individual with demonstrable experience of working in an administrative role, where you have been responsible for progressing work in line with established policies and procedures both independently and as part of a wider team. You will have experience of working in a deadline-driven environment, with the ability to manage their own workload effectively and efficiently, whilst understanding the coordination of their activities and contributions in the context of a wider team. You will have the ability to work in a regulatory and policy environment and to deliver business processes and assessments in accordance with relevant frameworks and to deadlines. What you would be doing: You will build and foster effective working relationships with key departments within your Faculty/School. You will ensure that you provide an excellent customer service to applicants and key external stakeholders in delivering the admissions officer activities. You will develop an expertise in admissions processes, policies and procedures that enable you to advise and guide stakeholders/customers though the admissions processes. You will develop an expertise in the admissions process, requirements and policies that enable you to effectively manage record progression and manage stakeholder interactions and expectations. What we are looking for: Experience of working in an administrative role, with the ability to work independently, managing your own workload as well as demonstrable experience as working well within a team. Experience of using databases, progressing records in line with policy, and possessing an understanding and appreciation for the sensitivity and confidentiality of records. Appropriate professional experience and business acumen which informs an appreciation of the importance of the effective delivery of business processes in a deadline-driven environment in the context of competing deadlines within the duties and responsibilities of the role. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time fixed term post for 14 months. If you require further information about the role please contact: David Gibbon at d.gibbon@imperial .ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29625": {
      "title": "Head of Learner Marketing and Recruitment",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Department: Academic Services; £71,793- £82,031 per annum; Lifelong Learning; Professional / Support Services Reference: ACA00538 About the role: Do you know how to build marketing functions that deliver measurable growth? Are you passionate about creating exceptional learner journeys, leading high-performing teams and turning strategy into results? Imperial Lifelong Learning is seeking an experienced Head of Learner Marketing and Recruitment to lead the delivery of marketing and learner recruitment activity across a rapidly growing portfolio of lifelong learning programmes. Reporting to the Director of Learner Marketing and Recruitment, you will be responsible for translating strategic priorities into impactful marketing campaigns, recruitment activity and conversion strategies that drive learner growth. You will lead a team of senior marketing professionals, oversee performance across the full learner recruitment funnel, and play a key role in scaling the marketing capability required to support Imperial's ambitious growth plans. This role is ideal for a commercially minded marketing leader who combines strong operational expertise with a passion for learner engagement, data-driven decision-making and continuous improvement. You will help shape how prospective learners discover, engage with and enrol onto Imperial's world-class lifelong learning programmes, ensuring every interaction delivers value and impact. What you would be doing: Translate strategic priorities into integrated marketing and recruitment plans that drive awareness, lead generation, application growth and enrolment. Oversee performance across the full learner journey, identifying opportunities to improve engagement, nurture, conversion and learner experience. Lead multi-channel marketing activity across digital marketing, content, CRM, social media, websites, PR, partnerships and events. Establish robust campaign planning, reporting and performance management frameworks that enable effective investment decisions and continuous optimisation. Champion a data-guided approach to marketing, embedding insight, audience intelligence and performance analytics into decision-making across the function. Drive the adoption of marketing technology, automation and AI-enabled tools that improve learner engagement, campaign effectiveness and operational efficiency. Develop scalable marketing processes, governance and ways of working that support sustainable growth across the portfolio. Lead, develop and inspire a team of senior marketing managers, creating a culture of accountability, collaboration, innovation and continuous improvement. Contribute as a senior leader within Lifelong Learning, supporting organisational projects What we are looking for: Significant experience leading marketing, recruitment, demand generation, customer acquisition or growth functions. Proven success delivering marketing strategies and campaigns that generate qualified leads, improve conversion and deliver measurable growth. Experience leading and developing high-performing teams within complex and fast-paced environments. Strong understanding of lead generation, digital marketing, CRM, customer journeys, performance marketing and marketing analytics. Experience using data, insight and audience intelligence to drive decision-making and optimise marketing performance. Demonstrable experience managing marketing budgets, priorities and resources to maximise impact and return on investment. Excellent stakeholder management and influencing skills, with the ability to build strong relationships across a diverse range of teams and seniority levels. Experience implementing new technologies, systems, processes or operating models that improve marketing capability, scalability and effectiveness. A customer-centric approach with a passion for creating exceptional learner experiences and delivering commercial outcomes. Desirable Experience in learner marketing, learner recruitment, and growth marketing. Experience working within EdTech environments, with an understanding of learner acquisition, conversion and engagement across the customer lifecycle. Understanding of skills transformation, professional learning or lifelong learning markets. Experience engaging employer, corporate and professional audiences through marketing activities. What we can offer you: Join an ambitious and rapidly growing Lifelong Learning department that is redefining how world-class education is delivered and experienced. The opportunity to shape and scale a high-performing marketing and recruitment function at one of the world's leading universities. A role with significant influence, autonomy and scope for innovation. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days' annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: At Imperial, we believe knowledge creates the greatest impact when it reaches the people who can use it. Imperial Lifelong Learning is building a world-class portfolio of educational experiences that help learners develop new capabilities, advance their careers and respond to an increasingly complex and fast-changing world. Through professional education, executive programmes, digital learning, summer schools and lifelong learning opportunities, we are creating new routes for learners to access Imperial's expertise and transform their potential. This is a full-time, open-ended role based at the South Kensington Campus."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29637": {
      "title": "Patient, Public Involvement and Engagement (PPIE) Facilitator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; School of Public Health; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06024 About the role: We are looking for an enthusiastic and highly organised Patient and Public Involvement and Engagement (PPIE) Facilitator to join the Neonatal Medicine Research Group and support PPIE activities for the COLLABORATE study. COLLABORATE is a five-year study exploring the best feeding methods for babies born very preterm (before 29 weeks of gestation). Working in partnership with people with lived experience of preterm birth or necrotising enterocolitis is an essential part of our research. You will work closely with the PPIE Lead to provide high-quality administrative and organisational support for the planning, coordination and delivery of PPIE activities, helping to ensure that parents, former patients and other public contributors are meaningfully involved in our research. What you would be doing: As PPIE Facilitator, you will support the planning and delivery of online and in-person PPIE activities, including meetings, workshops and focus groups. You will help coordinate venues, materials and communications, liaise with public contributors and support the facilitation and evaluation of activities. You will also administer PPIE expense claims, maintain accurate records, support the monitoring of PPIE activities and contribute to communications and dissemination, including blogs, newsletters and social media. Working with the PPIE Lead, you will help ensure that PPIE activities are inclusive, accessible and effectively communicated across the COLLABORATE study. What we are looking for: What we are looking for : A level of education to degree level or equivalent experience. Experience of providing administrative support in a busy working environment. Excellent interpersonal and written and verbal communication skills. Excellent organisational and time-management skills, with the ability to work to deadlines. Strong attention to detail and a well-structured approach to administration. The ability to use initiative, work independently and take a proactive approach. The ability to build effective working relationships with people across different organisations, disciplines and professions. Knowledge of current guidelines and standards for public involvement and engagement. A commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion and to ensuring that people from a broad range of backgrounds are able to contribute meaningfully. Experience of participatory research methods, organising focus groups, working within the NHS or higher education, and using survey platforms such as Qualtrics would be advantageous. What we can offer you: You will join the Neonatal Medicine Research Group within Imperial College London's School of Public Health and have the opportunity to contribute to research that places patients and the public at the heart of how studies are designed and delivered. A supportive and collaborative team environment. The opportunity to make a meaningful contribution by helping people with lived experience shape research. Opportunities for professional development in patient and public involvement and engagement. A sector-leading salary and remuneration package, including 41 days' annual leave and generous pension schemes. Access to Imperial's flexible working policy, family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. A diverse and inclusive working environment with staff networks and resources supporting personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: This is a part-time position at 0.2 FTE (equivalent to one day per week), fixed term for 12 months in the first instance. While the total hours are flexible, there is a preference for them to be worked across multiple days. The expected start date is January 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. You will be part of the Neonatal Medicine Research Group and will work closely with the Patient Experience Research Centre (PERC) at Imperial. Occasional out-of-hours working may be required to support PPIE events. For further information about the role, please contact Annemarie Lodder at a.lodder@imperial.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29628": {
      "title": "Research Associate in RF Design and Prototyping of Reconfigurable Intelligent Surfaces",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04070 About the role: The post is funded by UKRI Hub in All-Spectrum Connectivity (HASC) phase II to conduct original research in RF design, modeling, and prototyping of reconfigurable intelligent surfaces. The successful candidate will design and prototype “ beyond-diagonal reconfigurable intelligent surfaces ” (BD-RIS) using reconfigurable impedance networks and/or nonlocal metasurfaces, identify the full potential benefits of BD-RIS for next generation wireless networks, and assess the feasibility of BD-RIS. This full-time, in-person postdoctoral position is based at Imperial College’s South Kensington Campus in London, UK, and is funded till 30 September 2028 with possibility to extend till 31 Mar 2030 (after passing project mid-term review). In your application, clearly explain your expertise and your approach to design and fabricate reconfigurable impedance network to enable BD-RIS. What you would be doing: Main Duties: To take initiatives in the planning of research To identify and develop suitable techniques, and apparatus, for the collection and analysis of data To conduct data analysis To ensure the validity and reliability of data at all times To maintain accurate and complete records of all findings To write reports for submission to research sponsors To present findings to colleagues and at conferences To submit publications to refereed journals To provide guidance to staff and students To attend relevant workshops and conferences as necessary What we are looking for: Education Research Associate : Hold a PhD (or be very near completion, or equivalent) in RF design, modelling, and prototyping of radio systems, reconfigurable intelligent surfaces, and/or metasurfaces, and a significant publication track record. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. Experience Practical experience within a research environment and / or publication in relevant and refereed journals Practical experience in a broad range of techniques including Microwave circuit theory Antenna design/measurement RF system prototyping/experimentation Reconfigurable RF and microwave devices and microwave networks Reconfigurable intelligent surfaces and/or metasurfaces Experience presenting and publishing scientific and/or technical research What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: Please note that job descriptions cannot be exhaustive, and the post-holder may be required to undertake other duties, which are broadly in line with the above key responsibilities. For any specific queries regarding the post please contact Prof Bruno Clerckx (b.clerckx@imperial.ac.uk)"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29631": {
      "title": "Research Group Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; National Heart & Lung Institute; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06037 About the role: Are you an excellent administrator, looking for the next step in your career as a Research Group Administrator? This is a key role in supporting our Academics, researchers and their work within the National Heart and Lung Institute (NHLI). What you would be doing: This is a dynamic role, providing administrative support to the Section events and activities with a wide range of duties: You will be the first point of contact, welcoming visitors and promoting a positive image of NHLI and the college. You will manage diaries of our Head of Division and Sections and organise their travel. You will Act as Secretary for Section meetings and committees including drafting agendas, papers and minutes with the committee Chairs. Coordinate subcommittees and working groups as required. You will assist in the entering and maintenance of electronic/online information such as professional activities, CVs, publication lists, membership registration etc, ensuring proper record keeping You will be familiar with document referencing using software such as Symplectic What we are looking for: You will be educated to degree level or have equivalent experience with an open and flexible approach to your day-to-day work. Great communication skills both verbal and written, and the ability to deal with staff of all levels as well as students. You will have experience of organising seminars, interviews, conferences and workshops. Previous experience of working in a higher education office environment, ideally in a research setting. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a fixed-term, part-time post for 2 days per week for 12 months. If you require any further details about the role, please contact Matthew Smith, matthew.smith@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29640": {
      "title": "Senior Research Security Advisor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Provost’s Team; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Research Office; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRO01454 About the role: International collaboration forms a major part of Imperial College London’s research, innovation and education portfolio, and with such internationalisation we need to ensure that this activity is carried out with the highest degree of integrity and in line with legislation and applicable international regimes. The post of Senior Research Security Advisor will support the continued development and implementation of policy, process, and practice to underpin Imperial’s capabilities in taking a responsible and informed approach to research security – including compliance with export and trade control legislation and connected research security requirements and considerations. As a member of the University’s central Research Office, the role holder’s primary objective will be to promote and facilitate a security-conscious approach to research that is sensitive to the global context and protective of both the University’s academic independence and integrity. What you would be doing: You will: Help to raise awareness regarding research security considerations and requirements by co-delivering a programme of outreach and education Help to develop capability at individual, organisation unit and institutional levels in response to a dynamic research security environment within a global top ten university Develop and maintain an in-depth understanding of the university context, including as relates to international research, innovation, entrepreneurship and teaching opportunities Analyse and interpret how the regulatory frameworks intersect with core university activities; fundamental, applied and emerging science and technology; geopolitical change and academic priorities Provide expert support and advice to colleagues in identifying and managing research security risks in their interactions Represent Imperial in engagements with peer institutions and other external stakeholders both domestically and internationally Collaborate effectively with the Head of Research Security, other team members and the wider Research Office community in supporting research excellence, integrity and security What we are looking for: You should demonstrate a sound knowledge and understanding of the research security environment, including export controls and other core considerations underpinning relevant UK legislation. It is also preferable, but not essential, that the candidate should have gained previous experience of working in the HEI sector and be educated to degree level (or equivalent). Candidates must possess excellent communication skills, both written and oral, being able to work both collaboratively and independently, with a high degree of integrity on complex and sometimes sensitive matters. You will have the ability to contribute to multi-faceted process and policy matters in addition to offering accurate technical and regulatory advice in specific cases. What we can offer you: The opportunity to develop genuine leadership and expertise in the field of Research security. To become an ambassador for Trusted (Secure) Research Principles. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Mr. Diran Solanke, d.solanke@imperial.ac.uk. Appointments will be made at the starting point of the salary scale: PTOL-London-August-2026.pdf"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29613": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Institute of Clinical Sciences; Researcher Reference: MED06005 About the role: We are looking to appoint a Research Associate with a major interest and expertise in mass spectrometry and its clinical and translational applications. Research in the Steroid and Metabolism Group, headed by Prof. Wiebke Arlt, focuses on understanding the role of steroid metabolism and function on human physiology and health. We use mass spectrometry applications, mainly liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry, to assess the steroid metabolome in different matrices (serum, saliva, urine) derived from human cohorts and in vivo physiology studies as well as cell culture supernatant from human in vivo studies as well as human-based ex vivo and in vitro model systems. What you would be doing: The post holder will be a member of the Steroids & Metabolism group and will enjoy a high level of scientific responsibility. They will work in a focused and organised manner to produce independent and original research in collaboration with other members of the group and with internal and external collaborators. The post holder will have a key role in delivering the scientific goals of our NIHR-funded ACCelerate project https://fundingawards.nihr.ac.uk/award/NIHR503467 They will also support the supervision of postgraduate students in the group. The post holder will contribute to by preparing and delivering conference presentations, submission of original research manuscripts and review articles to refereed journals, and contribute to grant proposals. What we are looking for: PhD in analytical chemistry. First author publication(s) in a respected journal. Extensive experience in the preparation of biological and chemical samples for LC-MS, HPLC/UHPLC and solution-state NMR analyses, including method development, validation and data quantitation and analysis. Detailed troubleshooting knowledge for the Waters TQ/XS tandem mass spectrometry platform. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: Please note that this is a PhD level role but candidates who have not yet been officially awarded will be appointed as a Research Assistant . This is a full-time, fixed term post for 3 years. The expected start date is around 1 st November 2026."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29615": {
      "title": "Research Associate in RF Design and Prototyping of Microwave Analog Computing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Electrical and Electronic Engineering; Academic & Teaching Reference: ENG04071 About the role: The post is funded by UKRI TITAN phase II to conduct original research in RF design, modeling, and prototyping of microwave analog computing. The successful candidate will design and prototype Microwave Linear Analog Computer (MiLAC) using non-reconfigurable and reconfigurable microwave networks, identify the full potential benefits of MiLAC for next generation wireless networks, and assess the feasibility of MiLAC. This full-time, in-person postdoctoral position is based at Imperial College’s South Kensington Campus in London, UK, and is funded till 30 September 2028 with possibility to extend till 31 Mar 2030 (after passing project mid-term review). In your application, clearly explain your expertise and your approach to design and fabricate reconfigurable microwave network to enable MiLAC and perform matrix inversion in the analog domain. What you would be doing: Main Duties: To take initiatives in the planning of research To identify and develop suitable techniques, and apparatus, for the collection and analysis of data To conduct data analysis To ensure the validity and reliability of data at all times To maintain accurate and complete records of all findings To write reports for submission to research sponsors To present findings to colleagues and at conferences To submit publications to refereed journals To provide guidance to staff and students To attend relevant workshops and conferences as necessary To develop contacts and research collaborations within the College and the wider community To promote the reputation of the Group, the Department and the College To provide guidance to PhD Students Contribute to bids for research grants To conduct and plan own scientific work with appropriate supervision. To maintain highly organised and accurate record of experimental work To actively participate in the research programme of the Group What we are looking for: Education Research Associate: Hold a PhD (or be very near completion, or equivalent) in RF design, modelling, and prototyping of radio systems, reconfigurable intelligent surfaces, and/or metasurfaces, reconfigurable microwave networks, microwave computing, and a significant publication track record. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant. Experience Practical experience within a research environment and / or publication in relevant and refereed journals Practical experience in a broad range of techniques including Microwave circuit theory Antenna design/measurement RF system prototyping/experimentation Reconfigurable RF and microwave devices and microwave networks Reconfigurable intelligent surfaces and/or metasurfaces and/or microwave computing Experience presenting and publishing scientific and/or technical research Practical experience within a research environment and / or publication in relevant and refereed journals Knowledge of microwave circuit theory, antenna design/measurement, RF system prototyping/experimentation, reconfigurable RF and microwave devices and microwave networks, reconfigurable intelligent surfaces and/or metasurfaces and/or microwave computing What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: Please note that job descriptions cannot be exhaustive, and the post-holder may be required to undertake other duties, which are broadly in line with the above key responsibilities. For any specific queries regarding the post please contact Prof Bruno Clerckx (b.clerckx@imperial.ac.uk)"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29618": {
      "title": "Research Associate INDIGO",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Researcher Reference: MED06031 About the role: We are looking to recruit a skilled, reliable, and enthusiastic Research Associate who will be funded by Macmillan Cancer Care to work on the analysis of data from the INDIGO research trial (NCT06095024)). INDIGO is the largest ever study of cancer survivors, and has recruited 70 000 patients nationally. This work will build on our initial work and analysis, and provide the opportunity to apply cutting-edge computational tools to a large, real-world clinical dataset. The aim is to both deliver analyses and use those analyses to inform the development of simple interventions with the scope to improve the quality of life for cancer survivors at scale across the UK. What you would be doing: The postholder will carry out work based around the analysis, interpretation and publication of multiple cohorts from the INDIGO dataset, and to develop new methods for clustering and analysing free-text data, including linked national cancer data. They will also be involved in using that information to plan the next phase of trials, including working with other staff in the group. What we are looking for: You should hold a PhD in Data Science or a related discipline, with strong and significant experience of analysing tabular clinical data. You should have a good knowledge of common statistical approaches and software packages, and be willing to explore novel and developing approaches to deliver more refined insights into the data. You must be organised, highly self-motivated, have excellent communication skills and relish working towards milestones. What we can offer you: The Division of Cancer at Imperial College London offers a dynamic and supportive environment for new Research Associates, providing opportunities to engage in cutting-edge research. The Computational Oncology Group is a translational research group that uses computational and mathematical approaches to improve patient care. We have a strong track-record in clinical implementation, and this project offers the opportunity to continue this. New Research Associates will benefit from access to state-of-the-art facilities and the chance to collaborate with leading academics, clinicians, and commercial partners. This vibrant research community is dedicated to advancing cancer prevention, diagnosis, and treatment through multidisciplinary collaborations, fostering an outstanding environment for both academic growth and impactful research contributions Further information: This is a parttime, fixed term contract based at Charing Cross Hospital. For informal discussions about the post, please contact Dr. Matt Williams at email matthew.williams@imperial.ac.uk *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29607": {
      "title": "AI Solution Architect",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £71,793- £82,031 per annum; Information & Communication Technologies; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01458 About the role: Are you an architect who can see both the big picture and the detail of how artificial intelligence should be designed, governed and adopted, and would like to shape how a world-leading university puts AI to work safely and well? As AI Solution Architect, you will act as a cross-cutting design authority for AI across all of ICT and more widely across Imperial, shaping how generative AI, machine learning and data-driven capabilities are designed into our products and platforms. Working within Architecture & Technology Operations, you will partner with product and platform teams, engineering, data and research colleagues to design secure, scalable and responsible AI solutions aligned to Imperial’s Digital Strategy, enterprise architecture standards and our commitment to responsible AI, data governance and ethics. What you would be doing: Lead AI solution architecture: Lead the development of AI and data solution architectures across ICT product lines and Imperial, aligned to enterprise architecture principles, technology standards and the Digital Strategy. Design end-to-end AI solutions: Design AI solutions spanning generative AI and large language models, machine learning, and the data pipelines and MLOps needed to deploy and operate them responsibly and at scale. Set guardrails for AI agents: Define reference architectures and guardrails for AI agents, including tool access, permissions, autonomy levels and human oversight. Champion responsible AI: Ensure solutions meet responsible AI, data governance, security and regulatory obligations, and uphold cybersecurity, data protection and technology standards. Lead in governance: Contribute to the Technical Design Authority, technology working groups and communities of practice, reviewing and assuring solution designs across the ICT portfolio. Provide technical leadership: Guide delivery teams to implement approved designs and champion modern engineering practices, including API-first, cloud-native, DevOps, platform-based architecture and MLOps. Be the AI design authority: Act as a cross-cutting AI design authority, advising and enabling product lines across ICT and teams across Imperial on the responsible adoption and integration of AI. Support research: Support research groups adopting AI tools and platforms, balancing academic freedom with security and data obligations. What we are looking for: Experience designing complex digital or enterprise solutions within large, diverse or federated organisations Experience designing and delivering AI, machine learning or data-driven solutions, and integrating AI capabilities across multiple systems, product lines or domains A strong understanding of AI and machine learning concepts, including generative AI and large language models, and how they are applied in enterprise solutions Understanding of the data architecture, pipelines and MLOps needed to build, deploy and operate AI solutions, and of AI and ML platforms, tooling and services (cloud AI/ML services, LLM frameworks and vector data stores) The ability to design responsible, secure and ethical AI solutions, embedding guardrails, evaluation and human oversight, and to assess and apply AI responsibly, weighing value, risk, ethics, security and compliance A strong grasp of modern architecture approaches, including cloud, integration platforms, APIs, microservices and secure-by-design, across multiple technology domains Experience across the full product or platform lifecycle, from discovery and design through to live service and decommissioning Excellent communication skills, with the ability to engage audiences from senior stakeholders through to technical teams and suppliers, and to advise and enable teams on responsible AI adoption Desirable: awareness of AI-specific security and risk and of emerging AI regulation, experience of architecture governance or design assurance, and experience in the Higher Education sector What we can offer you: The opportunity to shape how AI is designed, governed and adopted across a world-leading university A central, cross-cutting role in Architecture & Technology Operations, with influence over enterprise architecture standards and strategic technology decisions The chance to work at the forefront of generative AI, machine learning and MLOps in a complex research and education environment A supportive and collaborative environment focused on learning, knowledge sharing and professional development Flexible and hybrid working arrangements Access to Imperial’s wider staff benefits and development opportunities The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 4 3 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes) . Further information: This is a full-time, open-ended position."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29599": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor in Particle Physics",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; Competitive; Department of Physics; Academic & Teaching Reference: NAT02311 About the role: The Department of Physics at Imperial College London seeks an outstanding experimental physicist to join us as an Assistant Professor or Associate Professor, and to build a leading research programme focused on searches for hidden-sector particles using accelerator experiments at the intensity frontier. Imperial participates in several current and future fixed-target experiments searching for feebly interacting particles. Imperial physicists played a leading role in proposing the SHiP (Search for Hidden Particles) experiment at CERN, and have helped drive the experiment from its conception, through CERN approval, and into its current design phase. SHiP will also pursue a distinctive neutrino programme, including studies of tau neutrinos. CERN approved the experiment in 2024, and it is now progressing through detailed design towards construction and long-term operation. Imperial also participates in several other experiments using high-intensity neutrino beams to constrain dark-sector models and search for feebly interacting particles, including T2K, SBN, and DUNE. The particle physics group also has core responsibilities in direct dark matter detection experiments (LZ and XLZD), and on the LHCb experiment. This post offers a unique opportunity to play a leading role in the design and construction of the SHiP experiment, with access to externally funded resources to support the experimental programme. While this will be a primary focus of the post, the successful candidate will also have the opportunity to contribute to a broader research programme in related areas and to the Department’s world-leading education provision. What you would be doing: As a member of our academic faculty, you will: Develop and lead an internationally excellent research programme in experimental particle physics, centred on hidden-sector searches and the SHiP experiment. Contribute to teaching and curriculum development at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Supervise and mentor PhD students and junior researchers, and contribute to doctoral training initiatives. Secure external research funding and build productive collaborations within and beyond Imperial. Play an active role in the life and strategic development of the Department and the wider Faculty. Contribute to the SHiP experiment through an appropriate combination of detector and instrumentation development, simulation, software, reconstruction, data analysis and physics interpretation. Contribute to a broader research programme in hidden-sector searches and related areas. Depending on your level of appointment, you may also: Contribute to departmental leadership and management. What we are looking for: We are seeking an outstanding academic who is committed to excellence in research, teaching, and contribution to the academic community. You will be able to demonstrate: A PhD in Physics or in a closely related field, or equivalent. A strong record in experimental particle physics, with publications and contributions of outstanding quality appropriate to your career stage. Experience of research within a major international particle physics collaboration, at a collider, fixed-target, beam-dump or neutrino experiment. Relevant expertise in at least one of detector or instrumentation development, experimental software or computing, event reconstruction, data analysis or physics interpretation. A clear plan for an internationally leading the SHiP experiment programme, and how it would be integrated in a broader research programme. The potential to collaborate across the Physics of Particles Community and the wider Department. Career-stage adequate experience with funding applications. Commitment to high-quality teaching and supervision of students. Excellent communication, collaboration, and leadership skills appropriate to your career stage. At the Assistant Professor level, you will have clear potential to develop an international reputation in intensity-frontier experimental particle physics. At the Associate Professor level, you will have an established profile and international recognition in intensity-frontier experimental particle physics, independent scientific leadership including leading a team of researchers, and sustained success in securing external research funding. It would also be desirable for you to have: Experience of detector hardware development, prototyping or construction. Experience in another experimental programme whose methods and technologies transfer readily to the SHiP experiment. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Access to state-of-the-art facilities and to Imperial’s long-established programme at CERN, together with significant computing resources through Imperial’s Research Computing Service. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (43 days off a year including public holidays and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further Information: Assistant Professor: £73,194 to £82,954 per annum Associate Professor: Minimum starting salary of £85,873 per annum This is an open-ended, full-time post (35 hours per week). The starting date will be agreed with the successful candidate. The Department of Physics is part of the Faculty of Natural Sciences at Imperial College London. We are an internationally renowned Physics department, which enables us to create groups of international standing, and with sufficient scale and critical mass, that they influence the research agenda and attract the world’s top researchers. The Department is structured into five Research Communities that drive new research directions: Light, Matter, Physics of Particles, Physics of the Universe, and Space, Plasma and Climate. The post will sit within the Department's Physics of Particles Community, which investigates particle interactions at the smallest scales to discover new particles, forces and phenomena, advancing our understanding of the Universe. This research drives technological innovation in accelerators, detectors, data handling, analysis and machine learning. Imperial College is committed to equality of opportunity, eliminating discrimination and fostering an inclusive workplace culture. The Department of Physics is also proud to hold an Athena SWAN Silver Award, recognising our commitment to fostering a supportive, inclusive, and thriving community. We value the strength that comes from diverse perspectives, and we warmly welcome applications from talented people of all backgrounds, and particularly those underrepresented in physics, to join our vibrant community supported by initiatives that promote collaboration, inclusivity, and professional development. Find out more about our inclusive environment. If you would like more information or have specific queries about the role, please contact: Professor Mitesh Patel, Head of the Physics of Particles Community – mitesh.patel@imperial.ac.uk, or Professor Stefan Söldner-Rembold, Head of the Department of Physics - stefan.soldner-rembold@imperial.ac.uk If you have any questions about the application process, please contact: the Physics Knowledge Hub – physicsconnect@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29603": {
      "title": "Development Analyst",
      "description": "Closing Date: 28/10/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; White City Development; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01459 About the role: This role is within the White City Development Team who are responsible for delivering a significant and exciting programme of development projects at Imperial’s newest campus. The campus extends across 22 acres with the first phase of development largely complete and operational. Imperial has outline planning consent for a c.2.7m sq. ft mixed-use masterplan for the second phase of development that it intends to bring forward, together with partners, over the next decade. Further asset management and development activities are progressing in North Acton and Old Oak as part of Imperial’s WestTech Corridor strategy. Imperial will progress with major mixed-use development in this location over the next two decades. The Analyst role will encompass strategy, asset management, transactions, planning & development. They will form a key part of a small team and have a varied and changing set of day-to-day development, financial and project management responsibilities. What you would be doing: The role will be to assist with the development of new buildings and to provide strategic input into existing property activities including asset management. Analysts will be allocated to oversee and support the day-to-day implementation of new and existing property activities, including major regeneration projects, working under direct instruction from the project lead. Analysts will have a level of delegated authority as determined on a case-by-case basis for the lead. Analyst will also be required to consult and engage with internal and external stakeholders including local community groups, local authorities, property consultants and legal advisors. Analysts will be expected to participate in other property work across Imperial’s portfolio and will have the opportunity to undertake training including property and/or investment related qualifications (not required). What we are looking for: We are seeking a candidate with strong research and analytical skills who can generate high-level reports and interpret complex financial and commercial data. The ideal candidate be able to identify value drivers and issues, in a complex environment, and managing multiple projects concurrently. Excellent organizational skills, proven experience writing concise reports, presentations and proposals, and advanced proficiency in Microsoft Excel / PowerPoint are essential. Flexibility in work location and hours to meet project deadlines is also required. Candidates should demonstrate robust project management abilities, including a track record of delivering on time and within budget. Experience contributing at a senior level and representing the organization to external stakeholders is valued, along with a sound understanding of commercial issues in a higher education setting. Prior experience as an analyst is preferred, but a Property Development or Construction background is not necessary. Most important will be a willingness to adapt and learn in a driven, dynamic environment. What we can offer you: Mentoring and up-skilling related to understanding and interest in the UK real estate sector, including financial, commercial and Sustainability performance The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This recruitment campaign is being managed by Higher Capital Consulting on behalf of Imperial College London. Please submit applications directly to Harry at Higher Capital Consulting - harry@highercapital.co.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29610": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £45,399 - £48,876 per annum; Department of Immunology and Inflammation; Researcher Reference: MED06035 About the role: We are looking for a talented Research Assistant to work on an exciting project exploring the underlying cause of the hepatotoxicity induced by an antibody drug conjugate Inotuzumab Ozogamicin, used to treat leukaemia. You will be working in Associate Professor Richard Burt’s laboratory, on a project exploring the hypothesis that Inotuzumab Ozogamicin causes hepatotoxicity through endothelial cell activation and damage. The post is funded by the Medical Research Foundation. What you would be doing: You will work closely with a post-doctoral research associate and be trained in techniques including endothelial cell culture, ‘mini-liver’ culture models, flow cytometry, single cell and bulk RNA Sequencing and transgenic mouse models of leukaemia. What we are looking for: You should have a first/masters degree (or equivalent) in Biology, or a related discipline. You should have experience of working in a laboratory research environment and conducting laboratory techniques, including multi colour flow cytometry and experience in sequencing methods. Prior experience of animal work is desirable, but not essential. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time, fixed-term post until 15 July 2029, with potential for an extension to November 2029 dependent on funding. If you have any further details about the role, please contact Dr Richard Burt (r.burt@imperial.ac.uk) or Dr Penny Sparkes (p.sparkes@imperial.ac.uk)."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29600": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 23/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Bioengineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04046 About the role: Are you a protein engineer or tumour immunologist who wants to see your molecules move towards patients with advanced prostate cancer? We are looking for a Research Associate to lead a Prostate Cancer UK-funded programme combining a T cell engager with another immunotherapy in metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer (mCRPC). T cell engagers can promote mCRPC regression, but responses are rarely durable: CD3 engagement delivers signal 1 without the co-stimulation that T cells need to persist. To overcome this challenge, combination strategies with inflammatory molecules will be tested alongside the engineering of novel design to trigger a complete and robust T cell activation. Any design and ideas will have to be explored thoroughly through rigorous validation of efficacy, mechanism and tolerability with the aim to bring this project to clinical translation with a clinical development partner. What you would be doing: You will take day-to-day scientific ownership of the programme, working with a PhD student and a research technician, and our clinical and academic collaborators. You wil: Design, express and purify bispecific antibody and protein fusion constructs in mammalian expression systems, and characterise them by SEC, SPR/BLI, DSF and functional binding assays. Run primary human T cell and NK cell assays — cytotoxicity, activation, exhaustion phenotyping, polyfunctionality and repeat-stimulation models. Deliver in vivo efficacy, biodistribution and tolerability studies in syngeneic and humanised mouse models of prostate cancer, including dose-sparing and dosing-interval regimens and pre-specified vascular and drug safety endpoints. Analyse tumour microenvironment changes by flow cytometry, immunofluorescence and spatial transcriptomic profiling, and integrate the results into a coherent mechanistic picture. Help run a collaborative laboratory: supervise and support students, keep rigorous records, and contribute to the smooth operation of shared facilities. What we are looking for: A PhD (or close to completion) in immunology, protein engineering, bioengineering, cancer biology or a closely related discipline. Hands-on experience of recombinant protein expression and purification, and of biophysical or biochemical characterisation of the proteins you make. Experience of primary immune cell assays and/or in vivo tumour models; a UK Home Office personal licence, or willingness to obtain one, is an advantage. A strong publication record relative to career stage, and evidence that you can drive a project rather than only execute one. The judgement to design experiments that answer a question — including the ones that could show the hypothesis is wrong — and the rigour to interpret them honestly. Excellent written and spoken communication, and the collaborative instincts to work well with students, clinicians and industrial partners. What we can offer you: A funded, well-defined three-year programme with a clear translational endpoint, in a group whose previous matrix-targeting work has moved from the bench into clinical development. An established collaborative network across clinical oncology, structural biology and industry, and active support in applying for your own fellowship funding. A purpose-built research environment at the White City Campus, with access to Imperial’s protein production, imaging, flow cytometry, single-cell and in vivo facilities. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: This is a full-time post (35 hours per week) fixed-term contract for up to 24 months, funded by Prostate Cancer UK. The expected start date is [1 January 2027], or as soon as possible thereafter. *please note, if you are yet to receive your PhD, you will be appointed as Research Assistant ( £45,399 - £48,876) The post sits in the Department of Bioengineering at Imperial’s White City Campus, where engineers, immunologists and clinicians work side by side on translational medicine. The Ishihara Laboratory develops matrix-anchored protein therapeutics — cytokines, checkpoint inhibitors and T cell engagers targeted to tumour collagen — and works closely with clinical partners in the UK, the US and Japan. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr Jun Ishihara — [j.ishihara@imperial.ac.uk]."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29586": {
      "title": "Bioinformatics Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; National Heart & Lung Institute; Researcher Reference: MED06012 About the role: Are you an ambitious bioinformatician looking to apply cutting-edge computational biology to one of the most exciting challenges in global health? As a Bioinformatics Research Associate in this specially created position by Imperial College to support the success of the £7.5 million ARIA-funded M2M (Mechanism to Medicine) programme, you will play a central role in helping to uncover the innate immune mechanisms that protect individuals from respiratory viral infections and accelerate the development of a new generation of preventative medicines. Working alongside Professor Ajit Lalvani and a multidisciplinary consortium of leading academic and industry partners (including Prof Marko Nikolic at UCL, Dr Adrian Grzybowski at AnuBio inc., and Dr Sarah Teichman at University of Cambridge among others), you will integrate and analyse complex multi-omics datasets, generate novel biological insights, and directly support AI-driven approaches to therapeutic discovery. What you would be doing: Lead the harmonisation, integration and analysis of large-scale multi-omics datasets, including single-cell transcriptomics, epigenomics and proteomics data. Develop, maintain and document robust computational pipelines for processing and quality control of next-generation sequencing and proteomics datasets. Identify innate immune correlates of protection through advanced bioinformatics analyses across clinical and laboratory-derived datasets. Work closely with AI and mathematical modelling teams to ensure bioinformatics outputs support downstream predictive modelling and therapeutic development. Collaborate with researchers across Imperial, UCL, industry partners and international collaborators to deliver key project milestones. Present findings to collaborators, funding bodies and at scientific conferences, while contributing to high-impact publications and future grant applications. Support implementation of FAIR data principles and open science practices across the programme What we are looking for: We are looking for a highly motivated and collaborative scientist with: A PhD in bioinformatics, computational biology, computer science, genetics, statistics or a closely related discipline (or equivalent experience). *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £48,876 per annum. Strong programming skills in Python, R, Bash and/or Perl and experience working in Unix/Linux environments. Hands-on experience analysing genomics and multi-omics datasets, including scRNA-seq, scATAC-seq, bulk RNA-seq and proteomics data. Experience developing reproducible computational workflows using tools such as Nextflow, Snakemake or equivalent workflow management systems. Experience working with HPC or cloud computing infrastructure and modern reproducible research practices. Excellent analytical, communication and problem-solving skills, with the ability to work independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. A passion for translating complex biological data into impactful scientific discoveries. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. As a member of research staff, you have 10 development days to use to develop your skills and explore your career prospects Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . The opportunity to work on a pioneering £7.5 million ARIA-funded programme with the potential to transform protection against respiratory viral diseases. A unique position at the interface of bioinformatics, immunology, artificial intelligence and translational medicine. Collaboration with leading researchers and partners from Imperial College London, UCL, industry and international institutions. Genuine opportunities to contribute to high-impact publications, establish scientific independence and support future funding applications. Further information: This is a full-time post (35 hours per week), fixed-term (48 months). If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Sofia Koletsi s.koletsi@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29582": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Experimental Pulmonary Hypertension",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £45,399 - £48,876 per annum; National Heart & Lung Institute; Researcher Reference: MED06007 About the role: We are seeking a motivated Research Assistant to join a British Heart Foundation (BHF)-funded project investigating how the complement system contributes to pulmonary arterial hypertension. Based within the Vascular Sciences Section of the National Heart and Lung Institute at Imperial College London, you will work on an exciting translational research programme combining animal models, primary cell culture, molecular biology and omics approaches to understand disease mechanisms and identify new therapeutic opportunities for patients. What you would be doing: Working closely with researchers, clinicians and collaborators, you will: Perform a range of laboratory techniques including primary cell culture, RT-qPCR, ELISA, western blotting and immunostaining. Support performing and monitoring animal models of pulmonary vascular disease. Generate, analyse and interpret experimental data and maintain accurate research records. Contribute to the day-to-day operation of the research laboratory. Participate in research group meetings and assist the preparation of publications and reports. Work alongside leading researchers in pulmonary vascular biology, cardiovascular disease and complement biology. What we are looking for: A BSc or MSc in Biological Sciences, Biomedical Sciences or a related discipline. Experience working within a research laboratory environment. Experience of molecular and cellular biology techniques including cell culture and common laboratory assays such as qPCR, ELISA, western blotting and immunostaining. Knowledge of cardiovascular, vascular or inflammatory disease biology. Strong communication and organisational skills, and ability to work as part of a multidisciplinary team. Desirable: Experience with animal models of disease. Experience in cardiovascular biology or pulmonary vascular disease research. Knowledge of complement biology. Experience with microscopy. What we can offer you: Working on a British Heart Foundation-funded project with clear clinical relevance. Training in a broad range of in vivo and in vitro experimental techniques. Collaboration with internationally recognised experts in pulmonary hypertension and complement research. Opportunities to contribute to high-impact publications and scientific conferences. A collaborative and supportive research environment. Further information: This is a full-time (35 h/w) post on a fixed-term contract for three years. The successful candidate will join the Vascular Sciences Section within the National Heart and Lung Institute and work closely with Professor Allan Lawrie, Professor Martin Wilkins, Professor Matthew Pickering and collaborators across academia and industry If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr Merve Keles – m.keles@imperial.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29592": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Genetic Vector Control",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Life Sciences; Researcher Reference: NAT02307 About the role: Do you have expertise in genetic engineering and want to contribute to developing next-generation strategies to control mosquito populations that transmit malaria? We have an exciting opportunity for a Research Associate to join a new research programme funded by the Gates Foundation and focused on developing self-limiting genetic strategies for the population suppression of Anopheles mosquitoes. The successful candidate will play a key role in conducting independent research as part of a small team. The experimental work will focus on improving proof-of-principle technologies that we have already developed, with the aim of advancing them towards potential future deployment, as well as investigating new target genes and developing novel molecular tools to support the next generation of these technologies. What you would be doing: As a Research Associate, you will play an active role in planning and delivering research within our programme. You will take initiative in designing and conducting experiments, developing appropriate molecular and experimental approaches, and analysing robust and reproducible data. You will maintain accurate records of your work and contribute to publications, presentations to funders and research meetings. You will work collaboratively with scientists, technicians and students at Imperial and with external collaborators in the UK and internationally. You will also contribute to the supervision and development of undergraduate and postgraduate students and research assistants and, where appropriate, help coordinate the work of small research teams. Alongside your research, you will contribute to the smooth running of the laboratory and to maintaining a safe, collaborative and productive research environment. What we are looking for: We are looking for a motivated and proactive researcher with experience in molecular biology and genetic engineering, including DNA cloning, genetic construct design and CRISPR-Cas9 or related genome-editing approaches. You will also have experience with mosquitoes or other model organisms and transcriptomic data analysis. Previous experience with molecular and genetic assays is required as well as basic experience in bioinformatics and data analysis. Experience in embryo microinjection work would be highly advantageous. What we can offer you: You will join a collaborative research environment at Imperial College London, with the opportunity to contribute to research addressing major global health challenges. The Group has a long-standing relationship with the Target Malaria research consortium, providing opportunities to engage with researchers and initiatives across the consortium and gain insight into the development, evaluation and potential deployment of genetic strategies for malaria control. You will have access to Imperial’s dedicated career development support for researchers, including 10 development days each year, alongside opportunities for progression, a competitive benefits package and a supportive, inclusive working environment. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: Please note that this is a PhD level role but candidates who have not yet been officially awarded will be appointed as a Research Assistant. This is a full-time post. This role is for a fixed-term contract 31 August 2028, extendable for an additional 12 months subject to funding availability. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr Federica Bernardini (f.bernardini11@imperial.ac.uk) or Ms Clelia Supparo (c.supparo@imperial.ac.uk)."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29596": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Marine Biogeochemistry",
      "description": "Closing Date: 30/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Life Sciences (Silwood Park); Researcher Reference: NAT02295 About the role: This research associated with this post will be part of a wider team within the new Horizon Europe OceanSOS project https://oceansos.net/ . This exciting interdisciplinary project aims to determine the impact of seamounts and the currents they create on stimulating the overlying biological carbon pump (BCP), to then inform marine spatial planning. The knowledge generated will provide a stronger foundation for assessing future ocean risks and developing sustainable management approaches. By improving understanding of the links between biodiversity, food webs, and carbon cycling, this work will support decision-making aimed at protecting ocean ecosystems and the processes that sustain them. This is an exciting opportunity to be part of an international team of biogeochemists, physical ocean modellers, ecologists and policy makers. What you would be doing: You will focus on identifying how seafloor topology influences the mesopelagic biological carbon pump, specifically looking at particle type, size and microbial remineralisation across a seamount and abyssal plain. You will be part of a wider team https://oceansos.net/wp3 capturing the contributions of larger organisms, deep-sea communities, and connections between the seafloor and the open ocean in the BCP. Your specific responsibilities will include going to sea in the North Atlantic (Summer 2027) and will use multiple techniques to determine the regional BCP, to then feed into the physical, food-web and statistical models, used by the OceanSOS team. The post-holder will also facilitate fieldwork south of South Africa (early Summer 2027) and the samples collected from this region. The tools you will use at sea include sediment traps, marine snow catchers and optodes to measure microbial remineralisation. You will be responsible for organising your component of the fieldwork so it is essential you have fieldwork experience at sea. You will also process the samples you collect to then lead and co-author peer-reviewed publications, and will work closely with the wider project team. You will have the opportunity to present findings to the research team as well as at national and/or international conferences. As this project is part of a large consortium, the you will have the opportunity, and be expected to, collaborate widely within our smaller sub-team, but also with the rest of the consortia including at annual science meetings. There will also be the opportunity to work with international partners. What we are looking for: Experience in fieldwork and sample analysis associated with the biological pump, sediment traps and/or microbial respiration A track record of overseeing successful experiments from design, manipulation and sampling, to data analysis and publishing the results Knowledge of processes involved in organic carbon cycling in the ocean Ideally some experience of leading your own work at sea Knowledge of how to analyse organic carbon samples Enthusiasm for research in ocean carbon science What we can offer you: Being part of a large international network of project partners from across Europe, with many travel opportunities Inclusion in a vibrant team at Silwood Park Campus working on many aspects of oceanic and freshwater carbon cycling The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: The post is funded by UKRI through Horizon Europe. This is a full time, fixed term role for up to 2 years. Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant. Applicants who may require or prefer part-time or flexible working are encouraged to apply. The post is based at Silwood Park Campus. Hybrid working is possible. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Emma Cavan, e.cavan@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29583": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Molecular Bacteriology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/10/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Infectious Disease; Researcher Reference: MED05988 About the role: Supported by funding from the BBSRC, we are seeking a talented post-doctoral research associate to join the team in the group of Professor Andrew Edwards at Imperial College London. This role will investigate how polymyxin antibiotics kill Gram-negative bacteria. The position is available full time, starting by October 2026 (or as soon as possible thereafter) and is available for 12 months . Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) represents a significant and growing global health threat, with recent projections estimating that 39 million deaths will be directly attributed to AMR between 2025–2050. Of particular concern are Gram-negative bacteria such as E. coli. The emergence of multi-antibiotic resistant strains necessitates the use of reserve (last resort) antibiotics such as the polymyxins. However, this class of antibiotics are poorly efficacious and efforts to improve their use are hampered by an incomplete understanding of how they work. What you would be doing: The Edwards group is based in the Department of Infectious Disease and the Centre for Bacterial Resistance Biology and encompasses scientists at all levels of experience. We are looking for candidates who can thrive in this dynamic and creative environment. The appointed candidate is expected to lead studies that aim to understand how polymyxins function, including experimental planning and execution, data analysis and presentation and writing of manuscripts. What we are looking for: Essential criteria for the role include: A PhD in bacteriology or related field. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £48,876 per annum. Experience in genetic manipulation of Gram-negative bacteria. Experience in nanoscale imaging techniques including AFM. Ability to work collaboratively, experience in experimental planning, troubleshooting, data analysis, and safe laboratory working practices. Desirable experience for this role includes one or more of the following: Experience in studying membrane biology of Gram-negative bacteria, knowledge of antibiotics and resistance, evolution of resistance, assay development. What we can offer you: Become part of a multidisciplinary team working on the major challenge of antibiotic resistance. The opportunity to develop your research career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time, on-site post (35 hours per week). This role is for a fixed-term contract for 12 months. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Professor Andrew Edwards – a.edwards@imperial.ac.uk."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29722": {
      "title": "Solution Architect - People and Finance",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £71,793- £82,031 per annum; Information & Communication Technologies; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01455 About the role: Are you an architect who enjoys solving complex enterprise challenges, and would like your work to shape how a world-leading university supports its people and manages its finances? As Solution Architect for People & Finance, you will lead the architecture of the systems that underpin Imperial’s people, payroll, finance and procurement services, with Oracle Fusion as the core enterprise resource planning (ERP) platform. Working within Architecture & Technology Operations, you will partner with product and platform teams, engineering, and colleagues in People and Finance to design secure, scalable and sustainable solutions aligned to Imperial’s Digital Strategy and enterprise architecture standards. What you would be doing: Own the Oracle Fusion architecture: Lead the solution architecture for Oracle Fusion (HCM and ERP/Financials), ensuring configuration, extension and integration decisions follow enterprise architecture principles and Oracle’s recommended practices. Connect the estate: Design how People and Finance data flows between Oracle Fusion and the wider Imperial application landscape, with secure, well-governed integrations that support accurate, timely reporting. Design with impact: Lead the development of solution architectures across the People & Finance domain, aligned to enterprise standards, product roadmaps and Imperial’s Digital Strategy. Apply AI and automation: Make effective use of AI and automation, including the AI capabilities within Oracle Fusion, to improve efficiency, insight and user experience, in line with organisational policy. Lead in governance: Contribute to the Technical Design Authority, technology working groups and communities of practice, reviewing and assuring solution designs across the ICT portfolio. Provide technical leadership: Guide delivery teams to implement approved designs and champion modern engineering practices, including API-first, cloud-native, DevOps and platform-based architecture. Bring stakeholders together: Facilitate architectural decision-making with People, Payroll, Finance and Procurement stakeholders, translating complex technical concepts into clear, outcome-focused solutions. What we are looking for: Experience designing complex enterprise solutions within large, diverse or federated organisations Experience delivering or architecting solutions on Oracle Fusion (HCM and/or ERP/Financials), or a comparable enterprise ERP/HCM SaaS platform, including configuration, extensions and integrations Knowledge of people, payroll, finance and procurement business processes and how they are supported by an ERP platform Understanding of Oracle Fusion’s integration, extensibility and reporting capabilities (e.g. Oracle Integration Cloud, REST and SOAP APIs, BI and analytics) Experience across the full product or platform lifecycle, from discovery and design through to live service and decommissioning A strong grasp of modern architecture approaches, including cloud, integration platforms, APIs and secure-by-design, with awareness of data protection, financial controls and audit requirements for People and Finance data The ability to assess and apply AI and data-driven technologies responsibly, weighing value, risk, ethics, security and compliance Excellent communication skills, with the ability to engage audiences from senior stakeholders through to technical teams and suppliers Desirable: TOGAF or Oracle Fusion certification, experience of architecture governance or design assurance, and experience in the Higher Education sector What we can offer you: The opportunity to own the architecture of business-critical People and Finance platforms that every member of Imperial relies on A central role in Architecture & Technology Operations, with influence over enterprise architecture standards and strategic technology decisions The chance to shape how AI and automation are adopted across Imperial’s enterprise systems A supportive and collaborative environment focused on learning, knowledge sharing and professional development Flexible and hybrid working arrangements Access to Imperial’s wider staff benefits and development opportunities Further information: This is a full-time, open-ended position. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Darshan Vigneswara - d.vigneswara@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29728": {
      "title": "Technical Operations Manager (Electrical & Mechanical)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Department of Bioengineering; Professional / Support Services Reference: ENG03875 About the role: The Department of Bioengineering at Imperial College London is a world-leading interdisciplinary department that brings together engineering, medicine and the life sciences to address some of the most pressing challenges in healthcare. Through pioneering research, education and innovation, we develop technologies and solutions that improve human health and wellbeing. We are looking for an experienced engineering leader to join us as our Technical Operations Manager (Electrical & Mechanical Engineering). This is a unique opportunity to lead and develop a high-performing multidisciplinary technical team supporting world-class research, innovative undergraduate teaching and cutting-edge engineering facilities across two campuses. Working at the heart of the department, you will play a key role in enabling the next generation of engineers and supporting researchers tackling some of society's biggest healthcare and technology challenges. As Technical Operations Manager (Electrical & Mechanical Engineering), you will provide strategic leadership for the department's mechanical, electrical and mechatronic engineering laboratories, workshops and makerspaces. You will oversee facilities that support hundreds of students and researchers each year, ensuring technical resources, expertise and infrastructure continue to meet the evolving needs of teaching and research. Reporting to the Head of Technical Support Operations and Safety, you will lead specialist technical teams across both South Kensington and White City, driving collaboration, innovation and service excellence. You will help shape the future direction of our technical services, ensuring the de What you would be doing: Leading and developing specialist teams across electrical and mechanical engineering disciplines. Managing and enhancing engineering laboratories, workshops and makerspaces across both campuses. Creating an inclusive, collaborative and high-performing technical culture. Working closely with academic and professional services colleagues to deliver outstanding support for teaching and research. Driving strategic planning, resource allocation and operational improvement across technical services. Providing expert technical leadership and guidance within your area of engineering expertise. What we are looking for: You will be a confident engineering leader with a passion for developing people, improving services and enabling excellence in education and research. Essential requirements include: Relevant Engineering degree (BEng/MEng) suitable for Chartered Engineer requirements Evidence of continuing professional development and industry engagement Demonstrated leadership in progressive engineering environments, including managing technical professionals Substantial experience supporting undergraduate engineering education in higher education settings Proven experience in agile resource management and priority setting in dynamic environments What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time, open-ended post. Interviews will be held 07 & 08 December 2026. For information on the role and application process, please contact Bhavna Patel, Resources and Staffing Manager ( bioeng.staffing@imperial.ac.uk)"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29719": {
      "title": "Associate or full Professor in Medical Statistics and Innovative Trial Design",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; Competitive; School of Public Health; Academic & Teaching Reference: MED06029 About the role: We are seeking to recruit an outstanding Associate Professor or Professor in Medical Statistics and Innovative Trial Methodology will be based in the Imperial Clinical Trials Unit (ICTU), School of Public Health (SPH), with key collaborations with academics across SPH, the wider College and national and international collaborators from both academic and industry setting. You will lead research on clinical trials and new experimental medicine theme across the Faculty of Medicine and contribute to postgraduate and undergraduate teaching in the School of Public Health. You will be responsible for establishing and leading a new programme of research in early phase and innovative clinical trials methodology and they will head up a new experimental medicine portfolio and develop a new research section in ICTU that will compliment and integrate with ICTUs existing research groups. What you would be doing: You will contribute to the academic aims of the Department by: Developing an adaptive and early phase clinical trials team within the Imperial Clinical Trials Unit Directing, leading, or contributing to, a research area at the highest scientifically rigorous levels. Teaching and examining courses at all levels – undergraduate, masters and higher research degrees – through lectures, seminars, tutorials, course work and personal supervision. Participating in the business planning and objective setting process for the academic team and the departments. Making a significant contribution to building and establishing links between the appropriate departments and colleagues in other parts of Faculty and wider University. What we are looking for: You will have: A PhD in Statistics or Mathematics or related field An established national/ international reputation and expertise in the field of innovative clinical trials methodology (commensurate with career stage) A track record in attracting high levels of research funding An excellent publication record and the ability to publish high quality research in peer reviewed journals. Experience of training Undergraduate and Postgraduate students Evidence of a developing research profile and clear future research plans that have the potential to contribute to and strengthen the Department's research priorities and strategic objectives. Full information on duties, responsibilities and criteria can be found in the attached Job Description. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (43 days off a year including public holidays and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Please see our Further Particulars for more information about the College and the benefits available to staff. Further information: Salary Associate Professor: Minimum starting salary of £85,873 per annum Professor: Minimum starting salary of £98,149 per annum Closing date: 16 November 2026, 23:59 (Midnight GMT) This is a full-time open-ended position If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Director of the Imperial Clinical Trials Unit (ICTU), Professor Victoria Cornelius, email: v.cornelius@imperial.ac.uk If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at: support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29708": {
      "title": "Clinical Project Manager in Cerebrovascular Experimental Medicine",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Department of Brain Sciences; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06040 About the role: We are looking to recruit a Clinical Project Manager to play a key role in our new Medical Research Council (MRC)-funded Experimental Medicine study, “MiroDose”. This study aims to characterise the role of PDE5 inhibition in cerebral small vessel disease and support development of treatments. to improve outcomes. Small vessel disease is a very common but under-recognised cause of major neurological disability, and is one of the main causes of ischaemic stroke, brain haemorrhage and dementia. You will primarily be working to manage and deliver the MiroDose study, led by Professor Alastair Webb , but will also support other studies in his Imperial Small Vessel Disease group that underpin recruitment to MiroDose. MiroDose is a two-centre, double-blind, placebo-controlled, dose-ranging study where patients with small vessel disease receive different doses of ‘mirodenafil,’ a drug similar to ‘Viagra’, for a few weeks at a time. They then undergo detailed physiological testing with ultrasound of the brain and technical, physiological MRI brain scans. You will be a key part of the Imperial Small Vessel Disease Group, working particularly closely with the other members of the MiroDose team at Imperial (Prof Webb, Dr Fatemeh Geranmayeh, a clinical research fellow and a research assistant), as well as the study team at our second site at St George’s. You will also be working with the other post-doctoral fellows, clinical research fellows, PhD students and research assistants that comprise the wider Small Vessel Disease group led by Prof Webb within the Department of Brain Sciences. What you would be doing: You will be responsible for all aspects of study management. This will include involvement and coordination of study set-up at Imperial College London, Imperial College Healthcare NHS Trust, St George’s Hospital and City St George’s University London, in close liaison with Prof Webb. Duties will include leading on the development of study documentation, regulatory submissions, drug logistics, liaison with our commercial collaborator (Aribio Ltd), protocol development, monitoring progress and performance of the study and recruitment from both centres, ensuring efficient recruitment and reliable data collection. This will be achieved through implementing / adapting established procedures to ensure the studies are conducted in accordance with contractual agreements, study protocol, study-specific processes and systems, SOPs, regulatory and ethical requirements and ICH-GCP guidelines, as well as delivered to time and within budget constraints. You will liaise closely with the participant-facing staff, including the clinical research fellows, Research Assistants, PhD students and NIHR RDN support staff. You will also support the coordination of patient and public involvement and engagement (PPIE) and study monitoring activities, and attracting further research funding to the group. This is an exciting opportunity to work on a study that could pave the way to a new treatment for one of the commonest causes of neurological disability, including stroke, dementia and declining mobility. What we are looking for: You should have experience in the coordination or management of biomedical or clinical research studies (preferably within a university, NHS, or research organisation setting). You should have proven ability to successfully deliver on complex research projects, including working to timelines, regulatory requirements, and recruitment targets, alongside of experience of clinical research delivery. You must be able to build effective working relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, and have an understanding of Patient and Public Involvement and Engagement (PPIE) in clinical research. What we can offer you: A unique opportunity to work on an exciting experimental study Join a highly multi-disciplinary group in a world-leading institution Further information: This is a part-time (50% FTE) fixed term post until 31 January 2030. You will be based principally at the Hammersmith Hospital and Charing Cross Hospital, in Prof Webb’s group, and will be expected to spend 60% of your time on-site in line with Imperial’s Work Location framework. You will be expected to travel between sites and also potentially to collaborating research sites, e.g., St George’s Hospital. Should you require any further details on the role please contact: Prof Alastair Webb ( alastair.webb@imperial.ac.uk )."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29704": {
      "title": "Imperial BHF CRE Transitional Fellowship (non-clinical)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/12/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £50,733 - £67,351 per annum; National Heart & Lung Institute; Researcher Reference: MED06046 About the role: The British Heart Foundation Centre of Research Excellence (BHF CRE) at Imperial College London invites applications to its Transitional Fellowship Programme open to exceptional postdoctoral candidates with a basic science background. This two‑year fellowship is supporting transition to competitive external funding and emerging independence that will add value to Imperial’s cardiovascular research portfolio. We also welcome applications from researchers who already hold an external career development award and wish to combine it with this transitional fellowship as a relocation package. This is a fantastic opportunity to embark on a path to leadership with the support of world-leading research centre. The BHF CRE is based across Imperial College London’s Hammersmith and White City campuses and comprises four research themes that focus on: population cardiovascular health, the factors driving heart adaptation during disease development, endothelial resilience, developing personal monitors of cardiovascular health. Further information on the research themes: https://www.imperial.ac.uk/bhf-research-excellence/ . Recruitment is open to expertise in all relevant areas including discovery science and molecular mechanisms of disease. The proposed research project will be hosted by one of our BHF CRE Principal Investigators and should be developed in liaison with your sponsor. Applicants must apply online via Imperial’s jobs website and include CV, research plan, statement on career intention, and a letter of support from a BHF CRE Principal Investigator confirming agreement to act as sponsor. What you would be doing: With the support of your sponsor, you will develop applications for highly competitive Career Development Awards, generate preliminary data, and gain experience in award management. You will also have extensive opportunities to network with UK‑based and international leaders, enhancing your research profile and fostering collaborations. What we are looking for: PhD (or equivalent) in cardiovascular research or related discipline and extensive postdoctoral experience. A proven track record of excellent academic research An ability to raise research funding from external sources, commensurate with your career stage. A strong interest in interdisciplinary working and translational research. Interest in widening participation and outreach activities. Excellent communication skills with the ability to convey ideas and concepts clearly and effectively to a range of audiences. \" A proven track record of contributing to the wider academic and institutional community\". What we can offer you: Support for the Career Development Award applications to external funders, such as BHF, Wellcome Trust, MRC, BBSRC, UKRI Future Leader Fellowship. This includes extensive academic and administrative support throughout the application process and interview preparation. Networking opportunities with UK-based and international leaders in the cardiovascular fields. Access to sector-leading career development and mentoring programmes offered by Imperial College London as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Access to cutting edge facilities at Imperial College London. Opportunities to attract students from undergraduate and postgraduate programmes at Imperial College London. Opportunities to develop teaching skills. Sector-leading remuneration package (including 41 days off a year and generous pension schemes) and access to local and national discounts through Imperial Perks at Work. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: How to apply: Please upload the following documents with your application: - CV - Research plan - statement on career intention - A letter of support from a BHF CRE Principal Investigator confirming agreement to act as sponsor. For available sponsors, please refer to the investigator lists under each research theme: https://www.imperial.ac.uk/bhf-research-excellence/. Application deadline 06.12.2026 . Interviews will be held in January. The role is full‑time and fixed‑term for up to 24 months (12 months contract will be also considered). Appointment will be offered at either the Research Associate (£50,773 - £59,484) or Research Fellow (£57,075 - £64,619) level, depending on candidate’s experience. We also offer research consumable support depending on the specific requirements of the project. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dr. Magdalena Zak ( m.zak1@imperial.ac.uk ), manager of Imperial BHF CRE."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29702": {
      "title": "Research Associate or Research Fellow in Computational Health Policy Modelling",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/11/2026 Department: Business School; £50,733 - £67,351 per annum; Researcher Reference: BUS00904 About the role: Build the Next Generation of Health Policy Simulation Models Do you want your research to influence how governments design policies to prevent chronic disease? Are you excited by the challenge of combining data, simulation, and policy to tackle the world’s biggest health problems? The Centre for Health Economics & Policy Innovation (CHEPI) at Imperial Business School is seeking a Research Associate / Research Fellow in Computational Health Policy Modelling to play a central role in developing Health-GPS — our state-of-the-art microsimulation model for non-communicable disease (NCD) policy analysis. Appointment will be made at Research Associate or Research Fellow level, depending on experience. Why This Role Matters Chronic diseases such as diabetes and cardiovascular disease remain leading causes of illness and premature death worldwide. Yet, the tools available to evaluate and design effective prevention policies are still evolving. Health-GPS provides a framework to explore how changes in diet, physical activity, and related risk factors affect long-term population health and economic outcomes. Health-GPS represents the gradual accumulation and interaction of individual-level risk factors — such as BMI, diet, and income — offering insights into the potential impact of different policy interventions over time. You will help develop and apply this model across major international projects including: GOLDFINCH – estimating the effects of fiscal policies on ultra-processed food consumption. JA Prevent NCD – a European Joint Action on integrated strategies for NCD prevention. JACARDI – a European initiative to reduce cardiovascular and diabetes risk. Working with economists, epidemiologists, and data scientists, you’ll push the boundaries of policy simulation and shape real-world decision-making. This is a hands-on computational role. Alongside using Health-GPS for policy analysis, you will write, test and review code in its C++ codebase, working within the team’s collaborative development workflow (version control, pull requests and code review). C++ experience is highly desirable, but we also welcome strong programmers in other relevant languages who are willing to work in C++. What you would be doing: Contribute to the architecture, development, optimisation, and maintenance of Health-GPS, writing and reviewing code in its C++ codebase and ensuring its continued scientific and policy relevance. Work with colleagues to plan and deliver modelling work across multiple projects, producing high-quality outputs on time. Help integrate new methodologies, including AI and machine-learning approaches, into simulation design. Conduct quantitative analyses to estimate the health and economic impact of proposed policies. Identify and manage relevant datasets in collaboration with internal and external partners. Communicate findings through academic papers, policy briefs, and stakeholder workshops. Contribute to funding proposals and the expansion of CHEPI’s modelling activities. What we are looking for: We’re seeking a creative and technically strong individual with: A PhD (or equivalent, or near completion) in health economics, epidemiology, computer science, or a related field. Experience building or using microsimulation models or other quantitative policy tools. Strong programming skills in at least one language (e.g. Python, R, or C++), and experience contributing to a shared codebase using version control (e.g. git). Experience working with large or longitudinal datasets. The ability to translate complex model outputs into actionable policy insights. Curiosity, independence, and enthusiasm for multidisciplinary collaboration. Experience with C++ is highly desirable. Experience applying AI or ML methods to health or policy modelling is also desirable. Candidates for appointment as Research Fellow should also demonstrate a strong publication record, experience contributing to funding proposals, and the ability to design and lead original research. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: Interested? Apply online with: A cover letter explaining your motivation and relevant experience, and indicating the level (Research Associate or Research Fellow) for which you are applying. Your CV. Contact details for two referees. This position is full-time and fixed-term for 24 months, with potential for extension depending on funding. Start date: as soon as possible. For queries about the post, contact Prof. Franco Sassi (f.sassi@imperial.ac.uk)."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29711": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £85,873 per annum; Department of Metabolism, Digestion and Reproduction; Researcher Reference: MED06028 About the role: We are looking to appoint a part-time Senior Research Fellow, The project will develop a new technology for isotope-resolved fluxomics by extending the Universal Fragmentation Model (UFM) to provide mechanistically justified, atom-resolved interpretations of MS/MS and, where informative, MSⁿ data. The research will: (1) implement persistent atom mapping and isotopologue support in UFM; (2) build and experimentally validate a flux-oriented fragment knowledgebase that classifies fragments by mechanistic reliability; (3) integrate UFM-derived fragment definitions with established EMU-based metabolic flux analysis tools; and (4) develop robust LC-MS/MS and MSⁿ acquisition and validation workflows, including studies of pentose phosphate pathway flux. Experimental work will use stable-isotope-labelled standards and tracer studies, with high-resolution MS/MS and MSⁿ on an Orbitrap ID-X at Imperial. What you would be doing: The post is funded by the Biotechnology and Biological Sciences Research Council (BBSRC) through the Transformative Research Technologies (25TRT) programme to develop predictive, mechanistically informed tandem mass spectrometry for high-resolution metabolic flux analysis. To plan, lead and undertake high-quality research in mechanistic tandem mass spectrometry, computational fragmentation modelling and isotope-resolved metabolic flux analysis. The post-holder will develop the UFM software for atom-resolved isotope tracing, perform computational and LC-MS/MS/MSⁿ validation, integrate UFM outputs with established MFA tools, analyse tracer data, and deliver reusable software, benchmark datasets and publications. The post-holder will also contribute to project management, dissemination and development of follow-on research opportunities. What we are looking for: PhD in chemistry, analytical chemistry, computational chemistry, mass spectrometry, metabolomics, systems biology, or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research, industrial or commercial experience What we can offer you: This is an opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution We will support your career development activities. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 38 days off a year) Further information: This is a Part time Fixed term role, based at our Hammersmith Campus. For informal inquiries or questions about the role, please contact Prof Zoltan Takats directly at z.takats@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29693": {
      "title": "Domestic Support Worker",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £30,699 per annum; Early Years Education Centre; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01452 About the role: We are a 210 place Early Years Education Centre with an outstanding Ofsted rating and reputation, and we serve the community of Imperial London caring for the staff and student’s children. We are looking to recruit one part-time Domestic Support Worker who is committed to the cleanliness of the Centre which provides professional high-quality childcare and education in an environment that is clean, safe, and hygienic for each of the children, and the staff and parents who attend the setting. Safeguarding children is our priority, and all post holders are required to adhere to the EYEC’s Safeguarding children and Whistle blowing policy. All post holders will be required to obtain a satisfactory enhanced level Disclosure and Barring check. What you would be doing: You will work from 06:00-09:00 Monday to Friday and be responsible for providing a high standard of hygiene and maintaining the cleanliness of the Centre’s indoor and outdoor environment by performing various daily cleaning duties. You will also be required to provide regular deep cleaning, identify areas that need particular attention and report any health and safety concerns to ensure a safe environment for all users of the Centre. What we are looking for: Someone willing to undertake training as and when required A willingness to receive training including, general knowledge of Health & Safety issues and Control of Substances Hazardous to Health (COSHH) Previous experience in a domestic role and working as part of a team Previous experience using equipment for polishing and cleaning various surfaces Excellent hygiene practices Understanding the safe storage and use of chemicals, germs and the prevention of spread of infection Understanding the importance of safeguarding children Be able to communicate effectively in English with all users of the Centre Be physically fit to undertake the full duties of the post requirements Show flexibility in relation to time, new ideas and helping other team members Comply with the protective clothing policy and have a clean, smart and tidy appearance at all times Honesty and integrity are essential as is discretion and confidentiality What we can offer you: Working hours are Monday to Friday from 06:00-09:00 Events to come together and celebrate from summer parties to Christmas breakfasts The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including the possibility of flexible working, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: If you require any further details on the role please contact: Liz-Anne Des Vignes, Deputy Head of EYEC – l.desvignes@imperial.ac.uk Please note that this post is part-time at 15 hours per week, the actual salary payable will be calculated on a pro rata basis. Closing date: Midnight on Thursday, 5th November 2026."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29697": {
      "title": "NIHR Clinical Lecturer General Surgery 2026 Award",
      "description": "Closing Date: 20/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £67,325 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Researcher Reference: MED06033 About the role: Clinical Lecturer in General Surgery (Breast Surgery) ST3-ST6 Job title Clinical Lectureship in General Surgery (Breast Surgery) ST3-ST6 Location Hamlyn Centre for Robotic Surgery & Charing Cross Hospital Breast Unit Imperial London Campus - Faculty of Medicine - Department of Surgery and Cancer Salary £67,325 - £76,582 (plus London weighting allowance of £2,162 per annum) Start Date 7 April 2027 (in line with clinical rotation date) Interview Date Thursday 26th Nov 1.30-5.30pm or on an alternative suitable date. Interview Address: Hamlyn Library, Hamlyn Centre, Bessemer Building / South Kensington Campus (Exhibition Rd, London SW7 2AZ) Please ensure you review the following when completing your application: Job Description Clinical Lecturer Person Specification Clinical Lecturer Important Information Document About the role We are looking for an ambitious surgical trainee to combine higher surgical training in breast surgery with a four-year research programme in robotic precision surgery. You will be leading research on the Hamlyn Trust-funded Precision Robotic Breast-Conserving Surgery programme at the Hamlyn Centre for Robotic Surgery. The programme is developing a robotic platform that detects surgical margins in real time during breast-conserving surgery. It combines fluorescence-guided margin imaging, structured-light breast-surface scanning and deformable registration, and shared robotic control on the Cornerstone Sentire surgical robot. We invite applications from ambitious, medically qualified doctors who aspire to combine clinical practice with research through integrated academic and clinical training, and to develop a career as a future clinical leader in academia. We are offering a locally funded Clinical Lectureship in General Surgery (Breast Surgery) , designed to support postdoctoral clinicians in developing independent research alongside continued clinical training. Appointments are on a fixed-term basis for up to four years, or until completion of clinical training (CCT), whichever occurs sooner. Successful candidates will join the vibrant and collaborative academic community at Imperial College London, contributing to high-impact research that aligns with key NIHR strategic priorities. This post offers an outstanding opportunity to work at the forefront of clinical research, while developing the skills, experience, and independence required for a successful career as an NIHR-supported clinical academic. Please ensure you read the Imperial College London - Clinical Lecturer Additional Information document prior to submitting your application as this contains further details in relation to eligibility. What you would be doing: In this role, you will: Undertake 50% protected research time alongside 50% clinical training Develop your academic portfolio and establish independent research directions Contribute to high-quality research outputs and funding applications Participate in teaching, mentoring, and academic leadership activities You will play a key role throughout the translational development pathway, shaping clinical protocols and guiding bench, phantom and cadaveric validation in the early stages of the project. You will then continue to work closely with the engineering team on preclinical in vivo (porcine) validation and the progression towards first-in-human evaluation. Throughout the programme, you will contribute across device design, biophotonics, AI-based image registration, human-robot control, and regulatory and ethical engagement, providing exposure to the full medtech innovation pipeline rather than a single narrow discipline. About the opportunity We are offering a Locally Funded Clinical Lecturer position in General Surgery (Breast Surgery) Specialty & FoM Department Recruitment and Academic Training Lead General Surgery Department of Surgery and Cancer Professor Daniel Leff, Professor of Breast Surgery, Chair in Surgical Performance & Precision Surgical Sciences. Email: d.leff@imperial.ac.uk Professor Ferdinando Rodriguez y Baena, Co-Director of Hamlyn Centre Professor Daniel Elson, Professor of Surgical Imaging, Hamlyn Centre Academic Programme Director: Professor Nick Watkin Clinical Programme Director: Miss Sabina Rashid Hamlyn Centre: https://www.imperial.ac.uk/hamlyn-centre/ Informal enquiries may be addressed to the named Recruitment and Academic Training Lead above. The successful applicants to these Clinical Lectureships will join internationally recognised research groups at Imperial College London. You will be trained in cutting-edge, state-of-the-art techniques and supported to undertake research that improves health and wellbeing. You will be encouraged to develop your own innovative and independent research programme, with the aim of delivering high-quality peer-reviewed publications and securing competitive research funding. Applicants must hold GMC registration at time of applying with a licence to practise by start date, have already completed a higher research degree (PhD, MD (Res) or equivalent) submitted at time of applying and awarded by time of appointment, and meet all essential criteria outlined in the relevant person specification. As this post is exempt from the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974, a satisfactory Disclosure and Barring Service (DBS) check , at the appropriate level, will be required for the successful candidate. What we are looking for: We are seeking candidates who: Hold (or been found appointable to hold) a National Training Number (NTN) Have completed a PhD, MD, or equivalent research degree – submitted at the time of application and awarded by start date. Demonstrate clinical and academic excellence Show evidence of research productivity and leadership potential Are motivated to develop a career as an independent clinician-scientist Can work collaboratively in a multidisciplinary environment What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various Further information: Please note that job descriptions are not exhaustive, and you may be asked to take on additional duties that align with the key responsibilities mentioned above. If you experience any technical issues while applying online, please don't hesitate to email us at support.jobs@imperial.ac.uk . We're here to help."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29699": {
      "title": "Programme Manager - Management Suite",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Department: Business School; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Professional / Support Services Reference: BUS00905 About the role: Imperial Business School is seeking to appoint a talented and motivated individual to join the Management Suite Programme Team to lead on the operational delivery of the MSc Strategic Management programme. This is one of the largest programmes at the Business School. What you would be doing: Reporting to the Senior Programme Manager, this is an exciting opportunity to become a key player in a dynamic, supportive, and engaging team supporting MSc students. This role has line management responsibility for a Programme Coordinator. You will be responsible for coordinating the effective and efficient delivery of our programme, overseeing the operation, review and development of administrative processes. Flexibility is essential as you strive to meet high standards of service delivery and customer satisfaction, ensuring an exceptional student experience. You will manage all aspects of programme delivery and proactively enhance the student experience by providing useful analytics, insights into pedagogical practices and promoting cross-functional teamwork. Your approach to programme management will focus on improving student experience, fostering innovation and driving continuous quality improvements. What we are looking for: You should have excellent communication skills and be customer service oriented. You will be comfortable balancing multiple deadlines and prioritising effectively. You will have a commitment to providing high-quality support and smooth operational delivery of the programme. You will also have the ability to work under pressure, working both independently and within a team. You should be able to implement change on both a strategic and operational level. Experience in the Higher Education sector would be an advantage, as would an interest in business education and technology. You will need to be an energetic, organised self-starter with strong communication and management skills. You should have experience working with MS Office and large amounts of data, as well as excellent attention to detail and organisational skills. Experience of managing, supporting, or coordinating projects, activities or events to time and budget is also essential. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . We are a friendly and collaborative team, with a passion for providing students and staff with high-quality programme support. Further information: This is a full time, fixed term position for 12 months based at South Kensington campus (hybrid). This is a temporary appointment to cover the duties of a member of staff whilst they are on maternity leave. The fixed term tenure given above may change should the member of staff exercise their right to return to work earlier or delay their return to work. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Julia Foglia, Senior Programme Manager ( j.foglia@imperial.ac.uk )"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29692": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Detecting Unverbalized Dangerous Capabilities in Frontier Models",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Computing; Researcher Reference: ENG04085 About the role: We are looking for a motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate (PDRA/PostDoc) to join the Safety&Alignment lab at Imperial College London, led by Dr. Oana-Maria Camburu in a fully funded research role. The position is fully funded in the context of Dr. Oana-Maria Camburu’s grant on the project Detecting Unverbalized Dangerous Capabilities in Frontier Models . The project focuses on the detection of frontier model’s dangerous capabilities that are not verbalized in their Chain-of-Thoughts (CoTs). Externalized reasoning oversight is a safety paradigm focusing on monitoring the CoTs of large language models (LLMs). As frontier models learn that they are monitored, they can become incentivized to hide their dangerous capabilities from their CoTs. Moreover, as these models become increasingly capable, their obfuscation capabilities also increase, allowing them to bypass monitors. This project investigates ways to detect and defend against such emerging scenarios. The post is based in the Department of Computing at Imperial College London at the South Kensington Campus. The Department of Computing is a leading department of Computer Science in the UK and worldwide and has consistently been awarded the highest research rating. In the previous REF assessment, the Department was ranked third (1st in the Research Intensity table published by The Times Higher) and was rated as \"Excellent\" in the previous national assessment of teaching quality. What you would be doing: We strive in publishing in top-tier conferences and journals. Some of the areas you will work on are: Designing and developing novel algorithms for detecting and defending against unverbalized dangerous capabilities in frontier models. Conducting independent and collaborative research within the group. Publishing results in top-tier conferences and journals (e.g., NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, ACL, EMNLP, AIJ, TACL, etc). Contributing to research projects involving frontier models. Contributing to the life and development of the Safety & Alignment lab, such weekly meetings, presentations, etc. Mentoring PhD and MSc students. What we are looking for: Candidates should have, or expect shortly to obtain, a PhD in Computer Science, Mathematics, Physics or a closely related discipline, or equivalent research/industrial experience. You will have practical experience within a research environment and publications in relevant and refereed journals/conferences, such as NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, ACL, EMNLP, TACL, AIJ, etc as well as experience in a broad range of techniques including model checking, monitoring, and CoT. Research experience in frontier models and in coding with deep learning libraries such as Pytorch is also essential. Please see job description for a full list of requirements. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: Full-time, fixed term for 12 months to start ASAP and before 8 th April 2027. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £48,876 per annum. In addition to completing the online application candidates should attach: A full CV with a list of all publications A research statement indicating what you see are interesting research issues relating to the above post and why your expertise is relevant Informal enquiries related to the position should be directed to Dr Oana-Maria Camburu o.camburu@imperial.ac.uk For queries regarding the application process contact Jamie Perrins: j.perrins@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29689": {
      "title": "Alumni Engagement Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: President’s Team; £38,608 - £41,666 per annum; Advancement; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRS00581 About the role: The Alumni Engagement Office is seeking a committed, enthusiastic and people-focused individual to join our dynamic, vibrant and customer-facing Alumni Engagement team. Are you energised by the opportunity to work for an organisation with an ambitious and inspiring vision? Do you have a strong interest in community engagement and a passion for delivering first-class customer service? Are you someone who enjoys connecting people, building networks and managing a varied workload to achieve excellent results? If so, this could be the perfect career opportunity for you. At Imperial, we believe in scientific imagination for world-changing impact. As a world-leading university for science, technology, engineering, medicine and business, we are deeply engaged in the forces shaping the future. From global health and climate change to AI and business leadership, our research and innovation are helping to address some of the world’s toughest challenges. Our growing global alumni community of more than 275,000 graduates plays a crucial role in the university’s long-term success. The Alumni Engagement team works to engage, support and inspire alumni in meaningful, strategic and sustainable ways. We provide trusted services and a wide range of benefits, including online engagement opportunities, a varied programme of events, volunteering initiatives and ways for alumni to stay connected with Imperial and with one another. What you would be doing: This role is central to maintaining Imperial’s high-quality engagement with its alumni community. As the first point of contact for alumni around the world, you will respond to, and process, a wide range of enquiries and requests, providing exceptional customer service by email, online, in writing, over the phone and in person. What we are looking for: We are looking for someone who is organised, collaborative and confident in a fast-paced environment. You will be a strong team player with excellent communication skills and a proactive approach to delivering positive outcomes. What we can offer you: The opportunity to make a meaningful contribution to a global community and support an institution whose work is changing lives around the world. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing Further information: This position is full-time and open-ended/permanent. Interviews to take place the w/c 16th November or 23rd November. Should you have any queries please contact: Kristin Gembiak, Alumni Engagement Officer (Groups and International), k.gembiak@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29677": {
      "title": "Incubator Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/11/2026 Department: Provost’s Team; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Enterprise; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRO01457 About the role: Are you passionate about innovation, entrepreneurship, and supporting deep tech startups within a vibrant community by providing engagement activities, and an outstanding customer experience? Could you help founders turn breakthrough ideas into successful, scaling ventures? Imperial is seeking an experienced and ambitious Incubator Manager to lead the delivery of our world-class startup incubation facilities across the West Tech London ecosystem, including ARC Hammersmith, Base One North Acton and the Quantum Transport Testbeds. The Imperial Incubator is a key part of Imperial’s Science for Humanity strategy focussing on amplifying impact and West Tech London. As the Incubator Manager, you will be responsible for Imperial's incubation, shared laboratory and testbed facilities, ensuring they effectively support the Imperial’s innovation and entrepreneurship strategy. This is a unique opportunity to manage one of the UK's leading deep-tech incubation environments, supporting student startups, Imperial spinouts and innovative SMEs working across sectors including life sciences, climate tech, engineering and quantum technologies. Working with significant autonomy, you will lead a multidisciplinary team, build strong relationships with founders and stakeholders, and ensure our facilities provide an outstanding environment for innovation and growth. What you would be doing: The role combines leadership, operations, community building and stakeholder engagement. You will: Lead the day-to-day operation of Imperial's incubation, laboratory and innovation facilities across multiple sites. Manage and develop a skilled operational team, creating a culture of collaboration, excellence and outstanding customer service. Build and nurture a thriving community of founders, innovators, researchers, investors and partners. Ensure facilities operate safely, efficiently and in compliance with relevant regulations and Imperial policies. Oversee occupancy, onboarding and customer experience across incubation spaces. Support networking programmes, workshops, events and VIP visits that strengthen the innovation ecosystem. Manage budgets, supplier relationships and operational contracts to ensure financial sustainability and value for money. Work closely with internal teams and external partners to develop new facilities, services and initiatives to contribute to the continued growth and reputation of Imperial's innovation and entrepreneurship ecosystem. What we are looking for: We are looking for an experienced leader who combines operational excellence with a genuine passion for innovation and entrepreneurship. You will have: A degree-level qualification or equivalent professional experience. Significant experience supporting startups, business growth initiatives or deep-tech facilities. Experience managing customer, client, member or stakeholder relationships in a complex environment. A strong track record of leading teams and delivering high-quality operational services. Experience managing health and safety, risk and compliance in laboratory, technical and multi occupancy customer-facing environments. Excellent communication, relationship-building and problem-solving skills. Experience managing budgets, suppliers and external service providers. Knowledge of the incubator, accelerator, innovation and technology ecosystem. A commitment to supporting entrepreneurial ventures and innovation-led growth. Experience gained within higher education, research environments, innovation districts, industry membership organisations such as UKSPA, One Nucleus, or startup ecosystems would be particularly advantageous. What we can offer you: The opportunity to lead one of the UK’s premier deep-tech incubation environments and support innovative ventures tackling global challenges. A key role within Imperial's thriving White City innovation ecosystem and the wider West Tech London network. The chance to work alongside entrepreneurs, researchers, investors and industry partners developing breakthrough technologies. Excellent opportunities for professional development within a world-leading university. A sector-leading benefits package, including generous annual leave and pension provision. Access to a wide range of staff benefits, wellbeing initiatives, family-friendly policies and staff networks. Enterprise is Imperial's innovation and commercialisation division, connecting businesses, researchers, students and entrepreneurs with expertise, funding, facilities and support to turn world-class research and ideas into real-world impact. Further information: This is a full time, open-ended position based at White City campus (100% on-site). If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Graham Hewson g.hewson@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29679": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £45,399 per annum; School of Public Health; Researcher Reference: MED06015 About the role: The post is funded through a SarcoidosisUK Research Innovation Award and will contribute to a research project using linked UK electronic healthcare records to better understand the diagnostic and treatment pathways experienced by people living with sarcoidosis. You will undertake analyses to characterise symptoms, healthcare interactions and diagnostic delays prior to sarcoidosis diagnosis, as well as investigate patterns of oral corticosteroid prescribing and their association with adverse health outcomes. Working closely with clinical researchers and people with lived experience of sarcoidosis, you will help generate real-world evidence to support earlier diagnosis, safer treatment and improvements in patient care. What you would be doing: You will utilise large datasets of linked electronic health records and undertake data management and epidemiological analyses to investigate diagnostic and treatment pathways in people with sarcoidosis. You will characterise symptoms, healthcare interactions and diagnostic delays prior to diagnosis, and examine patterns of oral corticosteroid prescribing and their association with adverse health outcomes. You will contribute to developing study protocols, preparing research outputs and manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals, and presenting and disseminating findings. You will work closely with epidemiologists, respiratory clinicians and people with lived experience of sarcoidosis and may also contribute to other related projects within the research group. This position would suit an early-career researcher with an interest in epidemiology, health data science and respiratory research who is looking to further develop their research skills and career. What we are looking for: A first / masters degree (or equivalent) in Statistics, epidemiology or other relevant quantitative discipline. Knowledge of using electronic healthcare record data to study respiratory disease. Excellent verbal communication skills and the ability to deal with a wide range of people. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: You will be embedded in the Respiratory Electronic Health Record group , led by Prof Quint and work with global collaborators. This is a full-time post (35 hours per week). This role is for a fixed-term contract for approximately 12 months, expected start date of 4 th Jan 2027 until 31 Dec 2027. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Professor Jennifer Quint – j.quint@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29672": {
      "title": "Clinical Trial Monitor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 03/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £55,434 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06026 About the role: We are looking for a Clinical Trial Monitor to join our team. The Imperial Clinical Trials Unit-Cancer (ICTU-Ca) , led by Professor Michael Seckl through its close association with the Imperial Clinical Trials Unit and Imperial Experimental Cancer Medicine Centre , provides a core accredited environment to develop and conduct world-class cancer clinical trials led by Imperial College. With a major focus on precision medicine and proof of concept clinical trials, the ICTU-Ca currently undertakes studies in the phase Ib to phase III space across multiple sites of disease, at both National and International levels. The cancer trial portfolio exploits the research expertise in our Department to enable trials that include new and exciting imaging modalities, translational research initiatives and innovative study designs as they evolve. ICTU-Ca forms part of the Imperial Clinical Trials Unit (ICTU). Cancer is just one of several therapeutic areas including cardiovascular and metabolic disease, respiratory, HIV / infectious diseases, surgery and emergency and critical care. These disciplines all come together under the overarching, UKCRC registered ICTU. What you would be doing: You will be responsible for monitoring clinical trial activities at UK and Ireland investigator sites participating in a large, multi-centre, randomised, phase III clinical trial. You will ensure that the clinical trial is being conducted in accordance with the trial protocol, standard operating procedures, regulatory and ethical requirements and Good Clinical Practice guidelines. They will also ensure appropriate training is delivered and ongoing support is provided through regular communication. What we are looking for: You should have proven monitoring experience in multicentre trials, preferably in an NHS / academic environment. A degree or equivalent in a biomedical/scientific/statistical or allied field or equivalent experience. Evidence of working in the field of cancer is desirable. Applicants should be motivated with proven ability to work well under unsupervised circumstances, well organised and able to work effectively as part of a team. Willingness to travel and flexibility to working Full essential and desirable criteria are detailed in the job description. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity • Benefit from sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes) • Get access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day 1, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and a cycle-to-work scheme • Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel • Be part of a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: This role is full-time and fixed term for 6 months in the first instance. You will need to complete an online application. Should you require any further details on the role please contact Mr Philip Badman – p.badman@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29669": {
      "title": "Finance Manager - Target Malaria (Global)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 19/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £71,793 - £77,747 per annum; Department of Life Sciences (Silwood Park); Professional / Support Services Reference: NAT02313 About the role: Are you an experienced finance leader who wants to use your expertise to support ambitious global health research? As Finance Manager for Target Malaria, you will lead the finance function for an international, not-for-profit research consortium developing and sharing novel genetic technologies to help reduce malaria transmission. Working across partner institutions in Africa, Europe, the United Kingdom and the United States, you will provide the financial leadership, insight and constructive challenge needed to support sound decisions, responsible stewardship and effective programme delivery. This is a senior management role within Target Malaria. You will work closely with the Global Principal Investigator, Director of Programme Management and consortium leadership, taking day-to-day ownership of a complex, multi-institutional and multi-currency portfolio. What you would be doing: Lead financial planning, budgeting, forecasting, monitoring and reporting across the consortium, ensuring resources remain aligned with strategic priorities and funding requirements. Act as the programme’s senior financial adviser, translating complex information into clear analysis, practical recommendations and constructive challenge for senior leaders. Own strategic financial plans, reforecasting, budget-versus-actual reporting and scenario planning, including the financial implications of programme growth and movement towards field implementation. Oversee subaward and partner finances, multi-currency budgets, foreign exchange reviews, funder reporting, grant compliance and the financial management of research sub-contracts. Develop and continuously improve financial systems, controls, templates and reporting tools so that information is accurate, timely, transparent and useful for decision-making. Provide financial and commercial input to grants, contracts, consultancy agreements and memoranda of understanding, working with research services, legal and procurement colleagues. Support internal and external audits and promote consistent, proportionate financial controls and assurance across partner institutions. Line-manage, coach and develop the Finance Officer, delegating routine activity through clear processes while building their confidence and independent capability. What we are looking for: You will combine strong technical finance expertise with sound judgement, clarity and the confidence to work collaboratively across a geographically dispersed partnership. In particular, you will bring: A recognised professional accountancy or finance qualification, such as ACCA, CIMA or ACA, or substantial equivalent senior-level experience in financial leadership and management. Substantial experience of financial planning, forecasting and managing significant budgets within an academic, research, global health or similarly complex multi-partner environment. Experience acting as a strategic finance business partner to senior leaders, providing insight, challenge and recommendations to support decisions, resource allocation, sustainability and risk management. Experience preparing and presenting reports for funders or donors, costing research proposals and applying Full Economic Costing methodology. Experience working internationally, including managing multi-currency budgets and reporting across multiple jurisdictions. Advanced Excel and financial-modelling skills, together with experience of research costing, grant-management and enterprise finance systems such as Worktribe, Oracle Grants Management, Oracle Financials, ICIS or equivalent. Proven line-management experience, with the ability to coach, develop and build confidence in others. Communication and influencing skills, including the ability to explain complex financial issues clearly to non-finance colleagues and build trusted relationships with partners and funders. Experience of supporting programmes operating across Africa, working in higher education or global health, and managing major philanthropic funding would be advantageous. What we can offer you: The opportunity to shape the financial management of an ambitious international programme working towards a world free of malaria. A visible senior role, working with programme leadership, scientists and professional teams across a diverse global consortium. The scope to improve systems, strengthen financial capability and influence how resources are prioritised as the programme evolves. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and contribute to Imperial’s mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to flexible working, family leave, workplace wellbeing support, staff networks and a range of other employee benefits. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: For an informal discussion about the role, please contact: Dr Karen E Logan, Director of Programme Management – Target Malaria, email: kelogan@imperial.ac.uk If you are a thoughtful, collaborative finance professional who can combine rigour with practical advice, we would be delighted to hear from you. About Target Malaria Target Malaria is a not-for-profit international research consortium aiming to develop and share novel tools for malaria control. Our vision is a world free of malaria. Led by Imperial College London, the programme brings together expertise in science, stakeholder engagement, regulatory affairs, risk, communications, quality assurance and programme management. Our work is guided by excellence, co-development, evidence, openness and accountability. Find out more at Target Malaria"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29675": {
      "title": "Research Associate for AI and Nuclear Engineering Modelling",
      "description": "Closing Date: 17/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Earth Science & Engineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04068 About the role: Could your expertise in computational modelling, neutronics or AI methods help design and manage the nuclear reactors of tomorrow as a Research Associate in Applied Nuclear Computation? Working within Imperial College London's world-leading Applied Modelling & Computation Group (AMCG), you will apply advanced numerical, physics-based and data-driven methods to one or two exciting and complementary projects: an industry-funded digital engineering platform focused on the design and optimisation of a novel thorium reactor, and a UKRI-backed agentic digital ecosystem supporting the full lifecycle of next-generation High-Temperature Gas-Cooled Reactors (HTGRs). What you would be doing: As a Research Associate in the AMCG, you will contribute to internationally leading research, taking the initiative in planning and executing your own scientific work to contribute to the broader goals of two cutting-edge nuclear projects. You will develop and apply advanced computational, numerical and physics-based methods, ensuring the validity and reliability of your data and maintaining meticulous records of your findings throughout. Your work will span the full research cycle — from identifying and developing appropriate techniques for data collection and analysis, to writing reports for research sponsors, submitting publications to high-quality refereed journals, and presenting your findings at national and international conferences. You will also have the opportunity to lead and mentor, directing small research teams and providing guidance to PhD students and research assistants, helping to shape the next generation of computational scientists. Collaboration will be at the heart of your role, as you build and maintain relationships with allied researchers both within Imperial College and across the wider international community, contributing to grant bids and actively participating in group meetings and seminars. In everything you do, you will be expected to uphold the highest standards of research integrity, safety and professionalism, promoting the reputation of the AMCG, the Department and Imperial College London as a whole. What we are looking for: With up to three posts available, we welcome applicants from a broad range of disciplines — including applied mathematical modelling, computational mathematics, reactor physics and AI — who are eager to bring their technical expertise to one of the most important clean energy challenges of our time. In particular, you will have: A strong computational and numerical foundation — you hold a PhD (or have equivalent experience) in Applied Mathematics, Physics, Nuclear Engineering or a closely related computational discipline, and have hands-on experience developing software implementing discretisation methods such as Finite Element or Finite Volume approaches. (Note: Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant.) Scientific programming proficiency — you are confident writing and deploying scientific code in one or more of Fortran, C/C++ or Python, ideally with experience developing and running codes in High Performance Computing (HPC) environments. Familiarity with reactor physics methods and tools — experience with neutronics, fuel cycle or burnup/depletion modelling — whether through codes such as OpenMC, Serpent, MCNP, WIMS or SCALE, or through knowledge of advanced reactor concepts such as HTGRs or thorium systems — is highly advantageous, though not essential. An interest in or exposure to AI and data-driven modelling — experience applying machine learning or reduced-order modelling techniques to physics problems, such as training neural networks on simulation data, would be a valuable addition to our multidisciplinary team. A track record of research delivery and communication — you can demonstrate experience working in a research environment, with the ability to write clearly for publication, present confidently at conferences, and engage effectively with both academic collaborators and industry partners. A proactive, collaborative and organised approach to work — you are able to manage your own research programme with appropriate supervision, prioritise effectively against deadlines, and contribute positively to a team environment, including supporting and motivating junior researchers and students. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a fixed term post for up to 33 months. If you require any further details about the role, please contact Chris Pain - c.pain@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29644": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Optimal Quantum",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £50,733 - £62,001 per annum; Department of Physics; Researcher Reference: NAT02308 About the role: We are looking for a candidate with experience in optimal quantum control for the collaborative QuantERA project FALCON. The successful candidate is expected to design optimised driving schemes that allow the experimental partner in Innsbruck (UIBK & AQT) to implement entangling gates with trapped ions that achieve high fidelities under practical conditions, i.e. at finite temperature and without accurate calibration. The candidate should have strong interest in collaborative work, and they should collaborate both with the experimental partner in Innsbruck and with the theory partner in Sofia. What you would be doing: Your main role would be the design of optimised driving patterns for the realisation of noise-resilient quantum gates of trapped ions. Together with the other consortium partners you would identify the main sources of imperfections in the consortium’s experiments that result in reduction of gate fidelities, and you would develop optimal control solutions to achieve resilience to the identified imperfections. You would use both analytical and numerical tools for your work, and you would interact on a regular basis with all the project partners. What we are looking for: We are looking for a candidate who holds a PhD with theoretical/numerical work in the fields of quantum information/quantum technologies. Additional experience as Postdoc is desired but not necessary. You should have a strong interest in collaborative work, and the ability to communicate with experimental partners. Ideally, you would have a strong background in optimal control and it’s application to quantum-optical systems. What we can offer you: What we can offer you the opportunity to grow in your career at a world-leading institution within a collaborative European project on the interface between academia and the quantum tech industry Access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time (35 hours per week), fixed-term role based at our South Kensington Campus. Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as Research Assistant The Department of Physics is proud to hold an Athena SWAN Silver Award, recognising our commitment to fostering a supportive, inclusive, and thriving community. We value the strength that comes from diverse perspectives, and we warmly welcome applications from talented people of all backgrounds, and particularly those underrepresented in physics, to join our vibrant community supported by initiatives that promote collaboration, inclusivity, and professional development. Please note that this is a readvertisement. Those candidates who have previously applied cannot apply again. Find out more about our inclusive environment. Queries relating to the application process should be directed to Light Community Administrator, Sunita Aubeeluck - light@imperial.ac.uk Queries relating to the research project should be directed to Professor Florian Mintert f.mintert@ic.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29666": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Civil Engineering Materials",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £73,194- £82,954 per annum; Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering; Academic & Teaching Reference: ENG04057 About the role: The Materials Section in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering is seeking to recruit an Assistant Professor with expertise in civil engineering materials. We welcome applications from candidates working across any area of materials relevant to civil engineering, including those with expertise spanning multiple materials or modelling approaches that complement our infrastructure materials characterisation facilities. Relevant areas include, but are not limited to, cementitious materials, steel and metals, glass, timber, ceramics, composite materials and biomaterials. The successful candidate will have an established research profile with strong potential for impact. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research can influence engineering practice, policy development, industry, government, regulators and international agencies. Experience of contributing to undergraduate and/or postgraduate teaching would be advantageous. The postholder will develop and lead an original programme of research, secure external funding, and build collaborations within the Department, College and wider research community. They will be expected to develop and manage research projects, supervise and mentor research students and staff, publish in leading journals, present at international conferences, and engage with industry and other stakeholders to maximise the impact of their research. What you would be doing: You will develop and lead an original programme of research in civil engineering materials, addressing important engineering and societal challenges. Working independently and collaboratively across the Department, Faculty and College, you will secure external research funding, lead research projects, and build a thriving research group comprising postdoctoral researchers and postgraduate and undergraduate students. The role includes supervising, training and mentoring research students and staff, including PhD candidates, publishing in leading international journals, presenting at major conferences, and engaging with industry, government and other stakeholders to maximise research impact. You will contribute to teaching and assessment across undergraduate, MSc and doctoral programmes through lectures, seminars, tutorials, coursework and supervision. This will include contributing to teaching on the MEng Civil Engineering programme and the third-year design project, as well as developing and supervising undergraduate and MSc research projects. In addition you will provide academic and pastoral support and contribute to curriculum development and the continuous enhancement of teaching, learning and assessment. What we are looking for: A PhD (or equivalent experience) in Civil Engineering Materials or a related field. A developing national reputation in Civil Engineering Materials. A strong publication record, commensurate with career stage. Experience of training undergraduate and postgraduate students, and supervising higher degrees (PhD) is desirable. What we can offer you: We are proud to support our staff with: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of science for humanity The opportunity to grow in your career with tailored programmes for academic staff including mentoring and dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days off annually and generous pension schemes) Further information: Further information can be found in the job description. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Professor Hong Wong hong.wong@imperial.ac.uk Any queries regarding the application process should be directed to Lucy Chivers at l.chivers@imperial.ac.uk . For technical issues when applying online please email sjobs@ic.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29654": {
      "title": "Early Stage Researcher on a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Innovative Training Network",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £44,284.07 - £48,796.89 per annum; Department of Chemistry; Researcher Reference: NAT02220 About the role: Please note interviews will be held on a rolling basis while the advert is live. It is therefore strongly recommended that you submit your application as early as possible. The advert will be closed once a suitable candidate is selected. Are you an early-career researcher passionate about advancing fundamental knowledge in the field of atomic, molecular and optical physics and its rapid translation into real-life applications? We are seeking a highly motivated individual to join the Department of Chemistry at Imperial College London as a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Doctoral Network Researcher, funded by the Horizon Europe Marie Curie Doctoral Network TRILOGY. TRILOGY advances the emerging field of ultrafast chirality by exploiting attosecond light-driven electron dynamics to overcome the limits of conventional chiroptical methods. Targeting applications in the life sciences and advanced technologies, we aim to develop next-generation, high-sensitivity spectroscopic tools to excite and probe electron motion in chiral molecules, supported by close collaboration with industrial partners to enable rapid translation into applications. TRILOGY brings together 11 leading institutions across Europe to train 16 Doctoral Candidates at the forefront of theory and experiment in ultrafast science. The programme offers a unique, interdisciplinary environment and equips researchers with highly competitive skills spanning fundamental physics, technology development, and industry. At Imperial College London, the Research Assistant will contribute to the work package titled: “Enantiosensitive imaging and manipulation with locally chiral evanescent waves.” What you would be doing: In this role, you will take a leading part in shaping the emerging field of ultrafast chirality. You will: Develop a theoretical and computational framework to model light propagation in confined environments and the ultrafast electronic response of chiral matter to such tailored light. Investigate how we can shape matter at the nanoscale to shape light’s polarisation in three dimensions for efficient chiral sensing and manipulation. Explore how to bring the outcome of fundamental research in atomic, molecular and optical physics to real-life applications. Work closely with international partners across the TRILOGY consortium and undertake up to two-month placements at academic and industrial partners, gaining first-hand experience of how fundamental research informs real-world applications. Build your research profile through journal publications, conference presentations, and collaborative project deliverables, supported by an exceptional network of academic and industry mentors across Europe. What we are looking for: A good Master’s degree (or equivalent) in Physics, Chemistry, or a closely related field. Strong knowledge in atomic, molecular and optical physics. Skills in computational modelling and data analysis (e.g., Python, MATLAB or similar). Demonstrated interest or prior experience in theoretical and computational approaches to light-matter interactions. Ability to meet the academic requirements for PhD registration at Imperial College London. Willingness to undertake international travel and placements within the consortium. TRILOGY brings together 11 leading institutions across Europe to train 16 Doctoral Candidates at the forefront of theory and experiment in ultrafast science. Eligibility requirements: Applicant must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in the country of the organization they are applying to for more than 12 months in the 3 years immediately prior to the recruitment. Applicant must not yet been awarded a PhD degree and must be in the first 4 years (full-time equivalent) of their research careers prior to the recruitment. The successful candidate will be employed as a Research Assistant and must apply for admission as a PhD student with Imperial College London and be able to meet our criteria for admission. What we can offer you: The opportunity to work at a world-leading institution developing novel approaches for ultrafast and highly efficient chiral sensing and manipulation through state-of-the-art theoretical and computational modelling. A sector-leading salary and benefits package, including 43 days' annual leave. Access to training modules, networking events and research exchanges within the Marie Curie Doctoral Network. Support for developing your PhD thesis, career skills and publication record. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . The Research Assistant will be paid an attractive salary for 36 months (as defined in MSCA guidelines). The exact (net) salary will be confirmed upon appointment and is dependent on local tax, social and health insurance regulations and on the country correction factor (to allow for the difference in cost of living in the different EU Member States). The Research Assistant will also be compensated with a Mobility Allowance for researchers who have a family, as well as a Family Allowance. All amounts are subject to deductions and taxes. Family is defined as persons linked to the researcher by (i) marriage, or (ii) a relationship with equivalent status to a marriage recognised by the national legislation of the country of the beneficiary or of the nationality of the researcher, or (iii) dependent children who are being maintained by the researcher; family status is determined at recruitment and does not evolve. Further information: Start date: As soon as possible, and no later than 01 March 2027. Duration: 36 months (full-time, fixed term). Base: Department of Chemistry, Imperial College London (White City Campus) Salary: £44,284.07 per annum (without Family Allowance) or £48,796.89 per annum (with Family Allowance). Contact: For informal enquiries, please contact Dr David Ayuso at d.ayuso@imperial.ac.uk You will be recruited for a duration of 36 months and must work towards a PhD at Imperial College London. You will undertake periods of placement to other academic or industry institutes. Please submit a CV, cover letter and completed application form. Please note this is a readvertisement; previous applicants need not apply. Please note interviews will be held on a rolling basis while the advert is live. It is therefore strongly recommended that you submit your application as early as possible. The advert will be closed once a suitable candidate is selected."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29642": {
      "title": "Innovation and Entrepreneurship Manager (Venture Catalyst Challenge)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Provost’s Team; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Enterprise; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRO01456 About the role: The Innovation & Entrepreneurship Manager for the Imperial Enterprise Lab’s Venture Catalyst Challenge programme is responsible for delivering this flagship entrepreneurship programme, specifically designed to support the most promising entrepreneurs at Imperial. As Innovation & Entrepreneurship Manager you will be responsible for supporting the delivery of Imperial Enterprise Lab’s activities including one-off events, workshops and programmes for our community of enterprising students, recent alumni, and post docs. This includes leading on our well-known flagship initiative the Venture Catalyst Challenge, a 3-month long programme for Imperial affiliated startups. You will be part of an energetic and passionate team who support the growth of new entrepreneurial ideas from our talented science, engineering, medicine and business students and post docs. This role will allow you to take the lead on a range of activities to support the development of early-stage ideas and their acceleration into commercial ventures. Now in its 14 th year, the VCC has supported nearly 300 startups to raise in excess of £250M in investment and grant funding. The post holder will play a pivotal part in designing and delivering the programme, securing partners and funders. What you would be doing: Manage the operational delivery of Imperial’s entrepreneurial programme, VCC Innovate and engage with stakeholders to ensure its successful delivery. Develop and deliver communications plans to ensure engagement and participation and attract support from internal & external experts and to launch a network of alumni. Identify and engage expert suppliers to deliver complex intellectual content and events Support the Head of the Enterprise Lab in securing funding and partnerships for the VCC programme and beyond Analyse and report progress or challenges to key internal & external stakeholders. What we are looking for: Experience of working in the area of innovation and entrepreneurship support at a level suitable to facilitate credibility and positive relations with staff, post docs, students, alumni and external partners Project management experience at a level conducive to delivering a high-quality education and professional development programme in a world-class institutional setting Ability to conceive, plan and own operational delivery strategies over multiple years to deliver near, mid- and longer-term College objectives. Important note Diverse teams really are the best teams. We know that candidates (especially women research tells us) may be put off applying for a job unless they can tick every box. So if you are excited about working with us and think you can do much of what we are looking for but aren’t sure if you are 100% there yet… why not give it a whirl? What we can offer you: Training, learning, and personal development opportunities The opportunity to work with groundbreaking young entrepreneurs A kind, fun, and supportive team and team culture The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year) Further information: This is a full time, open-ended position based at South Kensington campus (hybrid). Please do make the most of the opportunity to provide a supporting statement to demonstrate how your experience and aspirations make you a strong candidate for the role. If you require any further details on the role, or would like to have an informal and confidential discussion please contact: Chantelle Young c.young@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29651": {
      "title": "Internal Communications Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Department: President’s Team; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Communications Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRS00579 About the role: Are you an experienced internal communications professional looking for your next step? As Internal Communications Manager, you will be responsible for creating and delivering audience-led, multi-channel communications to engage, inform and inspire Imperial’s 8,000 staff and 23,000 students in the university’s work and mission. You will have a diverse workload with your own portfolio of stakeholders, campaigns and projects to manage, as well as working across the team’s regular channels. What you would be doing: In this role, you will be responsible for creating and delivering engaging, audience-focused internal communications campaigns that support Imperial’s key strategic priorities and organisational change. You will produce high-quality content across multiple channels, including newsletters, news stories, leadership communications, social media and video content. You will look for ways to improve our internal communications channels and outputs, using data, analytics and audience insight to measure the impact of communications. You will support and advise stakeholders and communication colleagues across the institution, building relationships and championing best practice. What we are looking for: Proven experience of managing audience-focused, multi-channel internal communication campaigns within a large and complex organisation, using data and insight to measure impact and inform decision-making. Writing, editing and proofreading abilities, including the ability to coach and improve the work of others. Relationship-building skills, with the confidence to work collaboratively and influence stakeholders at all levels. An organised and proactive communicator who can manage competing priorities, work independently and respond effectively to emerging issues in a fast-paced environment. What we can offer you: The opportunity to work on high-profile communications that support Imperial's strategic priorities and major institutional initiatives. Opportunities to broaden your skills across digital communications, content creation, employee engagement and strategic communications. A supportive and collaborative team environment committed to professional development and continuous learning. Access to Imperial's extensive training and development opportunities, helping you grow your career in communications. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing Further information: This is a new role as part of investment in the Internal Communications team. We are also recruiting for a new Internal Communications Officer. Led by the Head of Internal Communications, the team will be made up of four Internal Communications Managers and two Internal Communications Officers. If you have any questions and/or would like an informal call about the role, please contact: Elizabeth Nixon, Head of Internal Communications (line manager for this role) - e.nixon@imperial.ac.uk or Michael Haig, Deputy Director – m.haig@imperial.ac.uk The primary work location is the White City Campus (MediaWorks Building, White City Place), with some days spent at the South Kensington Campus. Hybrid (60% in the office; 40% working from home)."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29662": {
      "title": "Internal Communications Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Department: President’s Team; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Communications Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRS00580 About the role: Are you an internal communications professional who knows how to turn complex information into compelling stories? Join a collaborative Internal Communications team in a global top 10 ranking university and help deliver audience-led communications that keep our staff and students informed, engaged and connected. From newsletters and leadership communications to events and digital content, you'll help bring important stories and priorities to life. What you would be doing: You will help plan, deliver and evaluate impactful internal communications that engage our community of 8,000 staff and 23,000 students. Your role will involve creating compelling content, managing communication channels and supporting campaigns and events. You will work with colleagues in the Internal Communications team and wider Communications Division, and collaborate with teams across the university to ensure key messages reach the right audiences at the right time. What we are looking for: We're looking for an experienced communicator, content creator and relationship builder. You will bring: Experience of delivering and evaluating internal communications and community engagement activity. Excellent writing, editing and storytelling skills, with the ability to tailor content for different audiences and channels. Experience of managing and crafting content for digital communication channels, such as newsletters, blogs, websites and internal messaging boards. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to manage multiple projects and deadlines simultaneously. A track record of building effective relationships with a wide range of stakeholders. What we can offer you: This is a varied and rewarding role with significant scope to develop your skills, build your network and make a tangible impact across a world-leading institution. We offer: The opportunity to work on high-profile communications that support Imperial's strategic priorities and major institutional initiatives. Opportunities to broaden your skills across digital communications, content creation, employee engagement and strategic communications. A supportive and collaborative team environment committed to professional development and continuous learning. Access to Imperial's extensive training and development opportunities, helping you grow your career in communications. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a new role as part of investment in the Internal Communications team. We are also recruiting for a new Internal Communications Manager. Led by the Head of Internal Communications, the team will be made up of four Internal Communications Managers and two Internal Communications Officers. If you have any questions and/or would like an informal call about the role, please contact: Andrew Youngson, Internal Communications Manager (line manager for this role) - a.youngson@imperial.ac.uk Elizabeth Nixon, Head of Internal Communications - e.nixon@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29655": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 15/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Chemical Engineering; Researcher Reference: ENG04075 About the role: The Department of Chemical Engineering is seeking a researcher to undertake work on the development of a biobased solvent extraction system for recovering biological products. The project will be supervised by Professor Jason Hallett in collaboration with Professor Rodigo Ledesma. What you would be doing: You will carry out research in experimental design, solvent selection and green solvent extractions for lipid and protein products from fermentation systems. You will be responsible for project management and/or to supervise small multi-disciplinary teams. In addition, you will be submit publications to refereed journals and proposals to attract external research funding. What we are looking for: You will design, synthesize and test novel solvents to enhance the recovery of fermentation products via solvent extraction. Some of your key responsibilities will include: • Working with project partners integrating recovery methods with novel biomanufacturing systems • Synthesising sustainable solvents (ionic liquids and deep eutectic solvents) based on biocompatible and renewable components • Designing new extraction processes for recovery of bioproducts such as lipids and proteins • Performing solvent selection to determine options for further testing • Performing solvent extractions to optimise product yield and purity • Assessing product yield and purity through molecular analysis (HPLC, NMR) The successful candidate will have a first degree (or equivalent) in Chemical Engineering, Chemistry or a related discipline and a PhD (or equivalent) in the field of sustainable solvents, or related to bioprocessing. You will have knowledge of novel solvents and bioproduct recovery and characterisation. You will be able to perform experiments in solvent synthesis, solvent extraction, wet chemistry laboratory techniques and all accompanying analytical chemistry techniques, produce clear written reports and presentations and have the ability to collect and manipulate data. What we can offer you: This role is part of the wider European Union project PROPEL: Protein Production in Fully Sustainable Syncells for Biomaterials Manufacturing and Waste Upcycling. The project also involves Imperial’s Department of Bioengineering (Prof Rodrigo Ledesma) and EU academic and industrial project partners in Spain, Denmark and the Netherlands. · The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. · Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. · Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). · Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: The post is available immediately and the appointment is for a maximum of 24 months. The post is based in the Department of Chemical Engineering at Imperial College London (South Kensington Campus). If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Professor Jason Hallett (j.hallett@imperial.ac.uk)."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29658": {
      "title": "Research Associate  on the structural biology of bacterial secretion \nsystems",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £50,733 - £59,484 per annum; Department of Infectious Disease; Researcher Reference: MED06036 About the role: We are seeking to recruit an enthusiastic and motivated structural biologist to join the group of Prof Harry Low, Department of Infectious Disease ( www.thelowlab.org ). The research project focuses on determining the molecular mechanism of bacterial secretion systems including the Tad and Type 4 pilus systems. The position is funded by an 8 year Wellcome Trust Discovery award. What you would be doing: You will be leading your own project working from initial clones through to protein purification, structure determination and biochemical/biophysical mechanistic dissection. You will be working on challenging multi-component membrane complexes both in-vitro and in-cell. Specific methodology will include a range of structural biology techniques including cryo-electron microscopy (SPA, in-situ SPA and tomography), and TIRF microscopy combined with single molecule FRET to probe protein dynamics. What we are looking for: You will have a PhD in structural biology, either cryo-EM, X-ray crystallography or NMR. *Candidates who have not yet been officially awarded their PhD will be appointed as a Research Assistant within the salary range £45,399 - £48,876 per annum. Experience in the design and execution of workflow for structure determination of proteins, from molecular biology/biochemical experiments (e.g. DNA cloning and mutagenesis, protein purification) through to structural data collection, processing and model building is expected. Experience in cryo-EM workflows including sample preparation, data collection and processing is essential. Experience in light microscopy studies is desirable. You must have a strong research track record demonstrated by at least one first author publication in an international journal or bioRxiv. What we can offer you: An inclusive and friendly lab environment that aims to support you in your mission to make important discoveries and generate impactful publications. A well-funded lab underpinned by world class facilities. Opportunities to attend national and international conferences to showcase your work and grow your academic network. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time (35 h/w) and fixed-term for 24 months, with the possibility to extend for a further 24 months. Visa costs associated with moving to the UK will be covered. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Prof Harry Low – h.low@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29646": {
      "title": "Technician in Protein Biochemistry and Molecular Glue Discovery",
      "description": "Closing Date: 18/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Natural Sciences; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Department of Chemistry; Professional / Support Services Reference: NAT02293 About the role: We are seeking a talented technician to join the team in the group of Professor Ed Tate at Imperial College London. This role will support the production and characterisation of recombinant proteins for molecular glue discovery. The position is available full time, starting in January 2027 (or as soon as possible thereafter, with flexibility to start earlier). Molecular glues are an emerging class of small molecules which induce or stabilise protein-protein interactions, creating new opportunities to modulate biological processes and tackle ‘undruggable’ targets through induced proximity. Despite growing interest in the field, there remain limited approaches for the systematic discovery of molecular glues. As a result, there is a need for scalable and generalisable molecular glue discovery platforms that enable target proteins and compound libraries to be screened across the proteome. To support these efforts, we are establishing workflows for the production and characterisation of recombinant proteins for use in proteomic, biochemical, biophysical and cell-based assays. The successful candidate will play a central role in generating high-quality protein reagents and supporting downstream studies that underpin molecular glue discovery. What you would be doing: Your role will focus on the expression, purification and characterisation of recombinant proteins using mammalian cell expression to support molecular glue discovery projects. You may also contribute to the development of biochemical or biophysical assays using these proteins. You will be contributing to a dynamic and ambitious research programme, working in a multidisciplinary team spanning chemical biology, proteomics and medicinal chemistry. In addition to your core activities, you will support the day-to-day running of the laboratory. This includes maintaining equipment, purchasing and organising consumables, disposal of biohazard and chemical waste, participating in laboratory duty rotas, and providing introductory training for new users. The Tate group is culturally diverse and highly multidisciplinary, with project teams encompassing scientists at all levels of experience and from a variety of backgrounds (chemical biology, cell biology, proteomics, cancer biology, drug discovery, etc.). Technicians are expected to engage in the various projects across the Tate group and offer support on an ad-hoc basis as required outside of their core projects. We are looking for candidates who can thrive in this dynamic and creative environment and take optimal advantage of the many training opportunities it provides. What we are looking for: Essential criteria for the role include: A minimum of a BSc and/or MSc qualification in biochemistry, chemical biology, biotechnology, or a closely related field, or an equivalent level of professional qualifications and experience. Experience with mammalian cell culture, protein purification and characterisation Knowledge of, data analysis, and safe laboratory working practices. Desirable experience for this role includes one or more of the following: experience with HEK293, Expi293 or related mammalian expression systems; biochemical or biophysical assay development; experience with plate-based assays; experience of ternary-complex formation assays; relevant laboratory management; knowledge of the molecular glue field. What we can offer you: Working within the Tate group would give you the opportunity to work with >60 scientists at all levels from undergraduates to postdoctoral researchers with our state-of-the-art labs in West London, including the Agilent Measurement Suite and Imperial Drug Discovery Hub. You will be joining a team with an excellent track record in the areas of molecular glues and induced proximity mechanisms ( https://doi.org/10.1021/jacs.5c12299 ; https://doi.org/10.1021/jacs.5c01420 ; The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow your career: Gain access to Imperial’s sector-leading dedicated career support for researchers as well as opportunities for promotion and progression Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 39 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: Starting in January 2027 (or as soon as possible thereafter). Full time, fixed term, for up to 24 months in the first instance. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Professor Ed Tate: e.tate@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29752": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Geotechnical Engineering",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/12/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £73,194- £82,954 per annum; Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering; Academic & Teaching Reference: ENG04090 About the role: We are delighted to be recruiting a new colleague at Assistant Professor level in Geotechnical Engineering in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering within the Faculty of Engineering. The successful candidate will have research interests which complement the current research activity of our Geotechnics Section, together with a developing research profile and a clear vision for developing their research at Imperial. What you would be doing: You will plan, resource and direct an original and impactful research programme in Geotechnical Engineering, leading research projects and developing collaborative projects with colleagues across the Department and Imperial, as well as with external academic and industry partners. You will also contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate education in soil mechanics and geotechnical engineering, including teaching and the supervision of research students. What we are looking for: You will have a PhD (or equivalent) in Geotechnical Engineering or a related field and a developing expertise and national reputation in geotechnical engineering. You will also be able to provide evidence that your research can contribute to (i) address the challenges of delivering low carbon energy, (ii) mitigate the risks posed by climate change, (iii) contribute to develop sustainable resilient infrastructure, or (iv) address the UN sustainable development goals. Expertise in one of the following fields is desirable: Field testing / in-situ monitoring and testing, geoenvironmental engineering including brownfield development, underground infrastructure, earthworks, geological engineering, geotechnical earthquake engineering, or sustainable geotechnics. You will have a portfolio of research publications, confirming national standing (commensurate with career stage) plus experience of undergraduate and postgraduate education (experience of PhD supervision is desirable). What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. The opportunity to grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including mentoring and dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: Further information can be found in the job description. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Professor Catherine O’Sullivan – cath.osullivan@imperial.ac.uk . For technical issues when applying online please email sjobs@ic.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29749": {
      "title": "Associate Professor/Professor in Geotechnical Engineering",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/12/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; Competitive; Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering; Academic & Teaching Reference: ENG04091 About the role: We are delighted to be recruiting a new colleague at Associate Professor or Professor level in Geotechnical Engineering in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering within the Faculty of Engineering. The successful candidate will have research interests which complement the current research activity of our Geotechnics Section, together with an established research profile and a clear vision for developing their research at Imperial. Salary: £85,873 at Associate Professor level £98,149 at Professor level What you would be doing: You will plan, resource and direct an original and impactful research programme in Geotechnical Engineering, leading research projects and developing collaborative projects with colleagues across the Department and Imperial, as well as with external academic and industry partners. You will also contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate education in soil mechanics and geotechnical engineering, including teaching and the supervision of research students. What we are looking for: You will have a PhD (or equivalent) in Geotechnical Engineering or a related field, together with an established national and developing international reputation at Associate Professor level, or an established international reputation at Professor level, in Geotechnical Engineering. You will have an excellent research record and extensive experience of leading the design and delivery of research programmes. You will have a significant track record in attracting high levels of research funding at Associate Professor level, or a sustained track record at Professor level, and will demonstrate a coherent and sustainable vision for your future research at Imperial that aligns with and strengthens the Department's research priorities. You will also be able to provide evidence that your research can contribute to (i) address the challenges of delivering low carbon energy, (ii) mitigate the risks posed by climate change, (iii) contribute to develop sustainable resilient infrastructure, or (iv) address the UN sustainable development goals. Expertise in one of the following fields is desirable: Field testing / in-situ monitoring and testing, geoenvironmental engineering including brownfield development, underground infrastructure, earthworks, geological engineering, geotechnical earthquake engineering, or sustainable geotechnics. You will have experience of undergraduate and postgraduate education and the supervision of PhD students, together with proven skills in leading, motivating, developing and managing colleagues and contributing to the effective performance and development of a leading research department. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to continue science for humanity. Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for academic staff including mentoring and dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Further information: Further information can be found in the job description. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Professor Catherine O’Sullivan – cath.osullivan@imperial.ac.uk. For technical issues when applying online please email sjobs@ic.ac.uk ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29734": {
      "title": "Capital Project Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 08/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £71,793- £82,031 per annum; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01464 About the role: Imperial College London is seeking a Capital Project Manager to support the successful delivery of capital projects across our campuses, helping to create high-quality spaces that support research, education and collaboration. Working within the Capital Project Delivery Team, you will manage capital projects and work packages, typically up to £5m, from briefing and feasibility through to completion and handover. You will help ensure projects are delivered safely, to the required quality, within agreed programmes and capital budgets, while supporting Imperial’s wider Great Environment Strategy. What you would be doing: In this role, you will manage the day-to-day delivery of capital projects, supporting the development of briefs and specifications and monitoring project programme, budget, risk, quality and health and safety. You will work closely with consultants, contractors and internal teams to help ensure projects are delivered effectively and in line with Imperial’s standards and procedures. You will also support contractor and consultant appointments, monitor project performance, carry out site inspections and contribute to project reporting, approvals and stakeholder communications. Working with senior project colleagues, you will help identify and resolve delivery risks and support the smooth transition of completed projects into operation. What we are looking for: A recognised project management or relevant professional qualification, such as CIOB, RICS, RIBA, APM or CMI Relevant experience managing construction projects Knowledge of preparing and controlling project budgets Experience of working to tight programmes and deadlines A strong understanding of quality and value for money in construction project delivery Good organisational and time-management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities A proactive approach to identifying and resolving problems Strong communication skills and the ability to work effectively with colleagues, consultants and contractors The ability to work collaboratively within a complex organisation Good IT skills and a delivery-focused, flexible approach to project work What we can offer you: At Imperial, our values—respect, collaboration, excellence, integrity, and innovation—guide everything we do. You’ll be part of a supportive and forward-thinking team, working on projects that directly contribute to the safety and wellbeing of our community. We are committed to creating an inclusive working environment and welcome applications from all backgrounds. We particularly encourage interest from individuals who are underrepresented in our workforce. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: Please be advised that we are not engaging with recruitment agencies for this vacancy. If you require any further details on the role please contact: Mickie Turner-Goble, Property Divisional Administration Manager by email – m.turner-goble@imperial.ac.uk . Closing Date: Midnight on Sunday, 8 November 2026"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29742": {
      "title": "Chain Florey Predoctoral Fellowship",
      "description": "Closing Date: 17/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,309 - £64,690 per annum; Institute of Clinical Sciences; Researcher Reference: MED06039 About the role: The Chain Florey Predoctoral Fellowship Scheme presents clinical trainees with a unique opportunity to spend 12 months 100% FTE (or 24 months 50% FTE) in fields that range from biomedical discovery science to experimental medicine and translational clinical research at two world-renowned academic institutions, Imperial College and the Medical Research Council (MRC) Laboratory of Medical Sciences (LMS). The Chain Florey Fellowship provides an ideal foundation for those wishing to pursue a career in clinical academia and offer a pathway to subsequent funding opportunities. What you would be doing: You will plan and conduct research, helping you refine your research interests and identify which area best aligns with your clinical background and long-term academic goals. In parallel, you will be developing research collaborations within the College, the LMS and the wider community. You will have the opportunity to develop a competitive application for submission to externally funded Clinical Research Training Fellowship schemes. You will be focusing on your research and will only undertake clinical activity if required by your specialty (usually limited to 0.2 FTE) or your research project. What we are looking for: You will need to demonstrate an appropriate skill base and motivation to pursue scientific research, with the desire to progress on to a PhD afterward. With your application, please submit your CV and a 1-2 page cover letter outlining your motivation for applying and the LMS research groups you would be interested in exploring to develop a PhD project. Practising medic in training in the UK A medical degree Completed Foundation Year Training by the start date of the position Experience of conducting scientific research What we can offer you: The Chain Florey Predoctoral Fellowship provides an ideal base for those wishing to pursue a career in clinical academia with excellent opportunities for academic progression (PhD and postdoctoral opportunities), training and mentoring. Fellows with excellent progress will have access to our Chain Florey Clinical Research Training Fellowships and become a Fellow of our Chain Florey cohort. Further information: The positions are fixed-term and fully funded for 12 months, with the possibility of transitioning to a PhD for candidates who make excellent progress. If you require any further details on the role, please check the scheme’s webpage on the LMS website or contact the Chair of the Chain Florey Committee Professor Declan O’Regan ( declan.oregan@lms.mrc.ac.uk ) or Aurelie Tromp, the Chain Florey Programme Manager ( a.tromp@lms.mrc.ac.uk )."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29746": {
      "title": "Clinical Project Manager in Neuropsychopharmacology and Experimental Medicine",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Department of Brain Sciences; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06041 About the role: We are looking to recruit a Clinical Project Manager to run an exciting Medical Research Council (MRC)-funded Experimental Medicine study, “N,N-Dimethyltryptamine (DMT) for Disorders of Consciousness (DoC)” (DMT-DoC). In people with DoC, this study will investigate the effects of intravenous DMT administration on measures of neural complexity, neuroplasticity, and awareness. You will primarily be working to deliver the DMT-DoC study. This placebo-controlled study involves administering intravenous DMT and a saline placebo to patients with DoC. We will record electroencephalography (EEG) alongside other physiological and behavioural measures before, during, and after drug/placebo administration. You will work in the Computational Neurology group of academic neurologist Dr Gregory Scott , within the Department of Brain Sciences, with a team of collaborators including experts in clinical neurology, psychedelics, neuropsychopharmacology, DoC, EEG, computer science, and neurophysiology. What you would be doing: You will be responsible for all aspects of study management. This will include involvement and coordination of study set-up, in close liaison with Dr Scott. You will lead on the development of study documentation, regulatory submissions, monitoring progress and performance of the study and recruitment from participant identification centres, ensuring efficient recruitment and reliable data collection. This will be achieved through implementing / adapting established procedures to ensure the studies are conducted in accordance with contractual agreements, study protocol, study-specific processes and systems, SOPs, regulatory and ethical requirements and ICH-GCP guidelines, as well as delivered to time and within budget constraints. You will liaise closely with the participant-facing staff, including the Research Assistant, Post-doctoral Research Associate, trial medics, and psychedelics and brain injury specialists. You will also support the coordination of patient and public involvement and engagement (PPIE) and study monitoring activities, and attracting further research funding to the group. This is an exciting opportunity to work on a study that could pave the way to a new class of treatment for DoC and improve our understanding of the neural mechanisms of consciousness. What we are looking for: You should have experience in the coordination or management of biomedical or clinical research studies (preferably within a university, NHS, or research organisation setting). You should have experience of clinical research delivery and strong organisational skills. You must be able to build effective working relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, and have an understanding of Patient and Public Involvement and Engagement (PPIE) in clinical research. What we can offer you: A unique opportunity to work on an exciting experimental study Join a highly multi-disciplinary group in a world-leading institution Further information: This is a part-time (50% FTE), fixed term post until 31 March 2030. You will be based principally at the Hammersmith and White City Campuses, in Dr Scott’s group, and will be expected to spend 60% of your time on-site in line with Imperial’s Work Location framework. You will be expected to travel between sites, and also potentially to collaborating research sites, e.g., The Royal Hospital for Neurodisability, Putney. Should you require any further details on the role please contact: Dr Gregory Scott ( gregory.scott99@imperial.ac.uk )"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29743": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Fellow in Respiratory Viral Infections",
      "description": "Closing Date: 29/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £55,355 - £67,325 per annum; Department of Infectious Disease; Researcher Reference: MED06020 About the role: We are looking for a highly motivated clinician in training (Internal Medicine/Specialty Registrar Grade or equivalent) with a strong interest in infectious diseases and their prevention to investigate immunity in clinical volunteer studies involving rhinovirus and RSV controlled human infection. You will be part of a multi-disciplinary team focusing on human immunity against respiratory viral infections using exciting and unique studies designs alongside cutting-edge laboratory techniques. You will join a dynamic group of clinical fellows, research nurses and scientists led by Prof Chris Chiu (www.imperial.ac.uk/people/c.chiu) in the Department of Infectious Disease, to characterise the response to respiratory viruses in older adults. This will accelerate the development of improved prophylactic interventions respiratory viruses, while providing you with unparalleled experience in translational research, experimental medicine and laboratory analyses. What you would be doing: You will work closely with research nurses, clinical programme manager, other clinical fellows and laboratory scientists to set up and implement human challenge studies to identify determinants of susceptibility and protection against rhinovirus and RSV in older adult volunteers. You will manage the clinical studies, working with research nurses and other clinicians to recruit and screen and care for volunteers, administer virus, and obtain samples. What we are looking for: You should have a degree in Medicine and full GMC registration. It is essential that you are confident and skilled in practical clinical procedures and management of medical emergencies. You will have a strong background in acute medicine and, ideally, in one of the following disciplines: infectious diseases, virology, microbiology, immunology, ENT or respiratory medicine. You should also have good written and verbal communication skills, analytical and proven problem solving/project completion abilities, demonstrable ability to work to deadlines and to work in a team. What we can offer you: You will be appointed on an honorary contract with Imperial College Healthcare NHS Trust. There will be no fixed clinical duties and no on-call, but as part of the Academic Health Science Centre you may be able to participate in clinical activities to maintain your skills and support the clinical respiratory or infection services (up to 6 hours per week maximum), which will facilitate delivery of the research programme. Training will be provided for all aspects of your role, from the clinical skills required to the organisational responsibilities and research skills that will allow your development as a clinical academic. In addition, you will Grow in your career with tailored training programmes for clinical staff including dedicated support with navigating your career and managing research as well as a transparent promotion process. Sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 41 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Be part of a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources designed to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time post (35 hours per week). It is available to start in February/March 2027 and based mainly at the Hammersmith Campus with some travelling to the St Mary’s and Charing Cross Campuses or Chelsea and Westminster Hospital as required. This role is for a fixed-term contract for 24 months. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Prof Chris Chiu (c.chiu@imperial.ac.uk; https://profiles.imperial.ac.uk/c.chiu)."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29770": {
      "title": "Clinical Trial Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £59,808 - £61,352 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06056 About the role: Do you have excellent administrative insight and wish to diversity your skill set and develop a career as a Clinical Trial Manager? What you would be doing: The post holder will be responsible for all aspects of trial management, including close liaison with the Chief Investigator, Principal Investigators at other centres, and international collaborators. Management of the associated study and implementing and adapting established ICTU systems to ensure the trial is developed and conducted to the highest scientific and regulatory standards. What we are looking for: A bachelor's degree or equivalent in biomedical/scientific field or related field and proven clinical trial/project management and monitoring experience gained in multi-centre, randomised trials are essential. The post holder will need to show strong leadership and motivational skills as well as effective communication, organisational and time management skills What we can offer you: This varied role would be ideally suited to someone who wanted to be part of a growing group, working closely on the most high profile research area in the world, with previous administrative, office management and secretarial experience who is looking to build on their experience in the clinical trial sector The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time post (35 hours per week). This role is for a fixed-term contract for 12 months in the first instance. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Janis Best-Lane – j.best-lane@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29765": {
      "title": "Clinical Trial Monitor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £49,348 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06059 About the role: Join our clinical research team as a Clinical Trial Monitor, where you’ll play a vital role in ensuring clinical trials are conducted safely, compliantly, and to the highest standards. Working closely with investigators, sites, sponsors, and study teams, you’ll oversee trial progress, support site performance, review data quality, and help ensure participant safety and regulatory requirements are maintained. This is an excellent opportunity for an organised, detail-oriented clinical research professional who enjoys working collaboratively and making a meaningful contribution to the development of new treatments and healthcare innovations. What you would be doing: The post holder will be to support site management, data management and monitoring of studies within the Imperial Critical Care research team’s portfolio and wider academic activity within the Division of APMIC. What we are looking for: Educated to Degree level and with a proven interest in clinical trials and medical research with experience of working in an academic or medical environment. In addition, a proven ability to take the initiative and pro-actively contribute to role responsibilities with a creative approach to problem solving. What we can offer you: This varied role would be ideally suited to someone who wanted to be part of a growing group, working closely on some of the most high profile research in the world, with trial monitoring/management/equivalent experience who is looking to build on this experience in the clinical trial sector The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full-time post (35 hours per week). This role is for a fixed-term contract for 12 months in the first instance. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Janis Best-Lane – j.best-lane@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29732": {
      "title": "Head Porter",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Department: Chief Operating Officer (COO) Team; £31,677 - £34,810 per annum; Property Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: COO01448 About the role: Are you a friendly, organised professional with a flexible approach and a positive, can-do attitude? If so, we have an exciting opportunity for you to join the Property Division as a Head Porter at one of the world’s top 10 internationally renowned universities. As a Head Porter, you will contribute meaningfully to the smooth and efficient operation of Catering, Stores, and Kitchen areas by coordinating staffing levels, cleaning schedules, and the effective allocation of team members. You will also help create a safe and responsible workplace by supporting compliance with health, safety, environmental, manual handling, COSHH, and waste disposal requirements. What you would be doing: You will supervise Catering and Events Porters, coordinating daily tasks to ensure workloads are shared fairly and completed on time. You will oversee the cleaning and maintenance of kitchens, stores, equipment, utensils, floors, and associated areas, including opening and closing procedures and scheduled deep-cleaning programmes. You will support the safe receipt, storage, ordering, and control of catering supplies, cleaning materials, PPE, and porter equipment. You will also maintain accurate cleaning and temperature records, carry out routine inspections, and help ensure all working practices comply with food hygiene, HACCP, health and safety, and environmental requirements. You will lead regular team briefings, promote a positive working environment, and act as the first point of contact for porter-related issues. You will report defects and maintenance concerns promptly, identify opportunities to improve customer service, and support your Line Manager with any additional operational duties when required. What we are looking for: Previous experience of leading a team with proven ability to motivate and lead team members is an advantage Organisational and time management skills, in order to deliver an efficient and effective service A good understanding of the use of different equipment/machinery within the work area and the ability to operate such equipment/machinery Physically able to operate different equipment/machinery and lift objects of varying loads Full clean UK driving license Ability to use own initiative with limited supervision, to deal with problems which may hinder the delivery of an effective service A flexible approach to working, sufficient to be able to work a 5/7 days per week arrangement, work evenings and weekends, and able to work at any location/catering outlet, as reasonably requested Organisational and time management skills, in order to deliver an efficient and effective service Good communications skills, both written and verbal, in order to present and deliver information in an appropriate manner e.g. timesheets, cleaning records, staff rota What we can offer you: At Imperial, our values respect, collaboration, excellence, integrity, and innovation guide everything we do. You’ll be part of a supportive and forward-thinking team, working on projects that directly contribute to the safety and wellbeing of our community. We are committed to creating an inclusive working environment and welcome applications from all backgrounds. We particularly encourage interest from individuals who are underrepresented in our workforce. The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. Further information: If you require any further details on the role, please contact: Atesh Luximon, Head of Food Production, Service and Development- a.luximon@imperial.ac.uk . Closing Date: Midnight, Monday, 9 November 2026"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29762": {
      "title": "NIHR North West London Patient Safety Research Collaboration (NIHR NWL PSRC) Centre Manager",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Department of Surgery & Cancer; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06057 About the role: This role is perfect for a self-starter who wants to contribute to a wide range research projects focused on improving patient safety across North West London and beyond, by providing, financial management, administrative support, impact evaluation and dissemination and reporting to the funder and other senior stakeholders. This is an interesting and varied role working to support some deeply impactful work on medication safety, the safety of digital health implementation, using integrated care data to improve patient safety and behaviour change of both patients and healthcare workers to improve patient safety. What you would be doing: You will support project, financial, and partnership management activities for PSRC, as well as the PSRC Network (SafetyNet), developing an excellent working knowledge of the research portfolio, with responsibility for ensuring effective monitoring and reporting against objectives to meet external deadlines. Overseeing the management function of PSRC, you will act as first point of contact for internal matters related to resource, contractual, and finance management and with Centre based researchers and theme leads to ensure projects are managed effectively and deliverables and expenditure are on target. You will also liaise externally with project stakeholders (funders, collaborators, and consultants) to track project progress and resolve issues, escalating as appropriate, as well as ensure the centre is adaptive to changes in patient safety priorities. What we are looking for: Experienced programme, or centre manager who has managed multiple complex projects or workstreams with responsibility for oversight of financial, contractual and logistical resources. Flexible and proactive, able to contribute new ideas to deliver an efficient and effective service. Adept at working with a range of stakeholders from across academic and healthcare settings. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Develop in your role with access to learning and development opportunities. Benefit from sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days off a year and generous pension schemes). Gain access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and a cycle-to-work scheme. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: The NIHR PSRC: NWL seeks to address the patient safety challenges of today and tomorrow, by driving uptake of innovations and service transformation by patients, health and social care workers, health systems, policymakers and regulators. We support the development, validation and testing of such interventions. This is a full-time post (35 hours per week). This role is for a fixed-term maternity cover contract until 31 March 2028. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Emma Parnham e.parnham@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29768": {
      "title": "Operations and Communications Administrator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Medicine; £42,505 - £46,146 per annum; Faculty of Medicine Centre; Professional / Support Services Reference: MED06054 About the role: Are you an experienced administrator, with a passion for organisation, great communication skills and a keen eye for operational detail? The Faculty Education Office (FE0) in the Faculty of Medicine is seeking to appoint an Operations and Communications Administrator for a newly created role in the School of Medicine Secretariat team. The Faculty Education Office (FEO), part of Imperial’s leading Faculty of Medicine, is responsible for the delivery of Imperial College School of Medicine’s (ICSM) undergraduate programmes in Medicine and Medical Biosciences, and our new MSci Medical Science and Innovation from 2027. The Secretariat is one of the FEO’s five core sections, and the team are responsible for a range of operational and administrative areas that ensure a seamless staff and student experience, including (alongside communications and staffing operations areas) governance, quality, information governance and student welfare. In this role, you will support the Staffing and Operations Officer in overseeing the people and operational needs of the FEO’s teams across three campuses, and the Communications and Operations Manager with a range of communications and engagement activities. You will also offer administrative support to the Director of Undergraduate Science, who is leading the development and implementation of the new MSci programme. What you would be doing: The Operations and Communications Administrator is a key role in the centre of FEO activity, supporting a range of staff and activities across the FEO’s education-focused remit. You will be responsible for delivering the day-to-day operations across the FEO, supporting the management of the FEO’s offices across three campuses. You will act as a first point of operational contact for FEO staff, as well as providing personalised administrative support to the Director of Undergraduate Science, and wider administrative support to the Faculty Education Office and School of Medicine senior leadership teams. You will work collaboratively with the Communications and Operations Manager to support the development and delivery of effective communications and engagement activities for the staff and student community. What we are looking for: You will have office administration experience, with excellent organisation and record-keeping skills. You will be proficient in the use of the Microsoft Office suite broadly and particularly in Sharepoint, with a willingness to work with new platforms and develop current use of existing ones. You will have a demonstrably high level of oral and written communication skills in order to best engage with stakeholders and represent senior leadership when necessary and ideally have some experience drafting communications for a range of audiences. Experience supporting senior leadership and managing complex diary arrangements as an executive or personal assistant, ideally in Higher Education, would make you an ideal candidate for this role. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: Interviews for this role will be held in person at our Charing Cross Hospital Campus. This is a full-time, open-ended role. We offer hybrid working with an expectation of being on campus 60% of the time. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Cara O’Garro – Staffing and Operations Officer – c.ogarro@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29760": {
      "title": "Planning and Network Coordinator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: President’s Team; £42,505 - £47,213 per annum; Communications Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRS00583 About the role: Are you an excellent connector, planner and communicator who enjoys turning insight into action? We are looking for a Planning and Network Coordinator to play a pivotal role in shaping and coordinating communications activity across Imperial. Working closely with the Head of Operations and Planning, you will help ensure communications are strategically planned, insight-led and aligned with institutional priorities. You'll bring together information, people and ideas from across the university to support effective decision-making, collaboration and impact. What you would be doing: As our Planning and Network Coordinator, you will help create the conditions for successful communications across Imperial by improving planning, coordination and information sharing. You will: Lead horizon scanning and maintain planning systems that identify upcoming opportunities, risks and priorities across the institution. Coordinate information from across Imperial to support strategic communications planning and prioritisation. Develop strong working relationships with colleagues, senior stakeholders and communications professionals across the university. Support major cross-divisional communications projects and campaigns, ensuring activity is coordinated, aligned and effectively monitored. Produce briefings, planning materials, reports and content for a range of internal and external audiences. Gather, analyse and interpret audience insight, digital analytics, stakeholder intelligence and evaluation data to inform future communications activity. Share best practice, learning and evidence-based recommendations to improve communications effectiveness. Coordinate and grow Imperial's Communications Network through events, briefing sessions, communities of practice and peer networking opportunities. Help foster greater collaboration, knowledge sharing and connectivity across communications teams and professional networks. What we are looking for: We are seeking a confident and collaborative communications professional with strong planning and relationship-management skills. You will bring: Experience working within communications, press office, public affairs or journalism environment. Strong project management skills, with experience coordinating multiple priorities and projects simultaneously. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to tailor messages for a variety of audiences. The ability to analyse complex information, identify key insights and make practical recommendations. Experience working with senior stakeholders and building productive relationships across organisational boundaries. Excellent organisational skills, attention to detail and sound judgement. A proactive, self-starting approach and the confidence to work independently. Strong interpersonal and influencing skills, with the ability to bring people together around shared priorities. An interest in audience insight, evaluation and evidence-led communications. What we can offer you: The opportunity to shape communications planning and best practice at one of the world's leading universities. A highly visible and influential role working with communications professionals from across Imperial. The chance to develop expertise in communications planning, audience insight, evaluation and stakeholder engagement. Opportunities to build extensive networks across higher education, communications and policy communities. A sector-leading benefits package including 43 days' annual leave and generous pension arrangements. Flexible and hybrid working opportunities from day one. Access to professional development, training and wellbeing support. A collaborative, inclusive and innovative environment where your work will have a real institutional impact. Further information: The Communications Division supports and promotes Imperial's mission by communicating the impact of our world-leading research, education and innovation. We work with colleagues across the university to strengthen Imperial's reputation and engagement with key audiences in the UK and internationally. This is a full-time, open-ended position based at White City Campus with hybrid working available. This role would suit an experienced communications professional who enjoys communications planning, connecting people and ideas, and using insight to drive better decision-making and communication outcomes. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact Tabitha Pearson-Moore: t.pearson-moore@imperial.ac.uk."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29737": {
      "title": "Recruitment & Admissions Manager (Full-Time and Weekend MBA)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Business School; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Professional / Support Services Reference: BUS00906 About the role: Recruit, assess and nurture the next generation of leaders as a Recruitment & Admissions Manager (Full-Time & Weekend MBA) at a world-leading university. Imperial Business School is seeking a diligent professional to join our team and deliver end-to-end pipeline management across two of our flagship MBA programmes. You will be empowered to attract and admit exceptional candidates who will benefit business and improve society through the power of innovative thinking. This is an outward-facing role, grounded in data and driven by the robust global relationships you will build. What you would be doing: You will lead the recruitment and admissions processes for Imperial’s Full-Time and Weekend MBA programmes – overseeing the full applicant journey from initial enquiry through to enrolment. You will play a central part in shaping the composition, quality and diversity of our cohorts, ensuring a smooth, professional and engaging experience for every applicant. You will be responsible for implementing data-driven recruitment strategies, managing application and selection processes with rigour and efficiency, and ensuring a professional, personalised experience for every candidate. Through targeted engagement, events and communications, you will develop exceptional student cohorts that contribute to the global reputation of the programmes and the institution. Reporting to the Head of Recruitment & Admissions (Full-Time & Weekend MBA), you will collaborate closely with colleagues across the Recruitment & Admissions team, the Business School and the wider College. You will use data and insights to drive improvements, maintain strong relationships with internal and external stakeholders, and represent Imperial at domestic, international and online events. This people-focused role will require both analytical ability and sound professional judgement. What we are looking for: Experience in recruitment, admissions or a comparable customer-focused environment, ideally within the Higher Education sector. Proven ability to manage our applicant pipeline from enquiry to enrolment, building effective relationships and rigorously assessing candidate suitability to meet ambitious recruitment targets. Excellent communication, presentation and interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage with a diverse global candidate pool. Strong working knowledge of CRM systems and selection tools, able to interpret data to support strategy developments. A commitment to continuous process improvement, using insights and feedback to enhance the candidate journey. A proactive team player with the ability to work independently, manage competing priorities, and deliver under pressure. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: This is a full time, open-ended position based at South Kensington campus (hybrid). Interviews are intended to be conducted on the week commencing Monday 23 rd November, and are intended to be conducted in-person on our South Kensington campus. If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Laura O’Sullivan, Head of Recruitment & Admissions (Full-Time and Weekend MBA), at: laura.osullivan@imperial.ac.uk"
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29740": {
      "title": "Senior Data Analyst",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: Business School; £59,808 - £71,793 per annum; Professional / Support Services Reference: BUS00907 About the role: Ready to turn data into insights that shape the future of Imperial Business School? Join us as a Senior Data Analyst and lead the development of reporting, analysis and business intelligence that support our marketing, recruitment and admissions strategies. You’ll uncover trends, evaluate performance and identify opportunities for growth, helping colleagues make informed, evidence-based decisions across our programme portfolio. What you would be doing: As our Senior Data Analyst, you will lead the analysis and interpretation of complex datasets to generate actionable insights that inform strategic decision-making across Marketing, Communications, Recruitment and Admissions (MCRA). You will design and develop reports, dashboards and analytical models to monitor recruitment pipelines, marketing performance and market trends. Working closely with senior stakeholders, you’ll translate complex findings into clear recommendations, helping teams understand performance, identify opportunities and plan effectively. You will also champion data quality, governance and consistent reporting standards, ensuring colleagues can trust and make effective use of our data. With the autonomy to investigate complex business questions, challenge assumptions and recommend analytical approaches, you’ll play a key role in strengthening the Business School’s data and insight capabilities. What we are looking for: We are looking for an experienced analyst who combines strong technical and analytical expertise with the ability to turn complex evidence into meaningful insights and influence decisions. You will have experience of: Analysing complex, multi-source datasets and translating findings into actionable insights for non-technical audiences. Designing, developing and improving reports, dashboards and data visualisations using tools such as Power BI, Tableau, Looker or similar. Using SQL, Python, R or equivalent technologies to extract, transform, combine and analyse data. Working independently to investigate complex business questions, evaluate evidence and develop robust recommendations. Engaging with senior stakeholders to understand strategic priorities, present findings and influence decisions. Managing data quality and governance, including improving consistency, documenting definitions and supporting data protection compliance. Translating business questions into appropriate analytical approaches, performance measures and recommendations. You will be: A critical thinker with strong analytical and problem-solving skills, able to identify meaningful patterns and distinguish robust findings from the limitations of the available data. Confident exercising independent professional judgement, constructively challenging assumptions and providing authoritative analytical advice. An excellent communicator, able to explain complex findings clearly and persuasively to a range of audiences. Skilled at data visualisation and analytical storytelling, with a focus on making insights accessible and useful for decision-making. Highly organised and proactive, able to manage competing priorities and deliver high-quality work independently. Collaborative and adaptable, with the ability to build effective relationships across technical and non-technical teams. Experience working with CRM, marketing automation or admissions systems, developing forecasts or scenario models, or working in higher education would also be advantageous. What we can offer you: The opportunity to join Imperial Business School, a world-leading institution, and play a key role in shaping how data and insights inform strategic decisions, marketing performance and student recruitment. The chance to lead the development of high-quality reporting, analytics and business intelligence, helping teams understand performance, identify opportunities for growth and make evidence-based decisions. Work collaboratively with colleagues across Marketing, Recruitment and Admissions, Business School teams, and wider Imperial stakeholders on projects that directly support recruitment strategy, market development and the candidate journey. Be part of a forward-thinking Business Systems & Insights team, with opportunities to influence analytical approaches, reporting standards, data quality and governance, and the wider use of data across the Business School. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package, including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes. Access a range of workplace benefits, including flexible working options from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities, and cycle-to-work schemes. Join a diverse, inclusive, and collaborative community with access to staff networks, learning opportunities, and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing. You will join the Business Systems & Insights team, working at the intersection of technology, data, and business operations to support the School’s strategic objectives and enhance the experience of prospective students. Further information: This is a full-time, open-ended position based at South Kensington campus (hybrid). If you require any further details about the role, please contact: Dale Mayfrey – d.mayfrey@imperial.ac.uk."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29754": {
      "title": "Senior Translation Project Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 27/11/2026 Department: Faculty of Engineering; £48,246 - £58,318 per annum; Department of Bioengineering; Professional / Support Services Reference: ENG04094 About the role: The Department of Bioengineering at Imperial College London is a world-leading interdisciplinary department that brings together engineering, medicine and the life sciences to address some of the most pressing challenges in healthcare. Through pioneering research, education and innovation, we develop technologies and solutions that improve human health and wellbeing. We are looking for a Senior Translation Project Officer to join our growing team and work across scientific discovery and commercial application. Help manage pioneering research portfolio and turn science into technologies, partnerships and new ventures that could transform the future of food within the Bezos Centre. The Bezos Centre for Sustainable Protein is a $30 million initiative funded by the Bezos Earth Fund and led by Imperial College London. Bringing together world leading researchers and industry partners, the Centre is advancing sustainable food production through engineering biology, cultivated meat, plant-based foods and fermentation. Alongside the Microbial Food Hub, we are building a vibrant research and innovation ecosystem focused on delivering real world impact. This is an exciting opportunity to work across a diverse portfolio of research and innovation activities. You will help researchers develop promising technologies, build connections with industry and access the support needed to bring new ideas closer to market. You will contribute to major Centre initiatives including the Sustainable Food Accelerator, workshops and training programmes and the development of shared research and innovation capabilities and facilities. More information about the Centre can be found on the following websites: Bezos Centre for Sustainable Protein Sustainable Food Accelerator Follow the Centre on LinkedIn Bezos Earth Fund announcement What you would be doing: The role involves coordinating activities across the Centre and Hub's translation pipeline, research activities and supporting the delivery of programmes such as the Sustainable Food Accelerator, venture building initiatives and specialist training workshops. Working with technical teams, the postholder will also support the development and use of facilities to help researchers access the expertise and capabilities needed to advance their work. Building relationships with industry partners, investors and entrepreneurs will be an important part of the position. In collaboration with Imperial Enterprise and the Centre's Impact Units, the postholder will help develop partnerships, identify funding opportunities and connect research projects with appropriate routes to impact. Additional responsibilities include monitoring the research portfolio, engage with researchers and PIs, tracking progress against key objectives and contributing to new initiatives as the Centre and Hub develop. What we are looking for: We are looking for an enthusiastic and proactive individual with a background in science, engineering, biotechnology or a related field and an interest in turning scientific discoveries into practical applications. You will bring experience in research translation, innovation, commercialisation or project delivery. Strong organisational and project management skills are essential, including the ability to plan and coordinate activities, manage competing priorities and monitor progress across multiple projects. Excellent communication, attention to detail, and stakeholder management skills are important, alongside the confidence to work with academic researchers, industry partners and external organisations. You will be comfortable working independently whilst contributing to a collaborative team. Experience in industry engagement, entrepreneurship, accelerator programmes or venture building would be valuable. Above all, we value initiative, adaptability and a practical approach to solving problems and delivering results. What we can offer you: The opportunity to continue your career at a world-leading institution and be part of our mission to use science for humanity. Benefit from a sector-leading salary and remuneration package (including 43 days’ annual leave and generous pension schemes). Access to a range of workplace benefits including a flexible working policy from day one, generous family leave packages, on-site leisure facilities and cycle-to-work scheme. Interest-free season ticket loan schemes for travel. Be part of a diverse, inclusive and collaborative work culture with various staff networks and resources to support your personal and professional wellbeing . Further information: Based within the Department, the Bezos Centre for Sustainable Protein has a dedicated and growing team supporting research, innovation, partnerships and commercialisation. Working across the Centre and Microbial Food Hub, the team connects researchers with industry and wider stakeholders to accelerate scientific progress and deliver meaningful impact. The role will work across the Bezos Centre for Sustainable Protein and Microbial Food Hub at Imperial College London. You will work closely with the Centre management team, researchers, Imperial Enterprise and external partners. This is a full-time post (35 hours per week). This role is for a fixed-term contract for 24 months, with the potential of an extension. I f you require any further details about the role, please contact: Simon Partridge – [s.partridge@imperial.ac.uk] and Naheed Khan [ n.khan2@imperial.ac.uk] ."
    },
    "https://www.imperial.ac.uk/jobs/search-jobs/description/index.php?jobId=29756": {
      "title": "Systems and Operations Assistant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 10/11/2026 Department: President’s Team; £38,608 - £41,666 per annum; Communications Division; Professional / Support Services Reference: PRS00582 About the role: Are you an organised and proactive administrator looking to build your career in operations, systems and project support? We are looking for a Systems and Operations Assistant to join the Communications Division at Imperial College London. Working closely with the Operations and Resource Officer and Creative Services Manager, you will play a key role in supporting projects, systems, resources and business operations, helping to ensure the smooth delivery of communications activity across one of the world's leading universities. What you would be doing: As our Systems and Operations Assistant, you will provide essential operational and administrative support across the Communications Division. You will: Coordinate meetings, prepare agendas and minutes, and track actions to support effective decision-making and project delivery. Support project workflows, maintaining records, timelines and status updates across a range of communications activities. Assist with resource scheduling and help monitor project progress, highlighting potential issues and delays. Support procurement and finance activities, including raising purchase orders, processing invoices and maintaining expenditure records. Assist with recruitment, onboarding and training administration for staff, freelancers and casual workers. Maintain records and documentation within SharePoint and Microsoft 365 environments. Provide first-line support for communications systems, digital asset management platforms and other business systems used across the Division. Help manage supplier, contract and equipment records, ensuring information remains accurate and up to date. Act as a helpful first point of contact for operational queries, providing excellent customer service to colleagues and stakeholders. What we are looking for: We are seeking a highly organised and enthusiastic individual who enjoys supporting colleagues, improving processes and keeping operations running smoothly. You will bring: Experience providing administrative support in a busy office or organisational environment. Strong organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple tasks and competing priorities. Excellent attention to detail and accuracy when managing information and records. Experience using Microsoft 365 applications including Outlook, Word, Excel and Teams. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build positive working relationships. A proactive and customer-focused approach to solving problems and supporting colleagues. The ability to work independently while also contributing effectively as part of a team. Experience working with digital asset management systems, metadata and taxonomy structures, or a willingness to develop expertise in these areas. A genuine interest in learning new systems, processes and ways of working. What we can offer you: The opportunity to start or develop your career at a world-leading university and contribute to our mission to use science for humanity. A varied role with exposure to operations, project coordination, systems administration, finance and HR processes. Opportunities to develop specialist skills in digital systems, workflow management and communications operations. Access to excellent training and professional development opportunities. A sector-leading benefits package including 43 days' annual leave and generous pension arrangements. Hybrid working options from day one. Access to a comprehensive range of staff benefits, wellbeing initiatives and staff networks. A welcoming, collaborative and inclusive working environment where your contribution will make a real difference. Further information: The Communications Division is responsible for protecting and enhancing Imperial's reputation, communicating the impact of our teaching, research and innovation, and supporting engagement with key audiences across the College and beyond. This is a full-time, open-ended position based at White City Campus with hybrid working available. This role would particularly suit someone looking to develop a career in operations, project support, systems administration or business management within a large and complex organisation. For informal enquiries about the role, please contact Tabitha Pearson-Moore: t.pearson-moore@imperial.ac.uk"
    }
  },
